hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1801","1918 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1802","1919 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayyah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, and from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it, except that he did not mention that the second bowing was shorter than the first bowing, but mentioned that it was like it. He said: And that was the day Ibrahim died. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to this and said: Thus is the lunar eclipse prayer, four rak'ahs and four prostrations. And others opposed them in that and said: Rather it is eight bowings in four prostrations. And they argued in that by what:"
"1803","1920 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Tawus, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the lunar eclipse prayer, so he stood and opened, then recited, then bowed, then raised his head and recited, then bowed, then raised his head and recited, then bowed, then raised his head and recited, then bowed, then prostrated, then did the like of that another time."
"1804","1921 - Abu Zur'ah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us, from Sufyan, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1805","1922 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Sufyan, he said: Habib narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1806","1923 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn al-Hurr, he said: al-Hakam narrated to me, from a man called Hanash, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he prayed with the people in the solar eclipse likewise, then informed them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did likewise. And others opposed these and said: Rather it is six bowings in four prostrations."
"1807","1924 - And they argued in that by what Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Ata, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would stand and bow three bowings, then prostrate two prostrations, then stand and bow three bowings, then prostrate two prostrations, meaning in the lunar eclipse prayer."
"1808","1925 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Ata, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, on the prayer of the signs, he said: Six bowings and four prostrations."
"1809","1926 - Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Kufi narrated to us, he said: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from Ata, from Jabir ibn Abdillah: The sun eclipsed on the day Ibrahim son of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, so he prayed with the people, and he mentioned the like of the hadith of Rabi' from Asad, and added: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sun and moon are two signs of Allah's signs, they do not eclipse for anyone's death nor life, so when you see anything of that, pray until it clears. They said: And Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, did the like of this after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so they mentioned what:"
"1810","1927 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, he said: The earth quaked in the time of Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, so he said: I do not know which land, meaning what he had of discernment, thus al-Khasib mentioned, or the earth quaked. So it was said to him: The earth quaked, so he went out and prayed with the people, so he said takbir four times, then recited and lengthened recitation, and said takbir and bowed, then said: Allah hears who praises Him, then said takbir four times, so he said takbir and lengthened recitation, then said takbir and bowed, then said: Allah hears who praises Him, then said takbir four times, so he recited and lengthened recitation, then said takbir and bowed, then prostrated, then stood and did the like of that. So when he said salam he said: Thus is the prayer of the signs, and he recited in the first rak'ah Surah al-Baqarah, and in the other Surah Al Imran. And others opposed them in that and said: Rather he lengthens prayer so ever, bowing and prostrating, with no fixed measure in any of that until the sun clears. And they argued in that by what:"
"1811","1928 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Ya'la ibn Hakim, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said: Had the sun cleared in the fourth rak'ah, he would have bowed and prostrated. So this Sa'id ibn Jubayr informs from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that had the sun cleared for him in the fourth rak'ah he would have bowed and prostrated, and the fourth is the first of the second rak'ah. So this indicates he did not intend a known number of bowings in that, rather he bows while the sun is eclipsed until it clears, then ends prayer. And they went in that to the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): So pray until it clears. And others opposed them in that and said: The eclipse prayer is two rak'ahs like other supererogatory prayer, if you wish lengthen them, and if you wish shorten them. Then supplication after them until the sun clears. And they differed in that for what:"
"1812","1929 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he stood with the people and would hardly bow, then bowed, and would hardly rise, then rose, and would hardly prostrate, then prostrated, and would hardly rise. And he did in the second the like of that, so he raised his head while the sun had brightened."
"1813","1930 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it with his chain."
"1814","1931 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn Ata narrated to us, from his father, and Ata ibn al-Sa'ib from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1815","1932 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he prayed two rak'ahs."
"1816","1933 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdillah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from his father, from Abdullah ibn Amr: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the solar eclipse two rak'ahs and four prostrations, lengthening therein standing and bowing and prostration."
"1817","1934 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ayyub, from his uncle Iyas ibn Amir, that he heard Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) obligated four prayers: the residence prayer four rak'ahs, the travel prayer two rak'ahs, the eclipse prayer two rak'ahs, and the rites prayer two rak'ahs."
"1818","1935 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Tha'labah ibn Abbad, from Samurah ibn Jundub, he said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prayed with them the like of what Abdullah ibn Amr mentioned, exactly."
"1819","1936 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad narrated to us. And he mentioned the like of it with his chain."
"1820","1937 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, he said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he prayed two rak'ahs."
"1821","1938 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakr, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then the sun eclipsed, so he stood to the mosque dragging his cloak in haste, and the people gathered to him, so he prayed as you pray."
"1822","1939 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed us, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, that the sun or the moon eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: The sun and moon are two signs of Allah's signs, and they do not eclipse for anyone's death nor life, so when that is, pray until it clears."
"1823","1940 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi, he is al-Basri, narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Qilabah, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray in the solar eclipse as you pray, a bow and two prostrations."
"1824","1941 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Qilabah, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, he said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he would bow and prostrate."
"1825","1942 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Waki' narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Qilabah, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the eclipse like this prayer of yours, bowing and prostrating."
"1826","1943 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir or another, he said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he kept praying two rak'ahs and saying salam, and asking until it cleared, then he said: Men claim the sun and moon eclipse only for the death of a great one of the greats of the earth's folk, and that is not so, but they are two signs of Allah's signs, so when Allah manifests to a thing of His creation it humbles to Him."
"1827","1944 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, from Za'idah, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, he said: I heard al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, he said: The sun eclipsed on the day Ibrahim died, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sun and moon are two signs of Allah's signs, they do not eclipse for anyone's death nor life, so when you see that, pray and supplicate until it unveils."
"1828","1945 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us. (H). 1946 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: The sun eclipsed, so al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah prayed with the people two rak'ahs and four prostrations. So that indicated what he had learned of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayer and witnessed, the like of that."
"1829","1947 - Abu Hazim Abd al-Hamid ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Qilabah, from Qabisah al-Bajali, he said: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he prayed as you pray."
"1830","1948 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Qabisah al-Hilali or another: The sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out frightened dragging his garment, and I was with him that day in Madinah, so he prayed two rak'ahs lengthening them, then left, while the sun had cleared, and said: These signs Allah frightens with, so when you see them, pray as the latest prayer you prayed of the prescribed. So most reports in this chapter agreed with this last view, so we wanted to look into the meanings of the first views, so al-Nu'man ibn Bashir had informed in his hadith that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray two rak'ahs and say salam and ask, so it may be al-Nu'man learned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the prostration after each rak'ah, and those who agreed with him learned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed two rak'ahs, while those who said: he bowed two bowings or more than that before prostrating did not know, due to his long prayer, so validating this hadith of al-Nu'man with these reports is to make his prayer as al-Nu'man said; for what Ali and Ibn Abbas and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with them, reported enters into that, and the hadith of al-Nu'man adds over it, so it is worthier than all that opposed them. Then what Qabisah related of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): So when that is, pray as the latest prayer you prayed of the prescribed, strengthened that. So he informed us he only prays in eclipse as he prays the prescribed, then we returned to the saying of those who did not fix a measure in that for what they reported from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, so the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Qabisah: So pray as the latest prayer you prayed of the prescribed, was proof that prayer therein is timed, known, with known time and known number, so what the opponents of this hadith went to was nullified. But as for their saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: So when you see that, pray until it clears, so they said: In this is proof he should not end prayer when that is until it clears. So it is said to them: He said in some of these hadiths: So pray and supplicate until it unveils. And:"
"1831","1949 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abdullah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sun and moon are two signs of Allah's signs, they do not eclipse for anyone's death, I think, nor life, so when you see that, upon you is remembrance of Allah and prayer."
"1832","1950 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Burayd ibn Abdillah, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, he said: The sun darkened in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he stood frightened fearing the Hour had come until he came to the mosque, so he stood praying with the longest standing and bowing and prostration I ever saw him do in any prayer, then he said: These signs which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sends are not for anyone's death nor life, but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sends them frightening His servants with them, so when you see any of them, hasten to remembrance of Allah and His supplication and seeking His forgiveness. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered supplication at it and seeking forgiveness as he ordered prayer. So that indicated he did not intend from them at eclipse prayer alone, but intended from them what they draw near to Allah the Exalted with of prayer and supplication and seeking forgiveness and other than that. And:"
"1833","1951 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi' ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Za'idah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Fatimah, from Asma, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered freeing slaves at eclipse. So that indicated what we mentioned. And there has been reported in that from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what:"
"1834","1952 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: I heard Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The sun and moon are two signs of Allah's signs, they do not eclipse for anyone's death nor life, so when you see it, stand and pray. So in this hadith they were ordered to stand at seeing that for prayer, and in the first hadiths they were ordered with supplication and seeking forgiveness after prayer until the sun clears, so that indicated they were not ordered not to end prayer until the sun clears, and it is established thereby that they may lengthen prayer if they wish, and if they wish shorten it and join it with supplication until the sun clears. And:"
"1835","1953 - Ibrahim ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya al-Kalbi narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn al-Abbas used to narrate that Abdullah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to narrate of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayer the day the sun darkened with the like of what Urwah narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. al-Zuhri said: So I said to Urwah: Your brother, the day the sun darkened in Madinah, added nothing over two rak'ahs like the dawn prayer, so he said: Yes, he missed the sunnah. So this Urwah and al-Zuhri mentioned of Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr that he prayed for the solar eclipse two rak'ahs, while Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr is a man with companionship, and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were present then and none of them denied that of him. But as for Urwah's saying (he missed the sunnah), that with us is nothing. And all we clarified in this chapter of the eclipse prayer is that it is two rak'ahs, and the pray-er, if he wishes lengthens them, and if he wishes shortens them when he joins them with supplication until the sun clears. And it is the view of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, and it is the reasoning with us; for we saw all prayers of prescribed and supererogatory with each rak'ah two prostrations, so by reasoning that this prayer is likewise."
"1836","39 - Chapter: How is recitation in the eclipse prayer? 1954 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I heard nothing from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the eclipse prayer, not a letter."
"1837","1955 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us. (H). 1956 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Tha'labah ibn Abbad, from Samurah ibn Jundub, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us in the eclipse prayer, we heard no voice from him."
"1838","1957 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Ibn Abbad, a man of Banu Abd al-Qays, from Samurah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1839","1958 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Tha'labah, from Samurah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to these reports and said: Thus is the eclipse prayer, no loud recitation therein; for it is of daytime prayer. And of those who went to that is Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him. And others opposed them in that and said: Recitation is aloud therein, and of their proof in that is that Ibn Abbas and Samurah, may Allah be pleased with them both, may not have heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that prayer a letter, while he recited aloud therein, due to their distance from him. So this does not negate aloud; since it was reported from him that he recited aloud therein. So of what was reported from him in that is what:"
"1840","1959 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited aloud in the solar eclipse."
"1841","1960 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So this Aishah informs he recited aloud therein, so she is worthier for what we mentioned. And reasoning in that, when they differed, was that we saw Zuhr and Asr prayed in daytime on ordinary days with no aloud therein, and saw Friday prayed on a special day with aloud therein, so the obligations were so in ruling; what of them is done on ordinary days in daytime is lowered therein, and what of them is done on a special day is aloud therein. And likewise was made the ruling of supererogatories; what of them is done on ordinary days in daytime is lowered therein in recitation, and what of them is done on a special day, like the two-Eid prayer, is aloud therein in recitation. This none dispute among people, and the seeking-rain prayer, in the view of one who holds prayer in seeking rain, was so in ruling with him, aloud therein in recitation. And its odd saying supported what we reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before in this book of his reciting aloud in the seeking-rain prayer. So when what we described was established in obligations and sunnahs, it is established that the eclipse prayer is likewise also, since of sunnah done on a special day, its recitation ruling must be as the recitation ruling in sunnahs done on a special day, which is aloud, not low, by analogy and reasoning upon what we mentioned. And it is the view of Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them both. And that has also been reported from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him."
"1842","1961 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Shaybani, from al-Hakam, from Hanash: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, recited aloud in the solar eclipse, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, had prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in what we have reported of what preceded in this book of ours."
"1843","40 - Chapter: How is voluntary by night and day? 1962 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ya'la ibn Ata, he said: I heard Ali ibn Abdillah al-Bariqi narrating from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: And I think he raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Night and day prayer is two by two."
"1844","1963 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hunayni narrated to us, from al-Umari, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to this and said: Thus is night and day prayer, two by two, saying salam in each two. And they argued by these reports. And others opposed them in that, and said: As for day prayer, if you wish pray with one takbir two by two, saying salam in each two, and if you wish four, and they disliked adding over that anything, and they differed on night prayer, so some said: if you wish pray with one takbir two, and if you wish four, and if you wish six, and if you wish eight, and they disliked adding over that anything. And of those who said that: Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, and some said: Night prayer is two by two, saying salam in each two. And of those who said that: Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, and as for what we mentioned on day prayer, it is the view of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And of their proof against the first view: every one who reported the hadith of Ibn Umar, besides Ali al-Bariqi, and besides what al-Umari reported from Nafi' from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, only intends night prayer specifically, not day prayer. And we have mentioned that in the Witr chapter. And there has been reported from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, of his act after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates the corruption of these two hadiths also which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter."
"1845","1964 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to pray by night two and by day four."
"1846","1965 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Zayd, from Jabalah ibn Suhaym, from Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to pray before Friday four, not separating between them with salam, then after Friday two, then four. So it is impossible that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, reports from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what Ali al-Bariqi reported from him, then does contrary to that."
"1847","And as for what was reported in that from other than Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun informed us, he said: Ubaydah al-Dabbi informed us. (H). 1966 - And Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Ubaydah. (H). 1967 - And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Ubaydah, from Ibrahim, he is al-Nakha'i, from Sahm ibn Minjab, from Qaza'ah, from al-Qartha', from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) persisted in four rak'ahs after the sun's decline, so I said: Messenger of Allah, you persist in these four rak'ahs. So he said: Abu Ayyub, when the sun declines the gates of heaven are opened, and will not be shut until Zuhr is prayed; so I love that a righteous deed of mine ascend therein before they are shut. So I said: Messenger of Allah, is in all of them recitation? He said: Yes. I said: Between them a separating salam? He said: No, except the tashahhud."
"1848","1968 - Abd al-Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Fahd ibn Hayyan narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ubaydah, from Ibrahim, from Sahm ibn al-Minjab, from Qaza'ah, from Qartha', from Abu Ayyub, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Four rak'ahs before Zuhr, no salam in them, the gates of heaven are opened for them. Abu Ja'far said: So it is established by this hadith that one may voluntary in daytime four rak'ahs with no salam in them, so thereby the view of those we mentioned who went to that is established. This has also been reported from a group of the predecessors."
"1849","1969 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Ubaydah, from Ibrahim, he said: Abdullah used to pray four rak'ahs before Zuhr, and four rak'ahs after Friday, and four rak'ahs after Fitr and Adha, with no separating salam in them, and in all of them recitation."
"1850","1970 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Muhill al-Dabbi, from Ibrahim: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to pray before Friday four and after it four, not separating between them with salam."
"1851","1971 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Husayn, from Ibrahim, he said: They would not say salam in the four before Zuhr."
"1852","1972 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, he said: Muhill asked Ibrahim about the rak'ahs before Zuhr, does he separate between them with salam? He said: If you wish suffice with the salam of tashahhud, and if you wish separate."
"1853","1973 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ma'shar, that Ibrahim said: Night and day prayer is two by two, except that if you wish you pray of daytime four rak'ahs, not saying salam except at their end. Abu Ja'far said: So the ruling of day prayer is established upon what we mentioned, and what we reported in these reports did not repel that nor did anything oppose it, and as for night prayer we have mentioned therein of disagreement what we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter. So of the proof of those who allowed him to pray by night eight, not separating between them with salam, was the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would pray by night eleven rak'ahs, of them the Witr three rak'ahs. So it was said to him: al-Zuhri reported from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that he would say salam between each two of them. And this chapter is only taken from the direction of prescription and following what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did and ordered and his Companions did after him, so we found not with one who did it, nor from his saying, that he permitted praying at night with one takbir more than two rak'ahs, and by that we take, and it is the sounder of the two views with us in that."
"1854","41 - Chapter: How is voluntary after Friday? 1974 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever of you prays after Friday, let him pray four. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to that the voluntary after Friday not to be left is four rak'ahs, not separating between them with salam, and they argued in that by this hadith. And others opposed them in that and said: Rather the voluntary after Friday not to be left is two rak'ahs, like the voluntary after Zuhr. And they argued in that by what:"
"1855","1975 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would not pray the two rak'ahs after Friday except in his house."
"1856","1976 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Adim narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi': Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saw a man praying two rak'ahs after Friday, so he pushed him and said: Do you pray Friday as four? He said: And Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, would pray the two rak'ahs in his house and say: Thus did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And others opposed them in that and said: The voluntary after Friday not to be left is six rak'ahs, four then two. And they said: It is possible the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said what Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from him first, then did what Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, reported from him, so that was an addition to what preceded of his saying."
"1857","1977 - And the proof of what they went to in that is that Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Ata, Abu Ishaq said: He narrated to me more than once, he said: I prayed with Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, on Friday, so when he said salam he stood and prayed two rak'ahs, then he said: then he prayed four rak'ahs, then left. So this Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, would voluntary after Friday with two rak'ahs, then four, so it is possible he did that for what had been established with him of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that and his act, upon what we mentioned. And the like of that has been reported from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him."
"1858","1978 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: Whoever prays after Friday, let him pray six."
"1859","1979 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, he said: Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, taught the people to pray after Friday four, then when Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, came, he taught them to pray six."
"1860","1980 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, he said: Abdullah came to us and would pray after Friday four, then Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came after him, and when he prayed Friday he prayed after it two rak'ahs and four, so the act of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, pleased us, so we chose it. So it is established by what we mentioned that the voluntary not to be left after Friday is six, and it is the view of Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, except that he said: More beloved to me to begin with the four then follow with the two rak'ahs; for it is farther from having prayed after Friday its like, upon what has been forbidden. For:"
"1861","1981 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Sulayman ibn Mushir, from Kharashah ibn al-Hurr: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, disliked praying after the Friday prayer its like. Abu Ja'far said: For that Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy on him, deemed it good to put the four before the two rak'ahs; for they are not like the two rak'ahs, so he disliked putting the two rak'ahs first; for they are like Friday. And as for Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him, he went in that to the view we began by mentioning at the beginning of this chapter."
"1862","42 - Chapter: A man opens prayer sitting, is he allowed to bow standing or not? 1982 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq al-Uqayli, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say takbir for prayer standing and sitting, so when he prayed standing he bowed standing, and when he prayed sitting he bowed sitting."
"1863","1983 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her: He asked her about that, so she narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it exactly."
"1864","1984 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr al-Ataki narrated to us, he said: Abu Hilal narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it."
"1865","1985 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Budayl ibn Maysarah narrated to me, from Ibn Shaqiq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1866","1986 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Budayl, and he mentioned the like of it with his chain."
"1867","1987 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: I asked Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. And he mentioned the like of it."
"1868","1988 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Budayl ibn Maysarah and Humayd, from Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1869","1989 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from Abd ibn Ma'qil, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to disliking bowing standing for one who opened prayer sitting, and they argued in that by this hadith. And others opposed them in that; and saw no harm in it."
"1870","And of their proof in that was what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her: She informed him that she never saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray night prayer sitting ever until he aged, so he would recite sitting, until when he wanted to bow he stood and recited about thirty verses or forty verses, then bowed."
"1871","1990 - Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1872","1991 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, he said: Hisham narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1873","1992 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, freedman of al-Aswad ibn Sufyan, and Abu al-Nadr, freedman of Umar ibn Ubaydillah, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So in this hadith is other than what is in the hadith of Abdullah ibn Shaqiq; for in this he would bow standing after opening prayer sitting. And this is worthier than the first hadith which Ibn Shaqiq reported; for his remaining sitting until bowing sitting does not indicate he may not stand and bow standing, while his standing from his sitting until bowing standing indicates he may bow standing after opening sitting: so for this we made this hadith worthier than what preceded it. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"1874","43 - Chapter: Voluntary in mosques. 1993 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mutarrif ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Musa narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ishaq, from his father, from his grandfather: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Maghrib in the mosque of Banu Abd al-Ashhal, so when he finished he saw the people glorifying Allah, so he said: People, this prayer is only in houses."
"1875","1994 - Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn al-Harith, from Haram ibn Hakim, from his uncle Abdullah ibn Sa'd, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about prayer in my house and prayer in the mosque, so he said: You see how near my house is to the mosque, yet my praying in my house is more beloved to me than my praying in the mosque, except it be a prescribed prayer. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to that voluntary should not be done in mosques, except what should not be left, like the two rak'ahs after Zuhr and the two rak'ahs after Maghrib and the two rak'ahs upon entering the mosque, but what is besides that should not be prayed in mosques, but delayed to houses. And others opposed them in that and said: Voluntary in mosques is good, except that voluntary in homes is better than it. And they argued in that by what:"
"1876","1995 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Ali ibn Abdillah ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: Spend tonight with the family of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Isha, then prayed until none remained in the mosque but him. Abu Ja'far said: So this indicates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would voluntary in the mosque this long voluntary, so that with us is good, except that voluntary in houses is better than it; for the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The best prayer of a man is in his house except the prescribed. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them."
"1877","44 - Chapter: Voluntary after Witr. 1996 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Asbat narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Abu Ishaq, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform Witr at the beginning of the night and in its middle and in its end, then Witr became fixed for him at its end."
"1878","1997 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir and Affan narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, Abu Ishaq said: He informed me more than once, he said: I heard Asim ibn Damrah narrating from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1879","1998 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq ibn Abi Ayyad narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq. And he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1880","1999 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Isra'il informed us, and another time he said: Abu Isra'il informed us, from al-Suddi, from Abd Khayr, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came out to us while we were in the mosque, so he said: Where is the asker about Witr? So we went to him, so he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform Witr at the beginning of the night, then it appeared to him and he performed Witr in its middle, then Witr became fixed for him in this hour. He said: And that is at the rise of dawn. And this with us is upon near the rise of dawn, before it rises, until the meaning of this hadith and the meaning of the hadith of Asim ibn Damrah become consistent. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to that the time to be made for Witr is the predawn, and that none voluntary after it, and that one who voluntaries after it has broken it, and upon him to repeat another Witr, and they argued in that by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delaying Witr to the end of the night, and by what was reported from a group of his Companions after him that they held one who voluntaries after Witr has broken it. And they mentioned in that what:"
"1881","2000 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Musa ibn Talhah, that Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I perform Witr at the beginning of the night, so when I rise at the end of the night I pray one rak'ah, and I liken it only to a young she-camel I join to the camels."
"1882","2001 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr. And he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1883","2002 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Imran ibn Bashir, from his father, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib: Abu Bakr used to do that."
"1884","2003 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Harun al-Ghanawi, from Hittan ibn Abdillah, he said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, say: Witr is of three kinds: a man who performs Witr at the beginning of the night then wakes and prays two rak'ahs, and a man who performs Witr at the beginning of the night then wakes and joins to his Witr one rak'ah, then prays two by two, then performs Witr, and a man who delays his Witr to the end of the night."
"1885","2004 - Muhammad ibn Bahr narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah and Malik ibn Dinar, from Khilas, he said: I was sitting with Ammar when a man came to him and said to him: How do you perform Witr? He said: Are you content with what I do? He said: Yes. He said: I think Qatadah said in his hadith: I perform Witr at night with five rak'ahs, then I sleep, and when I rise at night I make even."
"1886","2005 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Yazid ibn 'Abdullah ibn Qusayt, from Abu Salamah and Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: Whoever performs Witr then decides to pray, let him add another one to it to make it even, until he performs Witr afterwards."
"1887","2006 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Masruq, he said: Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said something which I do by my own opinion and do not narrate, then he mentioned the like of that. Masruq said: And the companions of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to marvel at the practice of Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"1888","2007 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu al-Harith al-Ghifari, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man asked him for a ruling about a man who performed Witr at the beginning of the night then slept, then stood up: what should he do? He said: He completes it to ten. And the opposite of this statement has been reported from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. And we shall mention it after this, if Allah, the Exalted, wills. And others opposed them in that, saying: There is no harm in voluntary prayer after Witr, and that does not nullify the Witr. And they reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that what:"
"1889","2008 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah al-Bablutti narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, from Abu Salamah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed two rak'ahs after Witr, reciting in them, while he was seated; then when he intended to bow he stood up and bowed. And we have mentioned the like of that also, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in (the chapter of Witr) in the hadith of Sa'd ibn Hisham."
"1890","2009 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: 'Umarah ibn Zadhad narrated to us, from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite in the two rak'ahs after Witr al-Rahman and al-Waqi'ah."
"1891","2010 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Abu Ghalib, from Abu Umamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray them after Witr, while seated, reciting in them {When the earth is shaken} and {Say: O you disbelievers}."
"1892","2011 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Shurayh ibn 'Ubayd, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Thawban, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and he said: This journey is effort and hardship, so when one of you performs Witr, let him bow two rak'ahs; if he wakes up (it is well), otherwise they will count for him. So this Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed voluntary prayer after Witr with two rak'ahs while seated, and that did not nullify his preceding Witr. So this is worthier than what the people of the first view interpreted and claimed of the meaning of the hadith of 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ended his Witr at the time of dawn, besides that there is also no contradiction in that with this according to us, for it is possible that his Witr ended at dawn, then he performed voluntary prayer after it before the rising of dawn. If one says: It is possible that those two rak'ahs are the two rak'ahs of Fajr, so that would not be of the night prayer. It is said to him: That is not permissible for two reasons. As for one of them: Sa'd ibn Hisham asked 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, only about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at night, so that was from her an answer to his question and information to him about his prayer at night, how it was. And the other reason: No one may pray the two rak'ahs of Fajr seated while able to stand, because by that he abandons standing in them; rather it is only permissible to pray seated while able to stand that which one may abandon altogether, and its abandonment is allowed for him. So just as he may abandon it in its entirety, he may abandon standing in it. As for what he may not abandon, he may not abandon standing in it. So it is established by that that those two rak'ahs which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) volunteered after Witr were of the night prayer. And in that is what necessitates the statement of those who saw no harm in voluntary prayer at night after Witr and did not nullify the Witr by it. And there has been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that, of his statement, what also indicates this, which we have mentioned from him in the hadith of Thawban. And indeed:"
"1893","2012 - 'Imran ibn Musa al-Ta'i and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us. (Ha). 2013 - And Ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, they said: Ayyub ibn 'Utbah informed us, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There are no two Witrs in one night."
"1894","2014 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Mulazim ibn 'Amr narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Badr narrated to me, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"1895","2015 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym and Abu al-Walid narrated to us, they said: Mulazim narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Badr. And he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1896","2016 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Aqil, from Jabir ibn 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Bakr: When do you perform Witr? He said: At the beginning of the night after 'Isha. He said: You have taken hold of the firmest. Then he said to 'Umar: When do you perform Witr? He said: At the end of the night. He said: You have taken hold of strength."
"1897","2017 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Abu Bakr and 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, discussed Witr in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: As for me, I pray then sleep upon Witr; when I wake up I pray in pairs until morning. So 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: But I sleep upon even, then perform Witr at the end of the pre-dawn time. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him: This one is cautious, and said to 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him: This one is strong. So the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There are no two Witrs in one night, indicates what we mentioned of negating the repetition of Witr. And that agreed with the statement of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him: As for me, I perform Witr at the beginning of the night, then when I wake up I pray in pairs until morning. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving off rebuking him is proof that the ruling of that is as he used to do, and that Witr is not nullified by the supererogatory prayers performed after it. And that has also been reported from a group of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1898","2018 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, he said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about Witr, and he said: When you perform Witr at the beginning of the night, do not perform Witr at its end; and when you perform Witr at its end, do not perform Witr at its beginning. He said: And I asked 'A'idh ibn 'Amr, and he said the like of it."
"1899","2019 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah and Malik ibn Dinar, that they both heard Khilas, he said: I heard 'Ammar ibn Yasir - and a man asked him about Witr - and he said: As for me, I perform Witr then sleep; if I stand up I pray two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs. And this, according to us, is the meaning of the hadith of Hammam, from Qatadah, which we mentioned in the first section; for in that is: when I stand up I make even. So that could mean he makes even with one rak'ah as Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to do, and it could mean he prays in pairs. So in the hadith of Shu'bah is what clarifies that the meaning of the statement 'I made even' is: I prayed in pairs, and I did not nullify the Witr."
"1900","2020 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: The nullification of Witr was mentioned in the presence of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she said: There are no two Witrs in one night."
"1901","2021 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from 'Imran ibn Abi Anas, from 'Umar ibn al-Hakam, that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If I brought three camels and made them kneel, then brought two camels and made them kneel, would that not be Witr? He said: And he used to strike it as an example for the nullification of Witr. And this, according to us, is sound speech, and its meaning is that what you pray after Witr of the even prayers, it is with the Witr you performed, Witr."
"1902","2022 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Murrah, the freed slave of 'Aqil ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he asked Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: How did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform Witr? He said: If you wish I shall inform you how I do. I said: Inform me. He said: When I pray 'Isha, I pray after it five rak'ahs, then sleep; if I stand up at night, I pray two by two; and if morning comes, I am upon Witr. So these: Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and 'A'idh ibn 'Amr, and 'Ammar, and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them both, and 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, do not consider voluntary prayer after Witr to nullify Witr. So this is worthier, according to us, than what was reported from those who opposed them, since it agreed with what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his action and statement. And what was reported from the others also has no basis in reasoning; for when they wanted to volunteer, they prayed one rak'ah, making even with it a preceding Witr, while they had separated between it and what they made even with, by speech, action, and sleep. And this has no basis also in consensus, so this disagreement is built upon it. So when the matter was so, and those of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) whom we mentioned opposed it, and the opposite of it was also reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that is negated, and acting upon it is not permissible. And this statement which we have explained is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"1903","45 - Chapter of recitation in the night prayer, how is it? 2023 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Abi 'Amr, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray at night, and his recitation would be heard by whoever was behind the chambers while he was in the house."
"1904","2024 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Hilal ibn Khabbab, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, from his grandmother Umm Hani, she said: I used to hear the voice of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the middle of the night, while I was sleeping on my roof terrace, as he prayed repeating the Qur'an with tarji'."
"1905","2025 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar narrated to us, from Abu al-'Ala', from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, he said: Umm Hani said: I used to hear the voice of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was on my roof terrace. Abu Ja'far said: So some people went to that the recitation in the night prayer is thus, and they disliked silent recitation. And others opposed them in that, saying: If he wishes he recites silently, and if he wishes he recites aloud. And they adduced as proof in that what:"
"1906","2026 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Ali narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from 'Imran ibn Za'idah ibn Nashit, from his father, from Abu Khalid al-Walibi, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The recitation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning at night - he would raise at times and lower at times."
"1907","2027 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from 'Imran, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1908","2028 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, from 'Imran ibn Za'idah, from his father, from Khalid, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, and he did not mention Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him. So this Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, informs from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he used to raise his voice in his recitation at night at times, and lower it at times. So that indicates that one praying at night may raise if he likes and lower if he likes. And it is possible that what Umm Hani and Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, mentioned of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raising his voice with recitation in his prayer at night, was a raising done after lowering. So the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas and Umm Hani, may Allah be pleased with them, does not negate lowering, and the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, clarifies that the one praying may lower if he likes and raise if he likes, so it is worthier than these hadiths. And Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, hold this view."
"1909","46 - Chapter of combining surahs in one rak'ah. 2029 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Abu al-'Aliyah, he said: One who heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me, he says: For every surah a rak'ah."
"1910","2030 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: 'Asim al-Ahwal informed me, from Abu al-'Aliyah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For every surah a rak'ah. He said: So I mentioned that to Ibn Sirin, and he said: Did he name for you who narrated to him? I said: No. He said: Why did you not ask him? So I asked him, saying: Who narrated to you? He said: I know who narrated to me, and in which place he narrated to me; and I used to pray between twenty, until this hadith reached me. Abu Ja'far said: So some people adopted this, saying: It is not proper for a man to add in each rak'ah of his prayer over one surah with the Opening of the Book. And they adduced as proof in that this hadith, and what was reported from Ibn 'Umar."
"1911","2031 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata', he said: I heard Ibn Labibah say: A man said to Ibn 'Umar: I recited the Mufassal in one rak'ah, or he said: in one night. So Ibn 'Umar said: If Allah had willed He would have sent it down all at once, but He separated it, so that each surah be given its share of bowing and prostration. And others opposed them in that, saying: There is no harm that a man pray in one rak'ah whatever surahs he wishes. And they adduced as proof in that what:"
"1912","2032 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Kahmas ibn al-Hasan informed us, from 'Abdullah ibn Shaqiq, he said: I said to 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) join surahs together? She said: The Mufassal."
"1913","2033 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn 'Abd al-Malik narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Husayn, he said: Ibrahim informed me, from Nuhayk ibn Sinan al-Sulami, that he came to 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: I recited the Mufassal tonight in one rak'ah. So he said: Reciting hastily like the hasty recitation of poetry, and scattering like the scattering of inferior dates; it was only separated so that you separate. We know the counterparts which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to recite, twenty surahs: al-Rahman and al-Najm according to the arrangement of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, every two surahs in one rak'ah; and he mentioned al-Dukhan and {About what do they question one another} in one rak'ah. So I said to Ibrahim: What about less than that, what should I do? He said: Sometimes I recited four in one rak'ah."
"1914","2034 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us. (Ha). 2035 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu Wa'il, that a man said to 'Abdullah: I recited the Mufassal in one rak'ah. So he said: Hastily like the hasty recitation of poetry. I know the counterparts which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to join together."
"1915","2036 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Sayyar narrated to us, from Abu Wa'il, from 'Abdullah the like of it. Except that he said: Which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to join together, two surahs in each rak'ah."
"1916","2037 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. (Ha). 2038 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, they said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Alqamah and al-Aswad, they said: A man came to 'Abdullah and said: I recited the Mufassal in one rak'ah. So he said: Scattering like the scattering of inferior dates, and hastily like the hasty recitation of poetry. But the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not do what you did; he used to join every two surahs together, two surahs in each rak'ah, in each rak'ah: al-Najm and al-Rahman in one rak'ah, twenty surahs in ten rak'ahs."
"1917","2039 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah informed us, from Sulayman al-A'mash, from Sa'd ibn 'Ubaydah, from al-Mustawrid ibn al-Ahnaf, from Silah ibn Zufar, from Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, he said: I prayed beside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night, and he opened with Surah al-Baqarah; when he finished it, he opened Al 'Imran. Whenever he came upon a verse mentioning Paradise or Hell, he paused and asked, or sought refuge, or said words to this effect. So in these reports is that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to join two surahs together in each rak'ah. And this contradicts what Abu al-'Aliyah reported, and it is worthier, due to the soundness of its chain and the authenticity of its transmission. As for the statement of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, after that: It was only named al-Mufassal so that you separate it, he did not attribute that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And it is possible that is from his own opinion; and if it is from his opinion, 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, opposed him in that, for he used to complete the Qur'an in one rak'ah, and we shall mention that at the end of this chapter, if Allah, the Exalted, wills. And it has been reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he recited in one rak'ah of the Fajr prayer part of a surah."
"1918","2040 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us with that, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed us. (Ha). 2041 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Muhammad ibn 'Abbad ibn Ja'far, from Abu Salamah ibn Sufyan, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Sa'ib, he said: The Fajr prayer on the morning of the Conquest came upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he opened Surah al-Mu'min; when he came upon the mention of Musa and 'Isa, or Musa and Harun, peace be upon them, a cough seized him so he bowed. If one says: He only did that because of the cough that befell him. It is said to him: It has been reported from him that he used to recite in the two rak'ahs of Fajr two verses of the Qur'an; we have mentioned that in the chapter of recitation in the two rak'ahs of Fajr. And indeed:"
"1919","2042 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Hayyan Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated to us, from a man, he is Qudamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, or Ibn 'Abdullah, from Jasrah bint Dajajah, she said: I heard Abu Dharr say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite a verse of the Book of Allah, with it he would bow, with it he would prostrate, and with it he would supplicate."
"1920","2043 - 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah al-'Attabi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to us, from Qudamah ibn 'Abdullah, from Jasrah bint Dajajah, from Abu Dharr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood with one verse until morning: {If You punish them, they are Your servants; and if You forgive them, You are the Mighty, the Wise}."
"1921","2044 - 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan narrated to me, he said: Qudamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, he said: Jasrah bint Dajajah narrated to me, that she heard Abu Dharr narrating from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So this is proof that there is no harm in reciting part of a surah in one rak'ah. And it is established that there is no harm in reciting surahs in one rak'ah, due to what we have mentioned of what came in that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And it has come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: The best prayer is length of standing. So that also negates what Abu al-'Aliyah mentioned, for it necessitates that the best of prayers are those in which recitation is lengthened, and that is only by combining many surahs in one rak'ah. And all this is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And there has been reported from Ibn 'Umar the opposite of what we reported from him in the first section."
"1922","2045 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to us, from Nafi', he said: Ibn 'Umar used to combine two surahs in one rak'ah of the Maghrib prayer."
"1923","2046 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khattab ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar and Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to recite two and three surahs in one rak'ah."
"1924","2047 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Khattab ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, he said: Isma'il narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it. And he added: And he used to divide the long surah over the two rak'ahs of the obligatory prayer. And there has also been reported in that from 'Umar and others what indicates this meaning."
"1925","2048 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, led us in Fajr at Makkah, and recited in the first rak'ah Surah Yusuf until he reached: {And his eyes whitened with grief, and he was suppressed with sorrow}, then he bowed."
"1926","2049 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Amr ibn Maymun, he said: I performed Hajj with 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he recited in the last rak'ah of Maghrib {Have you not seen} and {For the accustomed security}."
"1927","2050 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid narrated to him, he said: I prayed with 'Abdullah the last 'Isha, and he opened al-Anfal until he reached: {An excellent Master and an excellent Helper}, then he bowed."
"1928","2051 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, from Ibn Sirin, he said: Tamim al-Dari used to keep the whole night alive with the whole Qur'an in one rak'ah."
"1929","2052 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, he said: I heard Abu al-Duha narrating from Masruq, he said: A man of the people of Makkah said to me: This is the station of your brother Tamim al-Dari; I saw him stand one night until morning, or almost morning, reciting one verse, bowing with it and prostrating, and weeping: {Or do those who commit evil deeds think}. The verse."
"1930","2053 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, that he recited the Qur'an in one rak'ah."
"1931","2054 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hammad, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: that he recited the Qur'an in one rak'ah, in the house."
"1932","2055 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: He led us in Maghrib prayer, and joined Surah al-Fil, {For the accustomed security of Quraysh} in one rak'ah. And this which we mentioned, with the consecutiveness of the narration in it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the multitude of those among his companions and their followers who adopted it, is the sound reasoning; for we have seen the Opening of the Book recited, it and another surah, in one rak'ah, with no harm in that, and no rak'ah is due for the Opening of the Book because it is a surah. So reasoning upon that is that likewise whatever besides it of surahs, no rak'ah is due for each surah of them either. And this is the school of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"1933","47 - Chapter of standing in the month of Ramadan: is it better in homes or with the imam? 2056 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Dawud, and he is Ibn Abi Hind, narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Hadrami, from Abu Dharr, he said: I fasted Ramadan with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not lead us in standing, until seven of the month remained. When it was the seventh night he came out and prayed with us until a third of the night passed; then he did not pray with us on the sixth, until he came out on the night of the fifth, and prayed with us until half the night passed. So we said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you would give us supererogatory prayer? So he said: When people pray with the imam until he finishes, the standing of that night is written for them. Then he did not pray with us on the fourth, until when it was the night of the third, he came out with his family, and prayed with us until we feared al-Falah would escape us. I said: And what is al-Falah? He said: Suhoor. Abu Ja'far said: So some people went to that standing with the imam in the month of Ramadan is better than in homes, and they adduced as proof the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that: Whoever stands with the imam until he finishes, the devotion of the rest of his night is written for him. And others opposed them in that, saying: Rather his prayer in his house is better than his prayer with the imam. And among the proof for them in that is that what they adduced of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that: Whoever stands with the imam until he finishes, the devotion of the rest of his night is written for him, is as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. But it has also been reported from him that he said: The best prayer of a man is in his house, except the obligatory, in the hadith of Zayd ibn Thabit. And that was when he had stood with them one night in Ramadan and they wanted him to stand with them after that, so he said to them this statement. So he informed them by it that their prayer in their homes alone is better than their prayer with him in his mosque; so their prayer in their homes is even more likely to be better than prayer with another than him in another than his mosque. So the authentication of the meanings of these two reports necessitates that the hadith of Abu Dharr is upon that there is written for him, by standing with the imam, the devotion of the rest of his night. And the hadith of Zayd ibn Thabit necessitates that what he does in his house is better than that, so that these two reports do not contradict."
"1934","2057 - Ibn Marzuq and 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu al-Nadr narrating from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) partitioned a chamber in the mosque of matting, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in it for nights, until people gathered to him; then they missed his voice, thinking he had slept, so some of them began clearing their throats for him to come out to them. So he said: That conduct of yours which I have seen since tonight has not ceased, until I feared the night standing would be prescribed upon you; and if it were prescribed upon you, you would not stand it. So pray, O people, in your houses; for the best prayer of a man is in his house, except the obligatory."
"1935","2058 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Bardan Ibrahim ibn Abi Fulan, and he is Ibn Abi al-Nadr, narrated to me, from his father, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The prayer of a man in his house is better than his prayer in this mosque of mine, except the obligatory."
"1936","2059 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asad and Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, they said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Abu al-Nadr, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best prayer of a man is his prayer in his house, except the obligatory. And there has been reported from other than Zayd ibn Thabit in that, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also, what we have mentioned in the chapter of voluntary prayer in mosques. So by the authentication of the meanings of these reports is established what we mentioned. And there has been reported in that, from those after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what agrees with what we authenticated it upon. Of that is what:"
"1937","2060 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he would not pray behind the imam in Ramadan."
"1938","2061 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: A man said to Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: Shall I pray behind the imam in Ramadan? He said: Do you recite the Qur'an? He said: Yes. He said: Pray in your house."
"1939","2062 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, and Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: If I had with me only two surahs I would repeat them; that is dearer to me than standing behind the imam in Ramadan."
"1940","2063 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: The night-worshippers used to pray in a corner of the mosque, while the imam prayed with the people in Ramadan."
"1941","2064 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: They used to pray in Ramadan, a man leading them, while some of the people prayed alone in the mosque. Shu'bah said: I asked Ishaq ibn Suwayd about this, and he said: The imam here used to lead us, and we had a row called the row of the reciters, so we would pray singly while the imam prayed with the people."
"1942","2065 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, he said: If I had with me only one surah, repeating it would be dearer to me than standing behind the imam in Ramadan."
"1943","2066 - Yunus and Fahd narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from 'Urwah, that he used to pray with the people in Ramadan, then leave for his home, and not stand with the people."
"1944","2067 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, he said: I know it only from Abu Bishr, that Sa'id ibn Jubayr used to pray in Ramadan in the mosque alone, while the imam prayed with them in it."
"1945","2068 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, he said: I saw al-Qasim, Salim, and Nafi' leaving the mosque in Ramadan, not standing with the people."
"1946","2069 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Ash'ath ibn Sulaym, he said: I came to Makkah, in Ramadan, in the time of Ibn al-Zubayr; the imam would pray with the people in the mosque, while some people prayed separately in the mosque. So these from whom we reported what we reported of these reports, all of them prefer their prayer alone in the month of Ramadan over their prayer with the imam, and that is the correct view."
"1947","48 - Chapter of al-Mufassal: is there prostration in it or not? 2070 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Abu Sakhr informed me, from Yazid ibn Qusayt, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, from his father, he said: I recited al-Najm to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and none of us prostrated."
"1948","2071 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh informed us, he said: Abu Sakhr informed us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1949","2072 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us. (Ha). 2073 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Qusayt, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Thabit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So some people adopted this hadith and followed it, and saw no prostration in al-Najm. And others opposed them in that, saying: Rather in it is a prostration; and there is not in this hadith, according to us, proof that there is no prostration in it, for it is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving prostration in it at that time was because he was without ablution, so he did not prostrate for that. And it is possible he left it because he was in a time in which prostration is not permissible. And it is possible he left it because the ruling with him in the prostration of recitation was: whoever wishes prostrates, and whoever wishes leaves it. And it is possible he left it because there is no prostration in it. So when his leaving prostration bore every one of these meanings, this hadith was not worthier of one meaning of them than another except by a proof indicating it from other than it. But we need to examine what comes after this hadith of hadiths to seek the ruling of this surah: is there prostration in it or no prostration in it?"
"1950","So we looked into that, and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had: 2074 - narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us. (Ha). 2075 - And 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Aswad, from 'Abdullah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: By al-Najm. And prostrated in it, and none remained but prostrated, except an old man, who took a handful of dust and said: This suffices me. 'Abdullah said: And I saw him afterwards killed as a disbeliever."
"1951","2076 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Mus'ab ibn Thabit, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited al-Najm. And prostrated, and the Muslims and the polytheists prostrated with him, until a man prostrated upon a man, and until a man prostrated upon something he raised to his face with his palm."
"1952","2077 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir and Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Harith ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: By al-Najm. And prostrated, and the people prostrated with him except two men who sought fame."
"1953","2078 - Ahmad ibn Mas'ud al-Khayyat narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: Makhlad ibn Husayn narrated to us, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: By al-Najm. And prostrated, and those present of jinn, men, and trees prostrated with him."
"1954","2079 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Abu Thabit al-Madani narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Hazim narrated to us, from al-'Ala', from his father, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, that he saw Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, prostrate at the end of al-Najm. Abu Salamah said: O Abu Hurayrah, did you see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrate in it? He said: Had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrate in it, I would not have prostrated in it."
"1955","2080 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from one who informed him, from Abu al-Darda', he said: I prostrated with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eleven prostrations, among them al-Najm."
"1956","2081 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from Ibn Tawus, from 'Ikrimah ibn Khalid, from al-Muttalib ibn Abi Wada'ah, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite al-Najm at Makkah, and prostrate; and I did not prostrate with him because I was not upon Islam. I shall never leave it. So in these reports is the confirmation of prostration in it; and there is not in what we mentioned in the first section what negates that in it is a prostration. So these are worthier, for it is not permissible to prostrate in other than a place of prostration. And it is permissible to leave prostration in its place, due to an impediment of the impediments we mentioned in the first section. If one says: In that is also a proof indicating that there is no prostration in it, then he mentioned what:"
"1957","2082 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn al-Husayn al-Luhbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to me, he said: Dawud ibn Qays narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, that he asked Ubayy ibn Ka'b: Is there a prostration in al-Mufassal? He said: No. He said: So Ubayy ibn Ka'b, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited to him the whole Qur'an; had there been prostration in al-Mufassal, he would have taught him the prostration of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it, when he came upon it in his recitation. And there is no proof for him in this, according to us, for it is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left that in it, for one of the meanings we mentioned in the first section. And a group of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held in the prostration of recitation that it is not obligatory, and that it harms the reciter not to do it. Of what was reported from them in that is what:"
"1958","2083 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him. (Ha). 2084 - And Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, recited the prostration verse while upon the minbar on Friday, then descended and prostrated, and they prostrated with him. Then he recited it on the next Friday, and they prepared for prostration, so 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Take it easy; Allah has not prescribed it upon us except if we wish. So he recited it and did not prostrate, and prevented them from prostrating."
"1959","2085 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, he said: Salman passed by people who had recited the prostration verse, and it was said: Will you not prostrate? He said: We did not intend it."
"1960","2086 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Abi Saghirah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, he said: Ibn al-Zubayr recited the prostration verse, while I was present, and did not prostrate. So al-Harith ibn 'Abdullah stood and prostrated, then said: O Commander of the Faithful, what prevented you from prostrating when you recited the prostration verse? He said: When I am in prayer I prostrate, and when I am not in prayer I do not prostrate. So these eminent ones did not consider it obligatory. And this is the sound reasoning according to us, for we saw them not disagreeing that the traveler, when he recites it while upon his mount, gestures with it, and it is not upon him to prostrate it upon the ground. So this was the description of voluntary, not the description of obligatory; for the obligatory is not prayed except upon the ground, while the voluntary is prayed upon the mount. And Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah be pleased with them, held in prostration the opposite of that, saying: It is obligatory. So it is established by what we described that what they mentioned from Ubayy has no proof in it that there is no prostration in al-Mufassal; for it is possible that the ruling in prostration with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon one of the meanings we mentioned in that from 'Umar, Salman, and Ibn al-Zubayr, so he left prostration in al-Mufassal for that. And perhaps he also did not prostrate in the recitation of what in it is prostration also of other than al-Mufassal. And a group of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed Ubayy ibn Ka'b in what he adopted of that."
"1961","2087 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr, from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The firm prostrations are Alif Lam Mim al-Tanzil, Ha Mim, al-Najm, and {Read in the name of your Lord}."
"1962","2088 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Asim, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1963","2089 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, led us in Fajr at Makkah, and recited in the second rak'ah al-Najm, then prostrated, then stood and recited: {When the earth is shaken}."
"1964","2090 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud, Wahb, and Rawh narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam narrated to us that he heard Ibrahim al-Taymi narrating from his father. He said: I prayed behind 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the like of it, and the wording is Rawh's."
"1965","2091 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Imran ibn 'Ubaydullah, or 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Imran, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that 'Umar prostrated in {When the sky splits asunder}."
"1966","2092 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Zayd, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Masruq, he said: I prayed behind 'Uthman the Fajr, and he recited al-Najm and prostrated in it, then stood and recited another surah."
"1967","2093 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that 'Umar and 'Abdullah - meaning Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both - prostrated in {When the sky splits asunder}. Mansur said: Or one of them."
"1968","2094 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1969","2095 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, he said: I saw 'Umar ibn al-Khattab and 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, prostrating in {When the sky splits asunder}."
"1970","2096 - Rawh narrated to us, he said: Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Layth, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from 'Abdullah. With that."
"1971","2097 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj informed me, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, prostrate in al-Najm in the Fajr prayer, then open with another surah."
"1972","2098 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik informed us, from al-Zuhri, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, led us in prayer and recited al-Najm, and prostrated in it."
"1973","2099 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar informed us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Bukayr, that Nafi' narrated to him that he saw Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, prostrating in {When the sky splits asunder} and {Read in the name of your Lord}, outside prayer."
"1974","2100 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Suwayd, he said: Nafi' was asked: Did Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, prostrate in al-Hajj two prostrations? He said: Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, died without reciting it, but he used to prostrate in al-Najm and in {Read in the name of your Lord}."
"1975","2101 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to prostrate in al-Najm."
"1976","2102 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, from Abu 'Abd al-Rahman: that Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to prostrate in {When the sky splits asunder}."
"1977","2103 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah, al-Thawri, and Hammad narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Zirr, that 'Ammar prostrated in it."
"1978","2104 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, that he used to prostrate in it. So these have opposed Ubayy ibn Ka'b in his statement: There is no prostration in al-Mufassal. And indeed:"
"1979","2105 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Zibyan, he said: Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said to me: Which recitation do you recite? I said: The first recitation, the recitation of Ibn Umm 'Abd. He said: It is the last recitation. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had the Qur'an presented to him every year - he said: I think he said: in every month of Ramadan - then when it was the year in which he died, he presented it to him twice. So 'Abdullah witnessed what was abrogated and what was altered. So this 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, has informed that 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, attended the recitation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the Qur'an twice, in the year in which he was taken. So he knew what was abrogated and what was altered. So if in the recitation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Ubayy ibn Ka'b was what indicated that Ubayy knew what in it of prostration of the Qur'an, until his statement: There is no prostration in al-Mufassal became proof that it is so with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then the attendance of Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, of the recitation of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the Qur'an twice is proof that he knew what in it of prostration of the Qur'an. So his statement: Al-Mufassal is of prostration, what we reported from him, became proof. And some people said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to prostrate in al-Mufassal at Makkah, then when he emigrated he left that. And they reported that from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, by a weak chain, the like of which is not established; and they reported from him of his statement: There is no prostration in al-Mufassal."
"1980","2106 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khatib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', that he asked Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about the prostration of the Qur'an, and he did not count for him in al-Mufassal anything. And this, according to us, even if established, would be unsound; for Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, we have reported from him in this chapter that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in al-Najm, and that he was present at that, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in {When the sky splits asunder}. And the Islam of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and his meeting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was only at Madinah three years before his death - and we have reported that from him in its places of this book of ours - so that indicates the unsoundness of what the people of that view adopted. And the reports have also consecutively come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his prostration in al-Mufassal. Of that is what:"
"1981","2107 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Qurrah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Ibn Shihab. And Safwan ibn Sulaym, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I prostrated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in: {When the sky splits asunder} and {Read in the name of your Lord who created}, two prostrations."
"1982","2108 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn 'Abdullah, from Nu'aym al-Mujmir, that he said: I prayed with Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, above this mosque, and he recited {When the sky splits asunder} and prostrated in it, and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrate in it."
"1983","2109 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Zayd informed us, from Abu Rafi', he said: I prayed behind Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, at Madinah, and he recited: {When the sky splits asunder} and prostrated in it. When he finished his prayer I met him and said: Do you prostrate in it? He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrate in it, so I shall never leave that."
"1984","2110 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Abu Rafi' narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, except that he did not mention his statement: (I shall never leave that ever)."
"1985","2111 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Marwan al-Asfar, he narrated to him, from Abu Rafi', and he mentioned the like of it with its chain, and added: (I shall never leave that until I meet Him)."
"1986","2112 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: al-Thawri, Ibn Jurayj, and Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ayyub ibn Musa, from 'Ata' ibn Mina, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We prostrated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in {When the sky splits asunder}."
"1987","2112 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: 'Ata' ibn Mina narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We prostrated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in: {Read in the name of your Lord} and {When the sky splits asunder}."
"1988","2113 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Rawh narrated to us, and the wording is Abu Dawud's, they said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he saw him prostrate in: {When the sky splits asunder}, and he said: Had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrate in it, I would not have prostrated."
"1989","2114 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"1990","2115 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us. (Ha). 2116 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar narrated to us, they said: Malik narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Abu Salamah: that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, recited with them {When the sky splits asunder} and prostrated in it; and when he finished he informed them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in it."
"1991","2117 - Ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, that he saw Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, prostrating in {When the sky splits asunder}. Abu Salamah said: So I said to him, when he finished: You prostrated in a surah in which I have not seen the people prostrate. He said: Had I not seen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrate in it, I would not have prostrated."
"1992","2118 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Ayyash, from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in {When the sky splits asunder}."
"1993","2119 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from two men, both of them better than Abu Hurayrah, that one of them prostrated in {When the sky splits asunder} and in {Read in the name of your Lord who created}, and the one who prostrated was better than the one who did not prostrate; and if it was not 'Umar, then he is better than 'Umar. So this Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, the narrations have consecutively come from him that he prostrated with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also in {When the sky splits asunder}. And his Islam was only at Madinah, so how is it permissible to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), after he emigrated, did not prostrate in al-Mufassal? And there has been reported from 'Amr ibn al-'As, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the prostration of al-Mufassal also what:"
"1994","2120 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from al-'Ala' ibn Kathir, from al-Harith ibn Sa'id al-Kindi, from 'Abdullah ibn Munayn al-Yahsabi, that 'Amr ibn al-'As prostrated in {When the sky splits asunder} and in: {Read in the name of your Lord who created}. So it was said to him about that, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to prostrate in them. So these reports have consecutively come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prostration in al-Mufassal; by them we say, and it is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. As for the reasoning in that, upon other than this meaning, it is that we saw the agreed-upon prostration is ten prostrations. Among them in al-A'raf, and the place of prostration in it is His statement: {Those who are with your Lord do not disdain His worship, and they glorify Him, and to Him they prostrate}. And among them al-Ra'd, and the place of prostration at His statement, mighty and majestic: {And to Allah prostrate whoever is in the heavens and the earth, willingly and unwillingly, and their shadows in the mornings and the evenings}. And among them al-Nahl, and the place of prostration in it at His statement, the Exalted: {And to Allah prostrate whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on earth of creatures} to His statement: {they are commanded}. And among them in Surah Bani Isra'il, and the place of prostration in it at His statement, the Exalted: {They fall upon their chins in prostration} to His statement: {in humility}. And among them Surah Maryam, and the place of prostration in it at His statement: {When the verses of the Most Merciful are recited to them, they fall in prostration and weeping}. And among them Surah al-Hajj, in it a prostration at its beginning at His statement: {Have you not seen that to Allah prostrate whoever is in the heavens and whoever is on earth} to the end of the verse. And among them Surah al-Furqan, and the place of prostration in it at His statement: {And when it is said to them: Prostrate to the Most Merciful} to the end of the verse. And among them Surah al-Naml, in it a prostration at His statement, the Exalted: {Why do they not prostrate to Allah who brings forth the hidden} to the end of the verse. And among them Alif Lam Mim al-Tanzil, the prostration in it a prostration at His statement, the Exalted: {Only those believe in Our verses who} to the end of the verse. And among them Ha Mim Tanzil from the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, and the place of prostration in it is disputed: some said its place is {you worship}, and some said its place is: {But if they are arrogant, those with your Lord glorify Him by night and day, and they do not tire}. And Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them, adopted this last school. And the early ones differed in that."
"1995","2121 - So Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Fitr ibn Khalifah informed us, from Mujahid, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to prostrate in the last verse of: {Ha Mim Tanzil}."
"1996","2122 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Fitr narrated to us, from Mujahid, he said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about the prostration in: {Ha Mim}. He said: Prostrate at the last of the two verses."
"1997","2123 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Mujahid, he said: A man prostrated in the first verse of {Ha Mim}, so Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: This one hastened with prostration."
"1998","2124 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Abu Wa'il, that he used to prostrate in the last verse."
"1999","2125 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Awn informed us, from Ibn Sirin the like of it."
"2000","2126 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid the like of it."
"2001","2127 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, from Qatadah the like of it."
"2002","2128 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid mention that 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, used to prostrate in the first verse of {Ha Mim}."
"2003","2129 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from a man, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it. So this prostration in Ha Mim is of what is agreed upon, while its place is disputed. And what we mentioned before this of prostration in the other surahs, they agreed upon them and upon their places which we mentioned; and the place of each prostration of them is a place of information, not a place of command. And we have seen prostration mentioned in places of command, among them His statement, the Exalted: {O Maryam, be devout to your Lord and prostrate}, and among them His statement: {And be of those who prostrate}. So all agreed that there is no prostration in any of that. So reasoning upon that is that every place of what is disputed, is there prostration in it or not, we look into it: if it is a place of command, it is only instruction, so no prostration in it. And every place in it information about prostration, it is a place of prostration of recitation. So the disputed place of Surah al-Najm: some said it is the place of prostration of recitation, and others said it is not a place of prostration of recitation, and it is His statement: {So prostrate to Allah and worship}. That is a command, not information. So reasoning, upon what we mentioned, is that it not be a place of prostration of recitation. And the disputed place also of {Read in the name of your Lord} is His statement: {No! Do not obey him; prostrate and draw near}. That is a command, not information. So reasoning, upon what we mentioned, is that it not be a place of prostration of recitation. And the disputed place of {When the sky splits asunder}, is it a place of prostration or not, is His statement: {So what is with them that they do not believe, and when the Qur'an is recited to them they do not prostrate}. That is a place of information, not a place of command. So reasoning, upon what we mentioned, is that it be a place of prostration of recitation; and everything of prostration be returned to what we mentioned: what of it was a command be returned to its like of what we mentioned, so no prostration in it; and what of it was information be returned to its like of the informations, so prostration in it. So this is the reasoning in this chapter. So it would follow upon that that the place of prostration of Ha Mim be the place to which Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, went; for it, according to him, is information: His statement: {But if they are arrogant, those with your Lord glorify Him by night and day, and they do not tire}, not as those who opposed him went, for they made the prostration at a command: His statement: {And prostrate to Allah who created them, if Him you worship}. So that was a place of command, while the other place was a place of information; and we mentioned that reasoning necessitates prostration be in places of information, not in places of command. So it would follow upon that that there be in Surah al-Hajj only one prostration, for the second disputed one, its place in the statement of whoever makes it a prostration is a place of command: His statement: {Bow and prostrate and worship your Lord}, the verse. And we clarified that the places of prostration of recitation are places of informations, not places of command. So had we been left with reasoning, the statement in the prostration of recitation would be that we look: what of it was a place of command, we make no prostration in it; and what of it was a place of information, we make prostration in it. But following what is established from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is worthier."
"2004","2130 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from 'Iyad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Sa'd, from Abu Sa'id, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prostrated in Sad."
"2005","2131 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: al-'Awwam ibn Hawshab informed us, he said: I asked Mujahid about prostration in Sad. He said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas about it, and he said: Prostrate in Sad. And he recited to me these verses of al-An'am: {And of his offspring Dawud and Sulayman} to His statement: {Those are whom Allah guided, so by their guidance follow}. So Dawud was of those your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to follow."
"2006","2132 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn 'Abbas was asked about the prostration in Sad. He said: {Those are whom Allah guided, so by their guidance follow}. So by this we take; we see prostration in Sad following what has been reported in it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and what reasoning necessitated."
"2007","Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Rawh narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Sa'd ibn Ibrahim informed me, he said: I heard a nephew of ours called 'Abdullah ibn Tha'labah say: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, led us in Fajr as far as I know - Sa'd said: He led us in Fajr - and recited al-Hajj and prostrated in it two prostrations."
"2008","2133 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Muhriz, that Abu Musa al-Ash'ari prostrated in it two prostrations."
"2009","2134 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it."
"2010","2135 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khumayr, he said: I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr and Khalid ibn Ma'dan narrating from Jubayr ibn Nufayr that he saw Abu al-Darda' prostrate in al-Hajj two prostrations."
"2011","2136 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abd al-A'la al-Tha'labi, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said concerning the prostration of al-Hajj: The first is firm and the second is instruction. So by this statement of Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, we take. And all what we adopted in this chapter of what the reports brought is the statement of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"2012","49 - Chapter of a man who prays in his lodging then comes to the mosque while the people pray. 2137 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from Busr ibn Mihjan al-Di'li, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he saw him while prayer had been established. He said: So I sat and did not stand for prayer; when he finished his prayer he said to me: Are you not a Muslim? I said: Yes. He said: What prevented you from praying with us? I said: I had prayed with my family. He said: Pray with the people even if you prayed with your family."
"2013","2138 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to me, from Busr ibn Mihjan al-Di'li, from his father, he said: I prayed in my house Zuhr or 'Asr, then went out to the mosque, and found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated with his companions around him; then prayer was established, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2014","2139 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us. (Ha). 2140 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Busr ibn Mihjan al-Di'li, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Except that he did not mention which prayer it is."
"2015","2141 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Busr ibn Mihjan al-Di'li, from his father or from his uncle, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2016","2142 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jurayj narrated to us. (Ha). 2143 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu 'Imran, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: My close friend enjoined me to pray prayer at its time; and if you catch the imam having preceded you, you have secured your prayer, otherwise it is supererogatory for you."
"2017","2144 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Shu'bah, he said: Budayl narrated to us, from Abu al-'Aliyah, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Samit, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, raising it, he said: He struck my thigh and said to me: How will you be when you remain among people who delay prayer from its time. Then he said to me: Pray prayer at its time, then go out; and if you are in the mosque and prayer is established, pray with them, and do not say: I have prayed so I shall not pray."
"2018","2145 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Ya'la ibn 'Ata' informed me, he said: I heard Jabir ibn Yazid ibn al-Aswad al-Sawa'i, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in Masjid al-Khayf in Fajr; when he finished his prayer, two men were seated at the back of the mosque, and they were brought with their sides trembling. He said: What prevented you from praying with us? They said: O Messenger of Allah, we prayed in our lodgings. He said: Do not do so; when you pray in your lodgings, then come to the people while they pray, pray with them, for it is supererogatory for you - or he said: voluntary. Abu Ja'far said: Some people adopted these reports, saying: When a man prays in his house an obligatory prayer, whichever prayer it is, then comes to the mosque and finds the people praying, he prays it with them. And others opposed them in that, saying: Every prayer after which voluntary is permissible, there is no harm to do in it what you mentioned of his praying it with the imam, as supererogatory for him, except Maghrib, for they disliked its repetition; for if repeated it would be voluntary, and voluntary is not odd, it is only even. And every prayer after which voluntary is not permissible, he should not repeat it with the imam; for it would be voluntary in a time in which voluntary is not permissible. And they adduced as proof in that what the narrations consecutively came with from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his forbidding prayer after 'Asr until the sun sets, and after Fajr until the sun rises. And we mentioned that with its chains in other than this place of this book of ours. So that, according to them, abrogates what we reported at the beginning of this chapter. And they said: When he clarified in some of the first hadiths, saying: Pray them, for they are supererogatory for you - or he said: voluntary - and forbade voluntary in these other reports, and consensus was reached upon acting with them, that was included in them, abrogating what preceded it of what contradicted it. And of those reports is what he did not say in: for they are voluntary for you. So that could mean the meaning of this in which he clarified, saying: for they are voluntary for you. And it could be that was in a time when they prayed the obligatory twice, so both would be obligatory, then they were forbidden that. Upon whichever of the two matters it was, what we mentioned has abrogated it. And of those who said that of prayers only Zuhr and the last 'Isha are repeated: Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And there has been reported in that, from a group of the early ones, what...:"
"2019","2146 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to us, from Na'im ibn Ujayl, freed slave of Umm Salamah, he said: I used to enter the mosque for Maghrib prayer, and see men of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated at the back of the mosque, while the people prayed in it, having prayed in their houses. So these of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not pray Maghrib in the mosque, since they had prayed it in their houses; nor would others of the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disapprove that of them. So that is proof, according to us, of the abrogation of what had preceded of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); for it is not permissible that the like of that of the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) escaped all of them, so that they be upon its opposite. Rather that was from them due to what was established with them in it of the abrogation of that statement. And there has been reported in that also from Ibn 'Umar and others what:"
"2020","2147 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me that Ibn 'Umar said: If you pray among your family then catch the prayer, pray it, except Fajr and Maghrib; for they are not repeated in a day."
"2021","2148 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim that he disliked the repetition of Maghrib except that a man fear authority, so he prays it, then makes even with a rak'ah."
"2022","50 - Chapter of a man who enters the mosque on Friday while the imam preaches: should he bow or not? 2149 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Sulayk al-Ghatafani came on Friday, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon the minbar; Sulayk sat before praying, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Have you bowed two rak'ahs? He said: No. He said: Stand and bow them."
"2023","2150 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir that a man entered the mosque on Friday, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2024","2151 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, he said: 'Amr ibn Dinar informed me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah say, and he mentioned the like of it."
"2025","2152 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: ... narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Ishkab al-Kufi narrated to us, Mu'awiyah, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Sulayk al-Ghatafani came on Friday while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached, and sat. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you comes on Friday while the imam preaches, let him pray two light rak'ahs, then sit."
"2026","2153 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Salih mentioning the hadith of Sulayk al-Ghatafani. Then I heard Abu Sufyan after that say: I heard Jabir say: Sulayk al-Ghatafani came on Friday while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Stand, O Sulayk, and pray two light rak'ahs, be brief in them. Then he said: When one of you comes while the imam preaches, let him pray two light rak'ahs, being brief in them."
"2027","2154 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn 'Isa narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from Sulayk ibn Hudbah al-Ghatafani: that he came while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preached upon the minbar on Friday, and he said to him: Have you bowed two rak'ahs? He said: No. He said: Pray two rak'ahs and be brief in them."
"2028","2155 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Ibn 'Ajlan narrated to me, from 'Iyad ibn 'Abdullah, he informed him, from Abu Sa'id, that a man entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was upon the minbar; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called to him, and kept saying: Come near, until he drew near. So he ordered him, and he bowed two rak'ahs before sitting, while upon him was a worn-out rag. Then he did the like of that in the second, and he ordered him with the like of that. Then he did the like of that in the third Friday, and he ordered him with the like of that. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the people: Give charity. So they cast garments. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered him to take two garments. Then after that he ordered the people to give charity, so the man cast one of his two garments. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry, then ordered him to take his garment. Abu Ja'far said: So some people went to that whoever enters the mosque on Friday while the imam is upon the minbar preaching, he should bow two rak'ahs, being brief in them. And they adduced as proof in that these reports. And others opposed them in that, saying: He should sit and not bow, while the imam preaches. And among the proof for them in that is that it is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordering Sulayk with what he ordered him of that, interrupting thereby his sermon, intending thereby to teach the people how they do when they enter the mosque, then resumed the sermon. And it is also possible that he built upon his sermon, and that was before speech in prayer was abrogated, then speech in prayer was abrogated, so it was abrogated also in the sermon. And it is possible that what he ordered him of that is as the people of the first view said, and it be an acted-upon sunnah."
"2029","2156 - So we looked: has anything been reported contradicting that? Bahr ibn Nasr had narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: I heard Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrating from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from 'Abdullah ibn Busr, he said: I was seated beside him on Friday, and he said: A man came stepping over the necks of people on Friday. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Sit, for you have harmed and delayed. Abu al-Zahiriyyah said: And we used to talk until the imam came out. So do you not see that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered this man to sit, and did not order him to pray. So this contradicts the hadith of Sulayk. And in the hadith of Abu Sa'id which we reported in the first section is what indicates that was in the state of permissibility of actions in the sermon before they were forbidden; do you not see him say: (So the people cast their garments). And the Muslims unanimously agree that a man removing his garment while the imam preaches is disliked, and that his touching pebbles while the imam preaches is disliked, and that his saying to his companion (Be quiet) while the imam preaches is also disliked. So that is proof that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Sulayk and the man upon whom he ordered charity, was in a state, the ruling in it in that contrary to the ruling in what is after. And the narrations consecutively came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that whoever says to his companion: Be quiet, while the imam preaches on Friday, has engaged in idle talk."
"2030","2157 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you say to your companion: Be quiet, while the imam preaches, you have engaged in idle talk."
"2031","2158 - Narrated to us Abu Umayyah, he said: narrated to us Abu Ghassan, he said: narrated to us al-Qasim ibn Ma'n, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the same as it."
"2032","2159 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abu Salih, he said: narrated to me al-Layth, he said: narrated to me Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz informed me, from Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Qariz and from Ibn al-Musayyib, that they both narrated to him from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he heard him saying: If you say to your companion 'be silent' while the imam is delivering the sermon on Friday, you have engaged in idle talk. So if a man's saying to his companion while the imam preaches 'be silent' is idle talk, then the imam's saying to a man 'stand and pray' is also idle talk. Thus it is established that the time in which the command from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Sulayk of what he commanded him was of a ruling different from the ruling of the time in which the like of that was made idle talk. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the like of that what:"
"2033","2160 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq, they said: narrated to us Makki ibn Ibrahim, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Sa'id, from Harb ibn Qays, from Abu al-Darda that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat on the pulpit on a Friday delivering the sermon to the people, and he recited a verse, and by my side was Ubayy ibn Ka'b, so I said to him: O Ubayy, when was this verse revealed? He refused to speak to me until when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) descended from the pulpit, he said: You have nothing of your Friday except what you engaged in idly. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned away, so I came to him and informed him, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, you recited a verse while by my side was Ubayy ibn Ka'b, so I asked him: when was this verse revealed? He refused to speak to me, until when you descended he claimed that I have nothing of my Friday except what I engaged in idly. He said: He spoke the truth; when you hear your imam speaking, be silent until he finishes."
"2034","2161 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was delivering the sermon on Friday and recited a surah. So Abu Dharr said to Ubayy ibn Ka'b: when was this surah revealed? He turned away from him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, Ubayy said to Abu Dharr: You have nothing of your prayer except what you engaged in idly. So Abu Dharr entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of that, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ubayy spoke the truth. Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded silence at the sermon, and made its ruling in that like the ruling of prayer, and made speech during it idle talk. Thus it is established that prayer during it is disliked. So if the people are forbidden from speech while the imam preaches, likewise the imam is forbidden from speech while he preaches other than the sermon. Do you not see that the followers are prevented from speech in prayer? So likewise is the imam. So whatever other than the imam is prevented from, the imam is prevented from it. So likewise, when other than the imam is prevented from speech during the sermon, the imam is also thereby prevented from speech during the sermon of what is other than it. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning that also what:"
"2035","2162 - Narrated to us Ibn Marzuq and Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Baghandi, they said: narrated to us Abu al-Walid, he said: narrated to us Abu Awanah, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Qartha', from Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know what Friday is? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. Then he said: Do you know what Friday is? I said on the third or fourth time: It is the day in which your father was gathered. He said: No, but I inform you about Friday: there is no one who purifies himself, then walks to Friday, then listens attentively until the imam finishes his prayer, except it will be an expiation for what is between it and the previous Friday, as long as he avoids the deadly sin."
"2036","2163 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Dawud, he said: narrated to us al-Himmani, he said: narrated to us Abu Awanah, from Mughirah, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2037","2164 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us al-Wahbi, he said: narrated to us Ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman and from Abu Umamah, that they both narrated to him from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri and from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever performs ghusl on Friday and cleans his teeth, and touches perfume if he has it, and wears of his best clothes, then goes out until he comes to the mosque, and does not step over people's necks, then bows whatever Allah wills him to bow, and listens attentively until the imam comes out, it will be an expiation for what is between it and the previous Friday."
"2038","2165 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Muhammad, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and Abu Sa'id, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2039","2166 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Munqidh, he said: narrated to us Ibn Wahb, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Whoever performs ghusl on Friday, then touches of his wife's perfume, and wears his best clothes, and does not step over people's necks, and does not engage in idle talk at the admonition, it will be an expiation for what is between the two."
"2040","2167 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abu Mushir, he said: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari, from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani, from Aws ibn Aws, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever washes and performs ghusl and goes early and arrives at the beginning, and draws near to the imam and listens attentively and does not engage in idle talk, he will have for the place of every step the deed of a year, its fasting and its standing in prayer."
"2041","2168 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Ahmad, he said: narrated to us Sufyan, from Abd Allah ibn Isa, from Yahya ibn al-Harith, and he mentioned the like of it with his chain."
"2042","2169 - Narrated to us Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb, he said: narrated to us Asad, he said: narrated to us Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, he said: my father informed me, from Ubayd Allah ibn Wadi'ah, from Salman al-Khayr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For a man to perform ghusl on Friday and purify himself with what he is able of purification, then oil himself from oil or touch of his household's perfume, then go, and not separate between two persons, and pray what Allah has decreed for him, then listen attentively when the imam speaks, he is forgiven what is between him and the other Friday. So in these reports also is the command to be silent when the imam speaks, and that is evidence that the place of the imam's speech is not a place of prayer. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. As for the aspect of reasoning, we saw them not differing that whoever was in the mosque before the imam preaches, the imam's sermon prevents him from prayer, so he becomes thereby in other than a place of prayer. So by analogy, one who enters the mosque while the imam preaches enters into other than a place of prayer, so he should not pray. And we saw the agreed-upon principle that the times which prevent prayer are equal regarding one who was before them in the mosque and one who enters the mosque during them in preventing them both from prayer. So when the sermon prevents one who was before it in the mosque from prayer, likewise it prevents one who enters the mosque after the imam's entering into it from prayer. This is the aspect of reasoning in that, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them. And there have been narrated concerning that reports from a group of the predecessors."
"2043","2170 - Narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Wahb, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Tawbah al-Anbari, he said: al-Sha'bi said: Did you see al-Hasan when he comes while the imam has come out and he prays, from whom did he take this? I saw Shurayh, when he came while the imam had come out, he did not pray."
"2044","2171 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abu Salih, he said: narrated to me al-Layth, he said: narrated to me Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, concerning a man who enters the mosque on Friday while the imam preaches, he said: He sits and does not glorify, i.e. he does not pray."
"2045","2172 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn al-Hasan, he said: narrated to us Ali ibn Asim, from Khalid al-Hadhdha that Abu Qilabah came on Friday while the imam preached, so he sat and did not pray."
"2046","2173 - Narrated to us Rawh ibn al-Faraj, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah informed us, from Ibn Hubayrah, from Abu al-Mus'ab, from Uqbah ibn Amir, he said: Prayer while the imam is on the pulpit is disobedience."
"2047","2174 - Narrated to us Yunus, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Tha'labah ibn Abi Malik al-Qurazi informed me that the imam's sitting on the pulpit interrupts prayer, and his speech interrupts speech. And he said: They used to converse when Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, sat on the pulpit until the mu'adhdhin fell silent, then when Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, stood on the pulpit, no one spoke until he finished both his sermons, then when Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, descended from the pulpit and finished his sermons, they spoke."
"2048","2175 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Isma'il ibn al-Khalil, he said: narrated to us Ali ibn Mushir, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: I saw Abd Allah ibn Safwan enter the mosque on Friday while Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr preached on the pulpit, wearing a waist-wrap, a cloak, sandals, and turbaned with a turban, so he touched the Corner then said: Peace be upon you, O Commander of the Believers, and Allah's mercy and His blessings, then he sat and did not bow."
"2049","2176 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Asim, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: It was said to Alqamah: Do you speak while the imam preaches, or after the imam has come out? He said: No. Then a man said to him: Shall I recite my portion while the imam preaches? Isa said: If it harms you, and perhaps it will not harm you."
"2050","2177 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Dawud, he said: narrated to us Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad, he said: narrated to us Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad, he said: narrated to us al-Hajjaj, he said: narrated to us Ata, he said: Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, used to dislike speech when the imam came out on Friday."
"2051","2178 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Abu Asim, from Sufyan, from Layth, from Mujahid that he disliked that one pray while the imam preaches. Thus we have narrated in these reports that the emergence of the imam interrupts prayer, and that Abd Allah ibn Safwan came while Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr preached, so he sat and did not bow, and neither Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr nor those present of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their followers disapproved that of him. Then Shurayh used to do that, and al-Sha'bi narrated it and argued against whoever opposed him, and that was strengthened by the narration from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what we have mentioned. Then from sound reasoning, what we have described, so what has been established by that should not be abandoned for other than it. If one says: It has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: When one of you enters the mosque, let him not sit until he bows two rak'ahs. And he mentioned concerning that what:"
"2052","2179 - Narrated to us Yunus, he said: narrated to us Sufyan, from Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman, he heard Amir ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr informing from Amr ibn Sulaym, from Abu Qatadah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you enters the mosque, let him bow two rak'ahs before he sits."
"2053","2180 - Narrated to us Rabi' al-Jizi, he said: narrated to us Abu al-Aswad, he said: narrated to us Bakr ibn Mudar, from Ibn al-Ajlan, from Amir ibn Abd Allah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2054","2181 - Narrated to us Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: narrated to us al-Qa'nabi, he said: narrated to us Malik, from Amir ibn Abd Allah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2055","2182 - Narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Abu Ishaq al-Darir, meaning Ibrahim ibn Abi Zakariyya, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from Amir ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from Amr ibn Sulaym al-Zuraqi, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So this indicates that whoever enters the mosque while the imam preaches should not sit until he prays two rak'ahs. It is said to him: There is in that no evidence for what you mentioned; this is only upon one who enters the mosque in a state in which prayer is permissible, not upon one who enters the mosque in a state in which prayer is not permissible. Do you not see that one who enters the mosque at sunrise or at sunset, or in one of these times forbidden for prayer therein, he should not pray, and he is not of those whom the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded to pray two rak'ahs upon entering the mosque, because he has forbidden prayer at that time. So likewise one who enters the mosque while the imam preaches has not to pray, and he is not of those whom the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded with that. Rather, there enters into the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which you mentioned everyone who, had he been in the mosque before that and preferred to pray, that would be his. As for one who, had he been in the mosque before that, it would not be his to pray at that time, he is not included in that, and he has not to pray, by analogy to what we mentioned of the ruling of the times forbidden for prayer therein which we described."
"2056","Chapter 51: A man enters the mosque while the imam is in the Fajr prayer and he has not bowed; does he bow or not? 2183 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Abu Asim, from Zakariyya ibn Ishaq, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: When the prayer is established, there is no prayer except the prescribed one."
"2057","2184 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man, he said: narrated to us Abu Mus'ab, he said: narrated to us Abd al-Aziz: Ahmad al-Isbahani said: the correct is Ibrahim ibn Isma'il, from Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn Mujammi' al-Ansari, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this hadith, and they disliked for a man to bow the two rak'ahs of Fajr in the mosque while the imam is in the Fajr prayer. Others opposed them in that and said: There is no harm in his bowing them without mixing with the rows, as long as he does not fear missing the two rak'ahs with the imam. And of the proof for them against the people of the first saying was that the origin of that hadith which they used as proof is from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); thus narrated it the memorizers from Amr ibn Dinar."
"2058","2185 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Umar al-Darir, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammad ibn Zayd informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, with that, and he did not raise it. So the origin of this hadith became from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in that, whose narrations we shall mention at the end of this chapter, Allah Almighty willing."
"2059","2186 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us Abu Salih, he said: narrated to me al-Layth, from Abd Allah ibn Ayyash ibn Abbas al-Qitbani, from his father, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: When the prayer is established, there is no prayer except the one for which it was established. So it is possible that he meant by this the prohibition of praying other than it in its very place in which it is prayed, so that its performer would have joined it with a voluntary prayer; so the prohibition is on account of that, not on account of praying at the back of the mosque then the one who prays it moving from that place, mixing with the rows, and entering into the obligation. And of what the people of the first saying used as proof for their saying also was what:"
"2060","2187 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Ma'bad, he said: narrated to us Yunus ibn Muhammad, he said: narrated to us Hammad, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Hafs ibn Asim, from Malik ibn Buhaynah that he said: The Fajr prayer was established, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon a man praying the two rak'ahs of Fajr, so he stood over him and the people gathered around him, and he said: Do you pray it four? Three times."
"2061","2188 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Dawud, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Sa'd, and he mentioned the like of it with his chain, except that he did not say: and the people gathered around him."
"2062","2189 - Narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Wahb, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah, and he mentioned the like of it with his chain, except that he did not say: three times. So against the people of this saying, the people of the other saying argue that it is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked that only because he prayed the two rak'ahs then joined them with the morning prayer, without having moved forward or spoken. So if it was for that that he said to him what he said, then this is a hadith upon which both parties agree together. So we wanted to look, has there been narrated concerning that anything indicating anything of that?"
"2063","2190 - So when Ibrahim ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: narrated to us Harun ibn Isma'il, he said: narrated to us Ali ibn al-Mubarak, he said: narrated to us Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by Abd Allah ibn Malik ibn Buhaynah while he was standing upright, i.e. standing praying there before the morning call, and he said: Do not make this prayer like a prayer before noon and after it, and make between them a separation. So this hadith clarifies that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked for Ibn Buhaynah was his joining it with the obligation in one place, without separating between them with anything, and not because he disliked for him to pray them in the mosque if, having finished them, he moved forward to the rows and prayed the obligation with the people. And the like of that has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in other than this hadith."
"2064","2191 - Narrated to us Abu Zur'ah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr, he said: narrated to us Abu al-Ashhab Hawdhah ibn Khalifah al-Bakrawi, he said: narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, from Umar ibn Ata ibn Abi al-Khuwar, that Nafi' ibn Jubayr sent him to al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid asking him: What did he hear from Mu'awiyah concerning prayer after Friday? So he said: I prayed Friday with Mu'awiyah in the enclosure, then when I finished I stood to perform voluntary prayer, so he took my garment and said: Do not do, until you move forward or speak, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to command that."
"2065","2192 - Narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Abu Asim, from Ibn Jurayj, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2066","2193 - Narrated to us Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin, he said: narrated to us Asad, he said: narrated to us Ibn Lahi'ah, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn al-Mughirah, from Safwan, freedman of Umar, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Do not join the prescribed prayer with the like of it of voluntary prayer in one standing place. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade in these hadiths joining the prescribed prayer with a voluntary one, until there be between them a separator of moving forward to another place or other than that. And the people of the first saying also argued for their saying with what:"
"2067","2194 - Narrated to us Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin, he said: narrated to us Asad, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah and Hammad ibn Zayd, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abd Allah ibn Sarjis, that a man came while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the morning prayer, so he bowed two rak'ahs, in the hadith of Hammad ibn Salamah: behind the people, then entered with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the prayer. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he said: O so-and-so, which of your prayers have you made, the one you prayed with us, or the one you prayed alone?"
"2068","2195 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Amir, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah. (Ha). 2196 - And narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Mu'ammal, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, from Asim, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. They said: So in this hadith is that he prayed them behind the people, yet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade him them. So of the proof against them for the others is that it is possible that his saying: he was behind the people, i.e. he was behind their rows, with no separation between him and them, so he was like one mixing with them, so that also enters into the meaning of what became clear from the hadith of Ibn Buhaynah, and this is disliked according to us; rather he should pray them at the back of the mosque, then walk from that place to the front of the mosque. As for praying them mixed with those praying the obligation, then no."
"2069","2197 - And: narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Abu Amir, from Abu Dhi'b, from Shu'bah, he said: Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say: O people, do you not fear Allah, separate your prayers. He said: And Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, used not to pray the two rak'ahs after Maghrib except in his house. So Abd Allah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, intended from them separation between the obligation and the voluntary, and that is what was intended in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and Ibn Buhaynah, and Ibn Sarjis, and Allah knows best. Abu Ja'far said: And we also deem desirable separation between the obligations and the voluntary prayers, with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded, in what we have narrated in this chapter, and we see no harm for one who had not bowed the two rak'ahs of Fajr until he came to the mosque, while the imam has entered into the morning prayer, to bow them at the back of the mosque, then walk to its front, and pray with the people. Do you not see that if that were in noon, afternoon, or night prayer, there would be no harm in it, and its doer would not be joining between an obligation and a voluntary prayer; so likewise if it is in morning prayer there is no harm in it, and its doer is not joining between an obligation and a voluntary prayer. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them. And it has been narrated from eminent predecessors."
"2070","2198 - Narrated to us Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb, he said: narrated to us Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, he said: narrated to us Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah, from Abu Ishaq, he said: narrated to me Abd Allah ibn Abi Musa, from his father, when Sa'id ibn al-As invited them, he invited Abu Musa, Hudhayfah, and Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them, before he prayed the morning prayer, then they left him while the prayer had been established, so Abd Allah sat at a pillar of the mosque and prayed the two rak'ahs, then entered into the prayer. So this Abd Allah did this while Hudhayfah and Abu Musa with him did not disapprove that of him, so that indicated their agreement with him."
"2071","2199 - Narrated to us Sulayman, he said: narrated to us Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: narrated to us Sufyan, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Musa, from Abd Allah that he entered the mosque while the imam was in prayer, so he prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr."
"2072","2200 - Narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mu'min al-Khurasani, he said: narrated to us Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq, he said: al-Husayn ibn Waqid informed us, he said: narrated to us Yazid al-Nahwi, from Abu Mijlaz, he said: I entered the mosque in the morning prayer with Ibn Umar and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, while the imam prayed. As for Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he entered into the row, and as for Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he prayed two rak'ahs then entered with the imam. When the imam gave salam, Ibn Umar sat in his place until the sun rose, then stood and bowed two rak'ahs. So this Ibn Abbas prayed two rak'ahs in the mosque while the imam was in the morning prayer. And Shu'bah, his freedman, narrated from him that he used to command people to separate between the obligations and the voluntary prayers, and he counted himself, when he prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr in part of the mosque then entered with the people in prayer, as separating between them; so likewise we say."
"2073","2201 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Umar al-Darir, he said: narrated to us Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim, he said: Mutarrif ibn Tarif informed us, from Abu Uthman al-Ansari, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abbas came while the imam was in the morning prayer, and he had not prayed the two rak'ahs, so Abd Allah ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, prayed the two rak'ahs behind the imam, then entered with them. And the like of that has been narrated from Ibn Umar."
"2074","2202 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd, they said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Salih, he said: narrated to me al-Layth, he said: narrated to me Ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, he said: Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, left his house, then the morning prayer was established, so he bowed two rak'ahs before entering the mosque while he was on the road, then entered the mosque and prayed the morning prayer with the people. So this, even if he did not pray them in the mosque, yet he prayed them after knowing of the establishment of prayer in the mosque, so that is contrary to the saying of Abu Hurayrah: When the prayer is established there is no prayer except the prescribed one, if its meaning is what the people of the first saying diverted it to."
"2075","2203 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us Abu Nu'aym, he said: narrated to us Malik ibn Mighwal, he said: I heard Nafi' saying: I woke Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, for the Fajr prayer while the prayer had been established, so he stood and prayed the two rak'ahs."
"2076","2204 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Shaybah, he said: narrated to us al-Hasan ibn Musa, he said: narrated to us Shayban ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he came while the imam prayed the morning prayer, and he had not prayed the two rak'ahs before the morning prayer, so he prayed them in the chamber of Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, then he prayed with the imam. So in this hadith, from Ibn Umar, that he prayed them in the mosque, because the chamber of Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, is of the mosque; thus that agreed with what we mentioned from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2077","2205 - Narrated to us Abu Bishr al-Raqqi, he said: narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah, from Mis'ar, from Ubayd ibn al-Hasan, from Abu Ubayd Allah, from Abu al-Darda that he used to enter the mosque while the people were in rows in the Fajr prayer, so he prayed the two rak'ahs in a corner of the mosque, then entered with the people in prayer."
"2078","2206 - Narrated to us Abu Bishr al-Raqqi, he said: narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah, from Abu Malik al-Ashja'i, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abd Allah, meaning Ibn Mas'ud, that he used to do that."
"2079","2207 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Dawud, he said: narrated to us Hisham ibn Abi Abd Allah, from Ja'far, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: We used to come to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them both, before praying the two rak'ahs before dawn while he was in prayer, so we prayed the two rak'ahs at the back of the mosque, then entered with the people in their prayer."
"2080","2208 - Narrated to us Rawh ibn al-Faraj, he said: narrated to us Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, he said: narrated to us Asim, from Abu Uthman, he said: We used to come while Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was in the morning prayer, so we bowed the two rak'ahs, then entered with him in prayer."
"2081","2209 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Dawud, he said: narrated to us Sa'id, from Husayn, he said: I heard al-Sha'bi saying: Masruq used to come to the people while they were in prayer, and he had not bowed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, so he prayed the two rak'ahs in the mosque, then entered with the people in their prayer."
"2082","2210 - Narrated to us Abu Bishr al-Raqqi, he said: narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah, from Asim al-Ahwal, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq that he did that, except that he said: in a corner of the mosque."
"2083","2211 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal, he said: narrated to us Yazid ibn Ibrahim, from al-Hasan that he used to say: If you enter the mosque and you have not prayed the two rak'ahs of Fajr, pray them, even if the imam is praying, then enter with the imam."
"2084","2212 - Narrated to us Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Mansur, he said: narrated to us Hushaym, he said: Yunus informed us, he said: al-Hasan used to say: He prays them in a corner of the mosque, then enters with the people in their prayer."
"2085","2213 - Narrated to us Salih, he said: narrated to us Sa'id, he said: narrated to us Hushaym, he said: narrated to us Husayn and Ibn Awn, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq that he did that. So all these have permitted the two rak'ahs of Fajr to be bowed at the back of the mosque while the imam is in prayer. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for by way of reasoning, those who held that he enters into the obligation and leaves the two rak'ahs said: His occupying himself with the obligation is more entitled and better than his occupying himself with the voluntary. So of the proof against them in that was that they have agreed that had he been in his house and known of the imam's entering into the Fajr prayer, he should bow the two rak'ahs of Fajr as long as he does not fear missing the imam's prayer; and if he fears missing the imam's prayer he does not pray them, because he was only commanded to make them before the prayer. And they have not agreed that his occupying himself with hastening to the obligation is better than his occupying himself with them in his house, while they have been emphasized as no voluntary prayer has been emphasized, and it is narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was not more constant upon any voluntary prayer than upon them, and that he said: Do not leave them even if the horses chase you. So when they had been emphasized with emphasis, and such encouragement was given for them, and such prohibition of leaving them, and they were bowed in houses before the obligation, they should also, by reasoning, be bowed in mosques before the obligation, by analogy to what we mentioned of that. And it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them."
"2086","Chapter 52: Prayer in a single garment. 2214 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Rawh ibn Ubadah, he said: narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, he said: Nafi' informed me that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, clothed him while he was a boy, then he entered the mosque and found him praying wrapped, so he said: Do you not have two garments? He said: Yes. He said: What do you think, had I sought your help behind the house, would you have worn them both? He said: Yes. He said: Then is Allah more entitled to be adorned for, or the people? Nafi' said: Rather Allah. So he informed him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him; Nafi' said: I have become certain that it is from one of them, and I do not think it but from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Let none of you wrap himself in prayer as the Jews wrap; whoever has two garments, let him wear a waist-wrap and a cloak, and whoever does not have two garments, let him wear a waist-wrap then pray."
"2087","2215 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Hajabi, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, from Ayyub, from Nafi', and he mentioned with his chain the like of it exactly."
"2088","2216 - Narrated to us Yazid ibn Sinan, he said: narrated to us Shayban ibn Farrukh, he said: narrated to us Jarir ibn Hazim, from Nafi', he said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated to us, and I do not know whether he raised it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or narrated it from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him; Nafi' doubted. Then he mentioned the like of what Nafi' narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, of the speech of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or the speech of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in the first hadith."
"2089","2217 - Narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Wuhayb, he said: narrated to us my father, he said: I heard Nafi', he said: I heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this, and they disliked prayer in a single garment for one able to have two garments, and they disliked prayer for one able only upon a single garment wrapped in it covered; they said: But he should wear it as a waist-wrap. And they argued with this hadith and said: It is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), no doubt in it. And they mentioned concerning that what:"
"2090","2218 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Zuhayr ibn Abbad, he said: narrated to us Hafs ibn Maysarah, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays, let him wear his two garments, for Allah is most entitled to be adorned for; and if he does not have two garments, let him wear a waist-wrap when he prays, and let none of you wrap himself in his prayer as the Jews wrap."
"2091","2219 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh, he said: narrated to us Ubayy, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Tawbah al-Anbari, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: When one of you prays, let him wear a waist-wrap and a cloak. He said: So this Musa ibn Uqbah, and he is of the eminent early companions of Nafi', mentioned that from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not doubt, and Tawbah al-Anbari agreed with him upon that. It is said to them: But other than Nafi' narrated from Ibn Umar, and mentioned it from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2092","2220 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Salih, he said: narrated to me al-Layth, he said: narrated to me Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Salim ibn Abd Allah informed me that Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saw a man praying covered, so Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him when he gave salam: Let none of you pray covered, and do not resemble the Jews; and if one of you has but one garment, let him wear it as a waist-wrap. So this Salim, and he is more established than Nafi' and more retentive, only narrated that from Ibn Umar, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So this hadith became from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, not from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Malik narrated it from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, as his saying, and did not mention therein the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) nor Umar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2093","2221 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah, he said: narrated to us Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr, he said: narrated to us Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he clothed Nafi' with two garments, then he stood praying in a single garment, so he blamed that upon him and said: Beware of that, for Allah is more entitled to be beautified for. And others opposed in that and said: There is no harm in prayer in a single garment. And they argued in that with what:"
"2094","2222 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus, he said: narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah, from Asim, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, is prayer performed in a single garment? So he said: Do all of you find two garments?"
"2095","2223 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Wahb. (Ha). 2224 - And narrated to us Ali ibn Ma'bad, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Bakr, they said: narrated to us Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2096","2225 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Rawh ibn Ubadah, he said: narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, Malik, and Muhammad ibn Abi Hafsah, they said: Ibn Shihab informed us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By my life, I leave my clothes on the clothes-rack and pray in a single garment."
"2097","2226 - Narrated to us Yunus, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, and he did not mention the saying of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2098","2227 - Narrated to us Husayn ibn Nasr, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2099","2228 - Narrated to us Husayn ibn Nasr, he said: narrated to us Yusuf ibn Adi, he said: narrated to us Mulazim ibn Amr, from Ubayd Allah ibn Badr, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2100","2229 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abu Salamah Musa ibn Isma'il, he said: narrated to us Aban ibn Yazid, he said: narrated to us Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Isa ibn Khuthaym, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, that he witnessed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while a man asked him about a man praying in a single garment, and he said nothing to him; then when the prayer was established, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) joined between his two garments and prayed in them."
"2101","2230 - Narrated to us Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin, he said: narrated to us Asad, he said: narrated to us Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Maqburi, from al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim, he said: We entered upon Jabir ibn Abd Allah while he prayed in a single garment, while his shirt and cloak were on the clothes-rack; then when he finished he said: By Allah, I did this only on your account; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about prayer in a single garment, so he said: Yes, and when does one of you have two garments?"
"2102","2231 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Rawh, he said: narrated to us Zam'ah ibn Salih, he said: I heard Ibn Shihab narrating from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of what Jabir mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So this Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, has narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the permission of prayer in a single garment."
"2103","Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Dawud, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed us, from his father, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a single garment in the house of Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"2104","2232 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam and Abd Allah ibn Salih, they said: narrated to us al-Layth, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a single garment, covered with it."
"2105","2233 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Ibn Abi Qutaylah, he said: al-Darawardi informed us, from Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, from his father, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa', he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I pursue hunting, so may I pray in a single shirt? He said: Yes, and button it even with a thorn. So in these reports is the permission of prayer in a single garment, and that contradicts what prohibited prayer in a single garment, and indicates that there is no harm in it in the state of sufficiency and in the state of need. And that is because the questioner asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Does one of us pray in a single garment? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him with an unrestricted answer, saying: Do all of you find two garments? i.e. had prayer been disliked in a single garment, it would have been disliked for one who finds but a single garment. So in his answering that is what indicates that the ruling of prayer in a single garment for one who finds two garments is as it is in prayer in a single garment for one who finds no other. Then we wanted to look how one should do with the single garment in which he prays: does he wrap with it or wear it as a waist-wrap?"
"2106","2234 - So we looked into that, and when Ibn Marzuq had narrated to us, he said: narrated to us Abu Amir al-Aqadi, he said: narrated to us Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Murrah, freedman of Aqil ibn Abi Talib, from Umm Hani bint Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with them, in a long hadith, she said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Fatimah and she poured for him water for washing, so he performed ghusl, then prayed in a single garment, opposing between its two ends, some rak'ahs."
"2107","2235 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah, he said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari, from Muhammad ibn Amr, he said: narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Abd Allah ibn Hunayn, from Abu Murrah, and he mentioned with his chain concerning prayer the like of it, and he said: eight rak'ahs."
"2108","2236 - Narrated to us Yunus, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Musa ibn Maysarah and Abu al-Nadr, freedman of Umar ibn Ubayd Allah, that Abu Murrah informed them both that Umm Hani bint Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with her, informed him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2109","2237 - Narrated to us Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin, he said: narrated to us Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth, he said: narrated to us al-Layth, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Sa'id ibn Abi Hind, that Abu Hurayrah narrated to him, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2110","2238 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Muhriz, he said: narrated to us Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, he said: narrated to us my father, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Salamah ibn Kuhayl and Muhammad ibn al-Walid narrated to me, from Kurayb, freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a Hadrami cloak of his, wrapped with it, with nothing else upon him."
"2111","2239 - Narrated to us Rabi' al-Jizi, he said: narrated to us Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus, he said: narrated to us Ya'la ibn al-Harith al-Muharibi, he said: I heard Ghaylan ibn Jami' narrating from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from a son of Ammar ibn Yasir, he said: My father said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in a single garment, wrapped with it."
"2112","2240 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Yahya ibn Hammad, he said: narrated to us Abu Awanah, from Sulayman, he said: narrated to us Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, he said: Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me that he entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and saw him praying in a single garment, wrapped with it."
"2113","2241 - Narrated to us Ibrahim ibn Munqidh, he said: Idris ibn Yahya narrated to me, from Bakr ibn Mudar, from Amr ibn al-Harith, that Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki informed him that he entered upon Jabir ibn Abd Allah while he prayed covered with his garment, while his clothes were near him; then he turned to us and said: I did this only so that you see, and I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing that."
"2114","2242 - Narrated to us Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibn Marzuq, they said: narrated to us Abu Asim, from Ibn Jurayj, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays in a single garment, let him wrap it around himself."
"2115","2243 - Narrated to us Yunus, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith and Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a single garment, opposing between its two ends upon his shoulders, while his garment was on the clothes-rack."
"2116","2244 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam, he said: narrated to us Abu Ghassan, from Asim ibn Abd Allah that he entered upon Jabir ibn Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with them both, then when the prayer came, he stood and prayed wrapped with a waist-wrap, while his clothes were on the clothes-rack; then when he prayed he turned to us and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray thus."
"2117","2245 - Narrated to us Yunus, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a single garment, in the house of Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, placing its two ends upon his shoulders."
"2118","2246 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Salih, he said: narrated to me al-Layth, he said: narrated to us Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a single garment, covered with it, opposing between its two ends upon his shoulders."
"2119","2247 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Sulayman ibn Harb, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah. (Ha). 2248 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah, he said: narrated to us Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Habib ibn al-Shahid, from al-Hasan, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out leaning upon Usamah, may Allah be pleased with him, wrapped with a cloak, and he prayed with them."
"2120","2249 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Musaddad, he said: narrated to us Isma'il ibn Ibrahim, Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal, and Yahya ibn Sa'id, they said: Hisham informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Ikrimah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays in a single garment, let him oppose between its two ends."
"2121","2250 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Rawh ibn Ubadah, he said: narrated to us Hisham ibn Hassan and Shu'bah, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Umar ibn Abi Salamah, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying in a single garment, opposing between its two ends. Thus these reports have successively come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prayer in a single garment, wrapped with it, in the state of finding other than it. And we have mentioned in some of these hadiths that he prayed while his clothes were on the clothes-rack, in a single garment, wrapped with it. So it is possible that that is upon wide garments specifically, not upon narrow ones, and it is possible that it is upon all garments, narrow and wide."
"2122","So we looked into that, and when Abu Zur'ah Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Dimashqi had narrated to us, he said: narrated to us Abu Nu'aym, he said: narrated to us Fitr ibn Khalifah, from Shurahbil ibn Sa'd, he said: Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: When the garment is wide, wrap it upon your shoulder, and when it is narrow, wear it as a waist-wrap then pray. So it is established by this hadith that wrapping is what is intended, and it is what should be done in the garments in which one prays; and if one cannot do it due to narrowness of the garment, he wears it as a waist-wrap. And we needed to look into the ruling of the wide garment, which one can wear as a waist-wrap and wrap with: does he wrap with it or wear it as a waist-wrap? And how does he do?"
"2123","2251 - So when Yunus had narrated to us, he said: narrated to us Sufyan, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: Let none of you pray in a single garment with nothing of it upon his shoulders."
"2124","2252 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us Abu Nu'aym. (Ha). 2253 - And narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Mu'ammal, they said: narrated to us Sufyan, from Abu al-Zinad, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2125","2254 - Narrated to us Ibn Munqidh, he said: Idris ibn Yahya narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Ayyash, from Ibn Hurmuz, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) he said: When one of you prays in a single garment, let him place upon his shoulders something of it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade in the hadith of Abu al-Zinad prayer in a single garment worn only as a waist-wrap. And there has come from him also that he forbade a man to pray in trousers alone, with nothing else upon him."
"2126","2255 - Narrated to us Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Wahb, he said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab informed me, from Abu al-Munayb, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with that. So this is like that, and this, according to us, is upon having other than it with him; but if he finds no other, there is no harm in prayer in it, just as there is no harm in the small garment worn as a waist-wrap. This is the verification of the meanings of these reports narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter. And there have been narrated from his Companions concerning that reports, among them what:"
"2127","2256 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Musaddad, he said: narrated to us Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal, he said: narrated to us Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Abu Hazim, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, that men of the Muslims used to attend prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), tying their garments upon their necks, with nothing upon any of them except a single garment."
"2128","2257 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Khattab ibn Uthman, he said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Himyar, he said: narrated to us Thabit ibn al-Ajlan, he said: Abu Amir Sulaym al-Ansari narrated to us that he prayed with Abu Bakr in his caliphate seven months, and he saw most of those who prayed with him of men in a single garment called a cloak, with nothing else upon them."
"2129","2258 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il, he said: narrated to us Sufyan, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: Khalid ibn al-Walid led us on the day of Yarmuk in a single garment, having opposed between its two ends."
"2130","2259 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abu al-Walid, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: Khalid ibn al-Walid led us on the day of Yarmuk in a single garment, having opposed between its two ends, and behind him were the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So in what we have narrated from those we mentioned of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of prayer in a single garment is what contradicts what we narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. Then there has been established from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the preceding reports what has agreed with that, so that is more entitled to be taken than what was narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. And what we have explained is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them."
"2131","Chapter 53: Prayer in camel resting-places. 2260 - Narrated to us Yazid ibn Sinan, Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman, and Bakr ibn Idris, they said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub Abu al-Abbas al-Misri narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Jubayrah, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer in seven places: in the rubbish heap, the slaughterhouse, the graveyard, the middle of the road, the bathhouse, camel resting-places, and atop the House of Allah."
"2132","2261 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us al-Khadir ibn Muhammad al-Harrani, he said: narrated to us Abbad ibn al-Awwam, he said: al-Hajjaj informed us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah, freedman of Banu Hashim, narrated to us, and he was trustworthy, and al-Hakam used to take from him, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Usayd ibn Hudayr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray in sheepfolds, and do not pray in camel resting-places."
"2133","2262 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah, he said: narrated to us Yusuf ibn Adi, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Idris, from al-A'mash, from Abd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Bara ibn Azib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A man said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): May I pray in sheepfolds? He said: Yes. He said: May I perform ablution from their meat? He said: No. He said: May I pray in camel resting-places? He said: No. He said: May I perform ablution from their meat? He said: Yes."
"2134","2263 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Ma'bad, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Bakr. (Ha). 2264 - And narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah, he said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Ansari, they said: narrated to us Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you find nothing but sheepfolds and camel resting-places, then pray in sheepfolds, and do not pray in camel resting-places."
"2135","2265 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah, he said: narrated to us Hajjaj, he said: narrated to us Hammad, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ja'far ibn Abi Thawr, from Jabir ibn Abi Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, may I pray in sheep resting-places? He said: Yes. He said: May I pray in camel resting-places? He said: No."
"2136","2266 - Narrated to us Muhammad, he said: narrated to us Hajjaj, he said: narrated to us Abu Awanah, from Uthman ibn Abd Allah ibn Mawhab, from Ja'far ibn Abi Thawr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"2137","2267 - Narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Abu Asim, from Mubarak, from al-Hasan, from Abd Allah ibn Mughaffal, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pray in sheepfolds, and do not pray in camel resting-places. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that prayer in camel resting-places is disliked, and they argued with these reports, until some of them were severe in its ruling and invalidated the prayer. And others opposed them in that and permitted prayer in that place. And of the proof for them was that these reports which forbade prayer in camel resting-places, people have spoken concerning their meaning and the cause for which the prohibition was. So a group said: Camel owners are of their habit to defecate near their camels and urinate, so they thereby render camel resting-places impure, so prayer in camel resting-places was forbidden for that, not for the camels' own cause; rather it is for the cause of impurity which prevents prayer in any place whatever it is. And sheep owners are of their habit to clean their sheep's places and leave urinating therein and defecating, so prayer in their folds was permitted for that. Thus was narrated from Sharik ibn Abd Allah that he used to interpret this hadith upon this meaning. And Yahya ibn Adam said: It is not from this cause in my view that the prohibition came, but because camels are feared to leap so one meeting them is thereby harmed; do you not see he said: For they are jinn of jinn created. And in the hadith of Rafi' ibn Khadij from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: These camels have wild runaways like the wild beasts' runaways. And this is not feared from sheep, so he commanded avoiding prayer in camel resting-places fearing that from their deed, not because they have an impurity which sheep do not have like it, and prayer in sheepfolds was permitted because what is feared from camels is not feared from them. 2268 - Khallad ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Ibn Shuja' al-Thalji, from Yahya ibn Adam, with both interpretations together."
"2138","2269 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Salih, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to me that Iyad said: Prayer in camel resting-places was only forbidden because a man screens himself with them to relieve himself; so this interpretation agrees with the interpretation of Sharik."
"2139","2270 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Sa'id and Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, they said: narrated to us Abu Khalid al-Ahmar, from Ubayd Allah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray toward his camel."
"2140","2271 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Sa'id, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr al-Abdi informed us, he said: Isra'il informed us, from Ziyad al-Musaffir, from al-Hasan, from al-Miqdam al-Rahawi, he said: Ubadah ibn al-Samit, Abu al-Darda, and al-Harith ibn Mu'awiyah sat. So Abu al-Darda said: Which of you memorizes the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prayed with us toward a camel of the spoils? So Ubadah said: I. He said: Then narrate. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed with us toward a camel of the spoils, then he stretched his hand and took a tick from the camel and said: Nothing is lawful for me of your spoils like this except the fifth, and it is returned among you. So in these two hadiths is the permission of prayer toward a camel; thus it is established that prayer toward a camel is permissible, and that he did not forbid prayer in camel resting-places because prayer in their direction is not permissible. And it is possible that the dislike is for the cause of what is of camels in their resting-places of their dung and urine. So we looked into that and saw sheepfolds, all have agreed upon the permissibility of prayer therein, and with that came the narrations which we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And the ruling of what is of camels in their resting-places of their urine and other than that was the ruling of what is of sheep in their folds of their urine and other than that, no difference between any of that in impurity or purity, because whoever made sheep urine pure made camel urine likewise, and whoever made camel urine impure made sheep urine likewise. So when prayer had been permitted in sheepfolds in the hadith in which prayer in camel resting-places was forbidden, it is established that the prohibition of that is not for the cause of the impurity of what is of them, since what is of sheep its ruling is like that. But the cause for which the prohibition was is what Sharik said, or what Yahya ibn Adam said. So if it is for what Sharik said, then prayer is disliked where there is feces and urine, whether a resting-place or other. And if it is for what Yahya ibn Adam said, then prayer is disliked where souls are feared for, whether a resting-place or other. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. As for its ruling by way of reasoning, we saw them not differing in sheepfolds, and that prayer therein is permissible, and they only differed in camel resting-places. And we saw the ruling of camel flesh like the ruling of sheep flesh in its purity, and we saw the ruling of their urine like the ruling of their urine in its purity or impurity. So it would come in reasoning also that the ruling of prayer in the place of camels be as it is in the place of sheep, by analogy to what we mentioned. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them."
"2141","2272 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam, he said: narrated to us al-Layth ibn Sa'd, he said: This is a copy of the letter of Abd Allah ibn Nafi' to al-Layth ibn Sa'd, in which he mentions: As for what you mentioned of camel resting-places, it has reached us that that is disliked, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray upon his mount, and Ibn Umar and those we met of the best of our land's people, one of them would place his she-camel between him and the qiblah, and pray toward it while it dropped dung and urinated."
"2142","Chapter 54: The imam misses the Eid prayer, does he pray it on the morrow or not? 2273 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn Salih, he said: narrated to us Hushaym ibn Bashir, from Abu Bishr Ja'far ibn Iyas, from Abu Umayr ibn Anas ibn Malik, he said: My paternal uncles of the Ansar informed me that the crescent was hidden from the people on the last night of Ramadan in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so they woke fasting, then they testified before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the sun's decline that they had seen the crescent the past night. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the people to break fast, so they broke fast that very hour, and he went out with them on the morrow and prayed with them the Eid prayer. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to this and said: When the people miss the Eid prayer at the beginning of the Eid day, they pray it on the morrow of that day, at the time they pray it. And of those who went to that was Abu Yusuf. And others opposed them in that and said: When the prayer is missed on the Eid day until the sun of its day declines, it is not prayed after that in that day nor in what is after it. And of those who said that was Abu Hanifah, may Allah Almighty have mercy on him. And of the proof for them in that was that the memorizers who narrated this hadith from Hushaym do not mention therein that he prayed with them on the morrow. So of those who narrated that from Hushaym without mentioning this therein: Yahya ibn Hassan, and Sa'id ibn Mansur, and he is the most precise of people for the wordings of Hushaym, and he is the one who distinguished for people what Hushaym used to conceal of other than it."
"2143","2274 - Narrated to us Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Mansur, he said: narrated to us Hushaym, he said: Abu Bishr informed us, from Abu Umayr ibn Anas, he said: My paternal uncles of the Ansar of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me, they said: The crescent of Shawwal was obscured to us, so we woke fasting; then a party of riders came at the end of the day and testified before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they had seen the crescent yesterday. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to break fast from their day, then to go out for their Eid on the morrow to their prayer-place."
"2144","2275 - Narrated to us Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb, he said: narrated to us Yahya ibn Hassan, he said: narrated to us Hushaym, from Abu Bishr, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. So this is the origin of this hadith, not as Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated it; and his commanding them to go out on the morrow for their Eid may mean he intended thereby that they gather therein to supplicate, or for their multitude to be seen, so that reaches their enemy and their affair becomes great to him, not that they pray as is prayed for Eid. And we have seen one who prays on the Eid day had been commanded the attendance of one who does not pray."
"2145","2276 - Narrated to us Salih, he said: narrated to us Sa'id, he said: Hushaym informed us, he said: Mansur informed us, from Ibn Sirin, from Umm Atiyyah, and Hisham, from Hafsah, from Umm Atiyyah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bring out the menstruating women and cloistered girls on the Eid day; as for the menstruating women, they keep apart and witness the good and the supplication of the Muslims. And Hushaym said: So a woman said: O Messenger of Allah, what if one of us has no outer garment? He said: Let her sister lend her her outer garment. So when the menstruating women go out not for prayer but to be touched by the supplication of the Muslims, it is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the people to go out on the morrow of Eid to gather and supplicate, so their supplication touches them, not for prayer. And Shu'bah narrated this hadith from Abu Bishr as Sa'id and Yahya narrated it, not as Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated it."
"2146","2277 - Narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Wahb, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Abu Bishr, he said: I heard Abu Umayr ibn Anas, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2147","2278 - And narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Abu al-Walid, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Abu Bishr, and he mentioned the like of it with his chain, except that he said: And he commanded them when they wake to go out to their prayer-place. So the meaning of that is also the meaning of what Yahya and Sa'id narrated from Hushaym, and this is the origin of the hadith. And when there was not in the hadith what indicates the ruling of what they differed in of prayer on the morrow, we looked into that and saw prayers are of two kinds. Of them is what the whole of time is a time for it, other than the times in which the obligation is not prayed; so what is missed of it in its time, the whole of time is a time for it to be made up therein, other than what its making up therein of times is forbidden. And of them is what has been made for it a specific time, and it has not been made for anyone to pray it in other than that time. Of that is Friday: its ruling is to pray on Friday from when the sun declines until the time of Asr enters; so when that time exits it is missed and it is not permissible to pray after that in that day of it nor in what is after it. So what is not made up in the remainder of its day after its time's missing is not made up after that. And what is made up after its time's missing in the remainder of that day of it is made up on the morrow and after that; and all this is agreed upon. And the Eid prayer had been made for it a specific time on the Eid day, its end the sun's decline, and all have agreed that when it is not prayed that day until the sun declines, it is not prayed in the remainder of its day. So when it is established that the Eid prayer is not made up after its time's exit in that day of it, it is established that it is not made up after that on a morrow nor other, because we saw what is permissible for one who missed it to make up on the morrow of its day is permissible for him to make up in the remainder of the day in which its time is, and what is not his to make up in the remainder of that day of it, it is not his to make up on its morrow. So the Eid prayer is likewise: when it is established that it is not made up when missed in the remainder of its day, it is established that it is not made up on its morrow. This is the reasoning in this chapter, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, may Allah Almighty have mercy on him, in what was narrated from some people, and we did not find it in the narration of Abu Yusuf from him; thus it was in the narration of Ahmad, may Allah have mercy on them both."
"2148","Chapter 55: Prayer in the Ka'bah. 2279 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah the judge, he said: narrated to us Abu Asim al-Nabil, he said: narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, he said: I said to Ata: Did you hear Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: We were only commanded with circumambulation, and we were not commanded to enter it, meaning the House? So he said: He did not forbid entering it, but I heard him saying: Usamah ibn Zayd informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he entered the House, supplicated in all its corners, and did not pray therein anything until he exited; then when he exited he prayed two rak'ahs and said: This is the qiblah."
"2149","2280 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Asim, he said: narrated to us Ibn Jurayj, he said: Amr ibn Dinar informed me, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that al-Fadl ibn Abbas informed him that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the House and did not pray, but when he exited he prayed two rak'ahs at the door of the House."
"2150","2281 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Zayd al-Fara'idi, he said: Musa ibn Dawud informed us, he said: narrated to us Hammam, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the Ka'bah, and therein were six pillars, so he stood at every pillar thus and did not pray. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to that prayer in the Ka'bah is not permissible, and they argued in that with these reports, and with the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he prayed outside the Ka'bah: This is the qiblah. And others opposed them in that and said: There is no harm in prayer in the Ka'bah. And they said: The saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) 'This is the qiblah' may bear what we mentioned, and may mean he intended thereby: this is the qiblah toward which your imam whom you follow prays, and at it is his standing place; so he intended thereby teaching them what Allah Almighty commanded of His saying: And take of the standing place of Ibrahim a place of prayer. And there is not in the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaving prayer therein evidence that prayer therein is not permissible. And there have been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) successive reports that he prayed therein."
"2151","2282 - Of that is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Nafi', from Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the Ka'bah, he and Usamah ibn Zayd, Bilal, and Uthman ibn Talhah al-Hajabi, and he closed it upon them, and remained therein. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: So I asked Bilal when he came out: What did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do? He said: He placed a pillar on his left and two pillars on his right, and three pillars behind him, and the House that day was upon six pillars; then he prayed and placed between him and the wall about three cubits."
"2152","2283 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Zayd, he said: narrated to us Musa ibn Dawud, he said: narrated to us al-Layth ibn Sa'd, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, and that he prayed between the two Yemeni pillars, except that he did not mention how he placed the pillars which Malik mentioned in his hadith."
"2153","2284 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Uzayz al-Ayli, he said: narrated to us Salamah ibn Rawh, from Uqayl, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, he said: Salim informed me that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed him, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2154","2285 - Narrated to us Yazid ibn Sinan, he said: narrated to us Duhaym ibn al-Yatim, he said: narrated to us Umar ibn Abd al-Wahid, from al-Awza'i, he said: Nafi' narrated to me from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it, except that he said: He informed me that he prayed facing it when he entered between the two pillars on his right."
"2155","2286 - Narrated to us Yazid ibn Sinan, he said: narrated to us Musa ibn Isma'il, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered on the day of the conquest of Makkah, with his riding companion Usamah ibn Zayd, and he made his camel kneel in the shade of the Ka'bah. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: So I outran the people, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Bilal, and Usamah had entered the House; so I said to Bilal from behind the door: Where did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray? He said: He prayed facing you between the two pillars."
"2156","2287 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Zayd, he said: narrated to us Musa ibn Dawud, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Zayd, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, from Bilal, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the Ka'bah."
"2157","2288 - Narrated to us Husayn ibn Nasr, he said: narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam, he said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far informed me, he said: al-Ala ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, he said: I was with my father, then we met Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, so my father asked him, while I heard: Where did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray when he entered the House? So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered between Usamah ibn Zayd and Bilal; then when he came out we asked them both: Where did he pray, meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So they said: Toward his direction."
"2158","2289 - Narrated to us Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah, he said: narrated to us Ahmad ibn Ashkab, he said: narrated to us Abu Mu'awiyah, from al-A'mash, from Umarah, from Abu al-Sha'tha, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw him enter the House, until when he was between the two pillars, he went until he clung to the wall, then stood praying; so I came and stood at his side, and he prayed four; so I said: Inform me where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed of the House? So he said: Here Usamah informed me that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray. So this Usamah ibn Zayd, Abd Allah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, has narrated from him that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray in the House. So he and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, have differed in what they narrated from Usamah of that, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, also narrated from Bilal the like of what was narrated from Usamah. So when the narrations from Usamah contradicted and were equal, they should be set aside and what was narrated from Bilal be established, since there was no disagreement from him in that. And it has been narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, unrestrictedly, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the Ka'bah."
"2159","2290 - Narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Wahb, he being Ibn Jarir, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah, from Simak al-Hanafi, he said: I heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the House, and there will come to you one who forbids you; so he heard his saying, meaning Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2160","2291 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us Abu Nu'aym, he said: narrated to us Mis'ar, from Simak al-Hanafi, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: Do not make anything of the House behind you, and follow it all. And I heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed therein. And there has been narrated from other than Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in that that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of what Ibn Umar narrated from Usamah and Bilal."
"2161","2292 - Of that is what Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: narrated to us Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr al-Humaydi, he said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Fudayl ibn Ghazwan, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Abu Safwan or Abd Allah ibn Safwan, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of the conquest, having come, so I gathered my clothes upon me, and I found he had exited the House. So I said: Where did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray in the House? So they said: Facing you, i.e. in front of you. I said: How many did he pray? They said: Two rak'ahs."
"2162","2293 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Shaybah, he said: narrated to us Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali, he said: Jarir informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Safwan, he said: I said to Umar: How did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do when he entered the Ka'bah? So he said: He prayed two rak'ahs."
"2163","2294 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abu al-Walid, he said: narrated to us Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: Abd Allah ibn Safwan. So this Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, has been related from him therein what agrees with what Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, related from Usamah and Bilal, of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayer in the House. And there has been narrated from Jabir ibn Abd Allah the like of that."
"2164","2295 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, he said: narrated to us Shabbabah, from Mughirah ibn Muslim, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the House on the day of the conquest and prayed therein two rak'ahs. And there has also been narrated from Shaybah ibn Uthman and Uthman ibn Talhah the like of that."
"2165","2296 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn al-Subh, he said: narrated to us Abu Isma'il al-Mu'addib, from Abd Allah ibn Muslim ibn Hurmuz, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Zajjaj, he said: I came to Shaybah ibn Uthman and said: O Abu Uthman, Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the Ka'bah and did not pray. He said: Rather he prayed two rak'ahs at the two front pillars, then placed his back against them."
"2166","2297 - Narrated to us Fahd, he said: narrated to us Muhammad ibn Sa'id, he said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Muslim, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2167","2298 - Narrated to us Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: narrated to us Affan, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, he said: Hisham ibn Urwah informed us, from Urwah, from Uthman ibn Talhah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the House and prayed therein two rak'ahs facing you between the two pillars. Abu Ja'far said: So if this chapter is taken by way of verifying the succession of reports, then the reports have succeeded that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the Ka'bah, as they have not succeeded with the like of it that he did not pray. And if it is taken by casting what is added of them from whom that is added and acting with what is besides that, then Usamah ibn Zayd, from whom Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, related that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he entered the Ka'bah, exited from it and did not pray: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, has narrated from him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he entered it, prayed therein; so that contradicted from him, and they negated each other. Then there has been narrated from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, Bilal, Jabir, Shaybah ibn Uthman, and Uthman ibn Talhah what agrees with what Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated from Usamah; so that is more entitled than what Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, was alone with from Usamah. Then there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying what indicates the permissibility of prayer therein."
"2168","2299 - Narrated to us Yunus, he said: narrated to us Sufyan, from Mansur ibn Safiyyah, from Safiyyah bint Shaybah Umm Mansur, she said: A woman of Banu Sulaym who delivered most of our household's people informed me, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to Uthman ibn Talhah and said: I had seen the two ram's horns when I entered the House, then I forgot to command you to cover them, for there should not be in the House anything that distracts one praying."
"2169","2300 - And there has been narrated from him also concerning that what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: narrated to us Ibn Abi Maryam, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, he said: Alqamah ibn Abi Alqamah narrated to us from his mother, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I loved to enter the House and pray therein, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took my hand and admitted me into the Hijr and said: Your people, when they built the Ka'bah, fell short in its building, so they excluded the Hijr from the House; so when you want to pray in the House, pray in the Hijr, for it is only a piece of it. So this Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has permitted prayer in the Hijr which is of the House. So it is established by what we mentioned, the verification of the saying of whoever went to permitting prayer in the House. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of verifying the meanings of the reports. As for its ruling by way of reasoning, those who forbid prayer therein only forbade that because the House all of it according to them is a qiblah; they said: So whoever prays therein has turned his back to part of it, so he is as one turning his back to part of the qiblah, so his prayer does not suffice him. So of the proof against them in that was that we saw one who turns his back to the qiblah, and turns it to his right or his left, that all that is equal, and that his prayer does not suffice him. And one who prayed facing a direction of the directions of the House, his prayer sufficed him by their agreement, and he is not in that facing all the directions of the House, because what is on the right of what he faced of the House and what is on its left, he is not facing it. And just as he was not made to worship by facing all the directions of the House in his prayer, but was only made to worship by facing a direction of its directions, so his leaving facing what remains of its directions after it does not harm him. So by analogy, one who prayed therein has faced one of its directions and turned his back to other than it. So what he turned his back to of that is in the ruling of what was on the right of what he faced of the House's directions and on its left when he was outside it. So thereby is also established the saying of those who permitted prayer in the House, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them. And that has also been narrated from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr."
"2170","2301 - Narrated to us Ibn Abi Dawud, he said: narrated to us Abu Umar al-Hawdi, he said: narrated to us Yazid ibn Ibrahim, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: I saw Ibn al-Zubayr praying in the Hijr."
"2171","Chapter 56: One who prays behind the row alone. 2302 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Dawud, he said: narrated to us Shu'bah. (Ha). 2303 - And narrated to us Ali ibn Shaybah, he said: narrated to us Yazid ibn Harun, he said: Shu'bah informed us, from Amr ibn Murrah, he said: I heard Hilal ibn Yasaf narrating from Amr ibn Rashid, from Wabisah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a man praying behind the row alone, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to repeat the prayer."
"2172","2304 - Narrated to us Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: narrated to us Sa'id ibn Mansur, he said: narrated to us Hushaym, from Husayn, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, he said: Ziyad ibn Abi al-Ja'dah took my hand and stood me before Wabisah ibn Ma'bad at al-Raqqah, and he said: He narrated to me that a man prayed behind the row alone, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him to repeat the prayer."
"2173","2305 - Narrated to us Ibn Marzuq, he said: narrated to us Habban ibn Hilal, he said: narrated to us Mulazim ibn Amr, he said: Abd Allah ibn Badr al-Suhaymi narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ali ibn Shayban al-Suhaymi, from his father, and he was one of the delegation, he said: I prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he finished his prayer while a lone man prayed behind the row; so the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood until he finished his prayer, then said: Face your prayer anew, for there is no prayer for a lone one behind the row. So a group went to that whoever prays behind a row alone, his prayer is void, and they argued in that with these reports. And others opposed them in that and said: Whoever does that has done wrong, and his prayer suffices him. And they said: There is not in these reports what indicates contrary to what we said. And that is because you narrated that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the one who prayed behind the row to repeat the prayer; so it is possible that he commanded him that because he prayed behind the row. And it is possible that he commanded him that for another meaning, as he commanded the one who entered the mosque and prayed to repeat the prayer, then commanded him to repeat it until he did that several times in the hadith of Rifa'ah and Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with them both. So that was not because he entered the mosque and prayed, but for another meaning other than that, which is his leaving hitting the obligations of prayer. So it is also possible what you narrated of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding the man who prayed behind the row to repeat the prayer, not because he prayed behind the row, but for another meaning of him in prayer. And in the hadith of Ali ibn Shayban is an added meaning over the meaning in the hadith of Wabisah, which is that he said: We prayed behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he finished his prayer, while a lone man prayed behind the row; so the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood over him until he finished his prayer, then said: Face anew, for there is no prayer for a lone one behind the row. Abu Ja'far said: So in this hadith is that he commanded him to repeat the prayer and said: There is no prayer for a lone one behind the row. So it is possible that his commanding him to repeat the prayer was for the meaning we described in the meaning of the hadith of Wabisah. As for his saying: There is no prayer for a lone one behind the row, it is possible that is like his saying: There is no ablution for one who does not mention the name, and like the other hadith: There is no prayer for the neighbor of the mosque except in the mosque. And that is not upon that when he prays thus he is in the ruling of one who did not pray, but he has prayed a prayer that suffices him, except it is not complete in causes in the obligations and the sunnahs, because of the sunnah of prayer with the imam is joining the rows and filling the gaps; thus should one praying behind the imam do; so if he falls short of that he has done wrong and his prayer suffices him, except it is not the complete prayer in its obligations and its sunnahs, so it was said for that 'he has no prayer', i.e. he has no complete prayer, as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The poor one is not he whom the date and the two dates turn away, but the poor one is he who is not known so he is given charity, and does not ask people. So the meaning of his saying 'The poor one is not he whom the two dates turn away' is only: he is not the complete poor one in poverty, since he asks and is given what sustains him and covers his nakedness. But the poor one is he who does not ask people and they do not know him so they give him charity. So in this hadith he negated one who was poor, not complete in the causes of poverty, to be poor. So it is also possible that he only negated by his saying 'There is no prayer for one who prays behind the row alone' one who prays behind the row to be praying, because he is not complete in the causes of prayer, while he has prayed a prayer that suffices him. So if one says: Do you find from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this anything indicating what you said? It is said to him: Yes."
"2174","2306 - Narrated to us Abu Bakrah, he said: narrated to us Abu Umar al-Darir, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us that Ziyad al-A'lam informed them from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, he said: I came while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bowed, and breath had outrun me, so I bowed short of the row, then walked to the row. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the prayer, he said: Which of you is it who bowed short of the row? Abu Bakrah said: I. He said: May Allah increase you in eagerness, and do not repeat."
"2175","2307 - Narrated to us al-Husayn ibn al-Hakam al-Jabri, he said: narrated to us Affan ibn Muslim, he said: narrated to us Hammad ibn Salamah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2176","2308 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, from Ziyad al-A'lam, he said: al-Hasan narrated to us that Abu Bakrah bowed before the row; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: May Allah increase you in eagerness, and do not repeat. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is that he bowed before the row, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not command him to repeat the prayer. So had one who prays behind the row had his prayer not suffice him, then one who enters the prayer behind the row would not be entering it at all. Do you not see that one who prays upon a filthy place, his prayer is invalid? And one who opens the prayer upon a filthy place then moves to a clean place, his prayer is invalid. So everyone who opens the prayer in a spot in which it is not permitted for him to perform the prayer therein in its entirety would not be entering the prayer. So since Abu Bakrah's entering into the prayer before the row was a valid entering, the prayer of one who prays entirely before the row is a valid prayer. If one says: then what is the meaning of his saying (and do not repeat). It is said to him: that, in our view, bears two meanings: it may bear: and do not repeat bowing before the row until you stand in the row, as Abu Hurayrah narrated from him."
"2177","2309 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn 'Ali narrated to me, he said: Ibn 'Ajlan narrated to us from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you comes to the prayer, let him not bow before the row until he takes his place in the row. And his saying (and do not repeat) may bear: and do not repeat hastening to the prayer with a haste in which your breath urges you on, as has come from him in other than this hadith."
"2178","2310 - Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: my uncle 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from his father. (H). 2311 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the prayer is established, do not come to it hastening, and come to it walking, with tranquility upon you; so what you catch, pray, and what you miss, complete."
"2179","2312 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah. Then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Except that he said: (so make up)."
"2180","2313 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, from Abu Salamah, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2181","2314 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2182","2315 - Isma'il ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2183","2316 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Hisham, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2184","2317 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad. Then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2185","2318 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the call for the prayer is made, do not come to it hastening, and come to it with tranquility and dignity upon you; so what you catch, pray, and what you miss, complete."
"2186","2319 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from al-'Ala', from his father, and Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah, that they both heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it, and added: For indeed one of you is in prayer as long as he is heading toward the prayer."
"2187","2320 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil informed us, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: When one of you comes, meaning to the prayer, let him walk in his manner; let him pray what he catches, and let him make up what he was preceded by of it. Abu Ja'far said - and reflection, in our view - indicates that one who prays behind the row, his prayer is permissible. That is because they do not differ concerning a man praying behind the imam in a row, then the place of a man before him becomes empty, that he should walk to it until he stands in it, and likewise it was narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2188","2321 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Murrah al-Kinani narrated to us, he said: I heard Khaythamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman saying: I prayed beside Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he saw a gap in the row, so he kept nudging me to advance to it, while only the tightness of my place kept me from advancing, lest sitting I become far from him; so when he saw that, he advanced himself. And one who advances from a row to a row as we mentioned is, between the two rows, in no row, yet that did not harm him, nor did it remove him from the prayer. So had prayer been valid only by standing in a row, his prayer would have become invalid thereby when he came to be in no row, even if that were the least little. Just as one who stands upon an impure place while praying, the least little, that invalidates his prayer. So since they agreed that they order this man to advance to what is empty before him of the row, and his being between the two rows in no row does not invalidate his prayer, that indicates that one who prays before the row, his prayer suffices him. And it was narrated from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they bowed before the row, then walked to the row, and counted the bow in which they bowed before the row."
"2189","2322 - From that is what Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Isa narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Mansur, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: I entered the mosque, I and Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, and we caught the imam while bowing, so we bowed then walked until we stood level with the row. So when the imam finished the prayer, I stood to make up, so 'Abdullah said: You have caught the prayer."
"2190","2323 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Bashir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Sayyar Abu al-Hakam narrated to me, from Tariq, he said: We were sitting with Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, when his announcer came and said: The prayer has been established, so he stood and we stood, and he entered the mosque and saw the people bowing at the front of the mosque, so he said the takbir and bowed and walked, and we did the like of what he did. So if one objecting objects herein that 'Abdullah only did that because he and his companions became a row."
"2191","2324 - It was said to him: there has been narrated from Zayd ibn Thabit in this matter what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, he said: I saw Zayd ibn Thabit enter the mosque while the people were bowing, so he walked until when he was able to reach the row while bowing, he said the takbir and bowed, then crept while bowing until he reached the row."
"2192","2325 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Malik and Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab. Then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2193","2326 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, he said: my father informed me, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, that Zayd ibn Thabit used to bow upon the threshold of the mosque with his face toward the qiblah, then walk sideways upon his right side, then count it whether he reached the row or did not reach. So if one says: then you oppose what you narrated from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Zayd, and you say: it is not proper for anyone to bow before the row. It is said to him: yes, but we argued thereby against you, so that you know that all the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do not invalidate the prayer of one who entered the prayer before reaching the row. So if one says: then what is it you held, that you opposed 'Abdullah and Zayd thereby? It is said to him: what we narrated in this chapter from the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: let none of you bow before the row until he takes his place in the row, and al-Hasan said likewise."
"2194","2327 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from al-Ash'ath, from al-Hasan that he disliked bowing before the row. And all that we explained in this chapter of this, and of permitting the prayer of one who prayed behind the row, is the saying of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy upon them."
"2195","2328 - And from the proof against them for the people of the first saying was what 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from Khilas, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: Whoever catches a rak'ah of the morning prayer before the sun rises, let him pray another to it."
"2196","2329 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever catches a rak'ah of the 'Asr prayer before the sun sets, his prayer is complete, and when he catches a rak'ah of the dawn prayer, his prayer is complete. In what we narrated, he mentioned building after sunrise upon what he had entered before sunrise. And from the proof against the people of this saying was that this may have been from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before his forbidding prayer at sunrise. For he forbade that, and reports forbidding that from him were successive, and we mentioned those reports in the chapter of prayer times. So what contained the permission may be abrogated by what contains the prohibition. So they said: the prohibition is only of voluntary prayer specifically, not of making up missed obligations. Do you not see that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade prayer after dawn until the sun rises, and after 'Asr until the sun sets. Yet that was not - in our view and yours - preventing a missed prayer from being made up in these two times. So likewise what you narrated from him of forbidding prayer at sunrise is not preventing, in our view, one from making up a missed prayer then; it only prevents voluntary prayer specifically. And from the proof of the others against them was that there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that the missed obligatory prayers have entered into what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade at sunrise and at sunset."
"2197","2330 - And that is that 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, he said: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a campaign, or he said, on a detachment, then when it was the end of the last part of night we camped, and we did not wake until the heat of the sun woke us, and a man among us would leap up frightened, bewildered. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke and commanded us, so we departed from our route until the sun rose high, then we alighted and the people relieved themselves, then he commanded Bilal and he called the adhan, so we prayed two rak'ahs, then he called the iqamah and prayed the morning prayer. So we said: O Prophet of Allah, shall we not make it up at its time tomorrow? So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Does Allah forbid you usury and accept it from you."
"2198","2331 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn 'Ubayd informed us, from al-Hasan al-Basri, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he was on a journey, and slept past the dawn prayer until the sun rose, so he commanded and the adhan was called, then waited until the sun shone bright, then commanded and the iqamah was called, so he prayed the dawn prayer."
"2199","2332 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abbad ibn Maysarah al-Minqari narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Raja' al-'Utaridi, he said: 'Imran ibn Husayn narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) journeyed by night with us and we camped with him, and we did not wake except by the heat of the sun; so when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke, they said: O Messenger of Allah, our prayer is gone, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your prayer is not gone, move from this place; so he moved a short distance, then alighted and prayed."
"2200","2333 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, he said: 'Awf informed us, from Abu Raja', from 'Imran, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2201","2334 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, from Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Abi Qatadah al-Ansari, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) journeyed by night on a campaign among his campaigns, and we were with him, so some of the people said to him: if only you camped. So he said: I fear you will sleep past the prayer, so Bilal said: I will wake you. So the people alighted and lay down, and Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, leaned his back against his mount, and sleep was cast upon them; so the people woke with the edge of the sun risen. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where is what you said, O Bilal? He said: O Messenger of Allah, never has a sleep like it been cast upon me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah took your souls when He willed, and returned them to you when He willed, so call the people to prayer; so he called them and they performed ablution, then when the sun rose high, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed the two rak'ahs of dawn, then prayed the dawn prayer."
"2202","2335 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn informed us. Then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2203","2336 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Thabit, from 'Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Abu Qatadah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he mentioned the like of his hadith from Rawh which we mentioned at the beginning of this section, except that he did not mention their asking the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 'Abdullah said: So 'Imran ibn Husayn heard me while I was narrating this hadith in the congregational mosque, so he said: Who is the man? So I said: I am 'Abdullah ibn Rabah al-Ansari. So he said: The people know their hadith best, see how you narrate, for I am one of the seven that night. So when I finished he said: I did not think anyone memorized this hadith other than me."
"2204","2337 - He said, and Hammad narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated to us, from Bakr, from 'Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Abu Qatadah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2205","2338 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Nafi' ibn Jubayr, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was on a journey, so he said: Who will guard us tonight, not sleeping until morning. So Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I will; so he faced the rising of the sun, and their ears were struck, until the heat of the sun woke them; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood and performed ablution and they performed ablution, then sat a little while, then prayed the two rak'ahs of dawn, then prayed the dawn prayer."
"2206","2339 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Abu Mus'ab al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) camped one night on the road to Makkah, and neither he nor anyone of his Companions woke until the sun struck them; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke and said: This is a dwelling wherein is a devil. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led his beast on and his Companions led theirs on, until the forenoon rose high, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made his camel kneel, and his Companions made theirs kneel, then he led them and prayed the dawn prayer. So when we saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) delay the dawn prayer when the sun had risen, though it is an obligation, and he did not pray it then until the sun had risen high, and he had said in other than this hadith: whoever forgets a prayer or sleeps past it, let him pray it when he remembers it, that indicated that his forbidding prayer at sunrise includes the obligations and the voluntary prayers, and that the time in which he woke is not a time for the prayer he slept past. So if one says: then why did you hold by part of this hadith and leave part of it? So I said: one who prayed a rak'ah of 'Asr then the sun set for him, that he prays its remainder. It is said to him: we did not hold by part of this hadith, nor by anything of it; rather we deemed it all abrogated by what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his forbidding prayer at sunrise, and by what is indicated by what we mentioned from the hadith of Jubayr, and 'Imran, and Abu Qatadah, and Abu Hurayrah, that the obligation has entered into that, and that it is not prayed then, just as the voluntary is not prayed. As for prayer at sunset for the 'Asr of its day, we have mentioned the discussion thereof in the chapter of prayer times. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of establishing the meanings of the reports. As for its aspect by way of reflection, we saw the time of sunrise until it rises high is a time in which prayer has been forbidden. So we wanted to consider the ruling of the times in which things are forbidden: is it upon the voluntary thereof apart from the obligations? Or upon all that? So we saw the day of Fitr and the day of Sacrifice: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade fasting them, and the proof from him thereof stood, and that prohibition was, with all the scholars, that neither obligation nor voluntary be fasted therein. So reflection thereupon, concerning the time of sunrise in which prayer has been forbidden, is that it be likewise: neither obligation nor voluntary prayed therein, and likewise comes by reflection at sunset. As for the Prophet's forbidding prayer after 'Asr until the sun sets, and after dawn until the sun rises: these two times were not forbidden for prayer because of the time; rather prayer therein was forbidden because of the prayer, and we saw that time permits one who has not prayed to pray therein the obligation and the missed prayer. So since the prayer is the forbidding thing and it is an obligation, it only forbids what is unlike its kind of voluntary prayers, not the obligations. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy upon them. And al-Hakam and Hammad said likewise."
"2207","2340 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I asked al-Hakam and Hammad about a man who sleeps past the prayer and wakes with part of the sun risen? They said: Let him not pray until the sun spreads."
"2208","58 - Chapter of the prayer of the healthy behind the sick. 2341 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us. (H). 2342 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, they said: Humayd ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Humayd al-Ruwasi narrated to us, from his father, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in Zuhr, with Abu Bakr behind him; so when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the takbir, Abu Bakr said the takbir to make us hear. Then he saw us standing so he said: Sit; he gestured that to them. So when he finished the prayer he said: You almost did the deed of Persia and Rome with their great ones; follow your imams: if they pray standing, pray standing, and if they pray sitting, pray sitting."
"2209","2343 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik narrated to him from Ibn Shihab, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode a horse and was thrown from it, so his right side was scraped; so he prayed one of the prayers while sitting, and we prayed behind him sitting. So when he finished he said: The imam was only made to be followed; so when he prays standing, pray standing, and when he prays sitting, pray sitting, all of you."
"2210","2344 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Layth and Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab. Then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2211","2345 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he mentioned the like of it."
"2212","2346 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, that 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in his house while ill, so he prayed sitting, and people behind him prayed standing, so he gestured to them to sit, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2213","2347 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2214","2348 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata', he said: I heard Abu 'Alqamah narrating from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever obeys me has obeyed Allah, and whoever disobeys me has disobeyed Allah, and whoever obeys the commander has obeyed me, and whoever disobeys the commander has disobeyed me; so when he prays standing, pray standing, and when he prays sitting, pray sitting."
"2215","2349 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Mus'ab ibn Muhammad al-Qurashi, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The imam was only made to be followed; so when he prays sitting, pray sitting, all of you."
"2216","2350 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The like of it."
"2217","2351 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Humran narrated to us. (H). 2352 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, they said: 'Uqbah ibn Abi al-Sahba' al-Bahili narrated to us, he said: I heard Salim saying: 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated to me that he was one day with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among a group of his Companions, so he said to them: Do you not know that I am the Messenger of Allah to you? So they said: Yes, we bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah. He said: Do you not know that Allah has sent down in His Book that whoever obeys me has obeyed Allah? They said: Yes, we bear witness that whoever obeys you has obeyed Allah. He said: Then from obedience to Allah is that you obey me, and from obedience to me is that you obey your imams; so if they pray sitting, pray sitting, all of you. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to this and said: whoever leads a people sitting, due to an ailment, they pray behind him sitting, even if they can stand. And others opposed them therein and said: rather they pray behind him standing, and the obligation of standing does not fall from them due to its falling from their imam."
"2218","2353 - And they argued therein with what Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us. (H). 2354 - And Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, they said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Arqam ibn Shurahbil, he said: I traveled with Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Madinah to Sham. So he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell ill with the illness in which he died, he was in the house of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, so he said: Call 'Ali for me, may Allah be pleased with him. So 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Shall we not call Abu Bakr for you, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: Call him. So Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Shall we not call 'Umar for you, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: Call him. So Umm al-Fadl said: Shall we not call al-'Abbas, your uncle, for you? He said: Call him. So when they were present he raised his head then said: Let Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, lead the people in prayer; so Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, advanced and led the people in prayer. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) felt some lightness in himself, so he came out supported between two men. So when Abu Bakr sensed him, they glorified Allah at him, so Abu Bakr went to move back, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gestured to him: your place. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed from where Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, had reached in the recitation, with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, standing, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting. So Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, followed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the people followed Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. Then no sooner had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished the prayer than he grew heavy, so he went out supported between two men, with his feet dragging upon the ground, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died and did not bequeath. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is that Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, followed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) standing while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting. And this is from the deed of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after his saying what he said in the hadiths in the first chapter."
"2219","2355 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Abi 'A'ishah narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, he said: I entered upon 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said: Will you not narrate to me about the illness of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So she said: Yes; the people were staying in the mosque, awaiting the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for the last 'Isha prayer, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer, and he would lead them those days. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) felt some lightness in himself, so he came out supported between two men for the Zuhr prayer, while Abu Bakr was leading the people; so when Abu Bakr saw him, he went to move back, so he gestured to him not to move back, and said to the two: seat me beside him, so they seated him beside Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. So Abu Bakr would pray standing with the prayer of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the people praying with the prayer of Abu Bakr, while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting. 'Ubaydullah said: So I entered upon Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and presented her hadith to him, and he did not deny anything of that."
"2220","2356 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) grew heavy, Bilal, may Allah be pleased with him, came to announce the prayer to him, so he said: Bring Abu Bakr, let him lead the people in prayer. She said, so I said: O Messenger of Allah, if you commanded 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, to lead them, for Abu Bakr is a tender man, and whenever he stands in your place he will not make the people hear. He said: Command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer; so they commanded Abu Bakr, and he led the people in prayer. So when he entered the prayer, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) felt lightness, so he stood supported between two men, with his feet dragging upon the ground; so when Abu Bakr heard his movement he went to move back, so he gestured to him to pray as you are; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came until he sat to the left of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray with the people, with Abu Bakr following the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while standing, and the people following the prayer of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. So some said: You have no proof in this hadith, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in that prayer a follower. And they argued therein with what."
"2221","2357 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Shabbabah narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Nu'aym ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Wa'il, from Masruq, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed in the illness in which he died, behind Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sitting."
"2222","2358 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni, Abu Qurrah, narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Humayd narrated to me, he said: Thabit al-Bunani narrated to me, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed behind Abu Bakr in a single garment, a cloak, differing between its two ends; and it was the last prayer he prayed."
"2223","2359 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa, from his father, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell ill, so he said: Command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer. So 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Abu Bakr is a tender man; so he said: Command Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer, for you are the companions of Yusuf. He said: Abu Bakr stood in the lifetime of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And from the proof against them therein was that this hadith which they mentioned has been narrated. But the deeds of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in that prayer of his indicate that he was the imam, and that is that 'A'ishah said in the hadith of al-Aswad from her: so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat to the left of Abu Bakr, and that is the sitting of the imam; for had Abu Bakr been his imam, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have sat to his right. So when he sat to his left with Abu Bakr to his right, that indicated that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the imam, and that Abu Bakr was the follower. And another proof: that 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said in his hadith: so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took up the recitation from where Abu Bakr had reached. In that is what indicates that Abu Bakr stopped the recitation, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited. That is proof that he was the imam; for otherwise he would not have recited, because that prayer was a prayer in which recitation is aloud, and otherwise the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not have known the place Abu Bakr had reached in the recitation, nor would those behind Abu Bakr have known it. So when it was established by what we described that that prayer was among those in which recitation is aloud, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited therein, and all people do not differ that the follower does not recite behind the imam as the imam recites, it was thereby established that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in that prayer the imam. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of the reports. As for its aspect by way of reflection, we saw the agreed-upon principle that the follower's entering into the imam's prayer may impose an obligation upon the follower which was not upon him before entering, and we did not see it lift from him an obligation which was upon him before entering. From that is that we saw the traveler enter into the resident's prayer, so it becomes obligatory upon him to pray the resident's prayer of four, which was not obligatory upon him before entering with him; rather his entering with him imposed it upon him. And we saw that had a resident entered into a traveler's prayer, he would pray with his prayer until when he finished he would complete the resident's prayer, and no obligation falls from the resident by entering with the traveler, and his obligation remained as it was with nothing falling from it. So reflection thereupon is that likewise the healthy one upon whom standing in prayer was obligatory, when he enters with the sick one from whom the obligation of standing in his prayer has fallen, that entering does not lift from him an obligation which was upon him before entering the prayer. So if one says: we have seen the slave upon whom there is no Friday, enter into the Friday, and it suffices him for Zuhr, and an obligation which was upon him before entering with the imam therein falls from him. It is said to him: this confirms what we said, and that is that Friday was not obligatory upon the slave before entering therein; so when he entered therein with one upon whom it is obligatory, his entering it imposed upon him what is obligatory upon his imam, so thereby when what is obligatory upon his imam became obligatory upon him, he became in the ruling of a traveler upon whom there is no Friday who entered into the Friday, and it became obligatory upon him due to its being obligatory upon his imam, and it sufficed him for Zuhr because it became a substitute for it. So likewise the slave: when Friday became obligatory upon him by entering therein, it sufficed him for Zuhr because it became a substitute for it. So it was established by what we mentioned that a man's entering into another's prayer may impose upon him what was not obligatory upon him before entering therein, and does not lift from him what was obligatory upon him before entering. So it was thereby established that the healthy one upon whom standing in prayer is obligatory, when he enters with one from whom the obligation of standing in his prayer has fallen, his entering does not lift from standing what was obligatory upon him before that. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah and Abu Yusuf, may Allah have mercy upon them. And Muhammad al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy upon him, would say: it is not permitted for a healthy one to follow a sick one praying sitting, even if he bows and prostrates. And he holds that what was of the Messenger of Allah's praying sitting in his illness with the people standing was particular, because he did therein what is not permitted for anyone after him to do: his taking up the recitation from where Abu Bakr had reached, and Abu Bakr's exiting, may Allah be pleased with him, from imamate to becoming a follower in a single prayer, and this is not permitted for anyone after him, by agreement of all the Muslims; so that indicated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was singled out in that prayer of his with what others were barred from."
"2224","2360 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Amr, he said: Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, informed me that Mu'adh would pray 'Isha' with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then return to his people and lead them in it; it was for him voluntary, and for them obligatory. And from the proof of the others against them was that Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated this hadith from 'Amr ibn Dinar, as Ibn Jurayj narrated it, and brought it complete, and conveyed it better than Ibn Jurayj's conveying, except that he did not say therein this which Ibn Jurayj said: it was for him voluntary, and for them obligatory. So it may be from the saying of Ibn Jurayj, and may be from the saying of 'Amr ibn Dinar, and may be from the saying of Jabir. From whichever of these three the saying was, there is in it no proof of the reality of Mu'adh's deed whether it was so or not, because they did not relate that from Mu'adh; they only said a saying, upon that it was in their view so, and it may in reality be otherwise. And had that also been established from Mu'adh, there would be in it no proof that it was by the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor that had the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) been informed of it he would have affirmed it or changed it. And this 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them both, when Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' informed him that they would have intercourse in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and not wash until they ejaculated, 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to them: Did you inform the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of that and he approved it for you? He said: No; so 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, did not make that a proof. So likewise this deed: had it been established that Mu'adh did it in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), there would be in it no proof that it was by the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And we have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates the contrary of that."
"2225","2361 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us. (H). 2362 - And 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Maslamah ibn Qa'nab narrated to us, they said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini narrated to us, from Mu'adh ibn Rifa'ah al-Zuraqi, that a man from Banu Salimah called Sulaym came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: We remain in our labors, then come in the evening and pray, then Mu'adh ibn Jabal comes and prayer is announced, so we come to him and he lengthens upon us. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O Mu'adh, do not be a trial; either pray with me, or lighten for your people. So this saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Mu'adh indicates that he - in the view of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - would do one of the two things: either prayer with him, or with his people, and that he would not combine them, because he said: either pray with me, meaning and do not pray with your people, or lighten for your people, meaning and do not pray with me. So since there was in the first reports nothing from the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and there was in this report what we mentioned, it was established by this report that there was nothing prior from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein for Mu'adh, nor did we know that there was therein also anything later from him, so the proof becomes binding upon us thereby. And had there been therein a command from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as the people of the first saying said, it might have been from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at a time when the obligation would be prayed twice, for that was done at the beginning of Islam until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade it, and we mentioned that with its chains in the chapter of the fear prayer. So this deed of Mu'adh which we mentioned may have been before the forbidding of that, then came the forbidding and abrogated it, and may have been after that. So no one may place it in one of the two times except that his opponent may place it in the other time. So this is the ruling of this chapter by way of the reports. As for its ruling by way of reflection, we saw the prayer of the followers included in the prayer of their imam in its soundness and invalidity; sound reflection imposes that. From that is that we saw that when the imam forgets, there becomes obligatory upon those behind him for his forgetfulness what became obligatory upon him; and had they forgotten while he did not forget, there would not become obligatory upon them what becomes obligatory upon the imam when he forgets. So when it was established that the ruling of forgetfulness becomes obligatory upon the followers for the imam's forgetfulness, and is removed from them by its removal from the imam, it was established that their ruling in their prayer is the ruling of the imam in his prayer, as if their prayer is included in his prayer. And since their prayer was included in his prayer, their prayer may not be unlike his prayer. So it was thereby established that the follower may not have his prayer unlike the prayer of his imam. So if one says: we saw that they did not differ that a man may pray voluntary behind one praying an obligation; so just as one praying voluntary may follow one praying an obligation, so likewise one praying an obligation may pray it behind one praying voluntary. It is said to him: the cause of the voluntary is part of the cause of the obligation, and that is that one who enters the prayer intending nothing besides that of voluntary or obligation is thereby entering into a voluntary, and when he intends entering the prayer and intends the obligation, he is thereby entering into the obligation; so he comes to be entering into the obligation by the cause by which he entered into the voluntary, and by another cause. So since that was so, one praying voluntary while following one praying an obligation is in a prayer in all of which he has an imam, while one praying an obligation and following one praying voluntary is in a prayer in part of its cause by which he entered therein he has an imam, and has none in its remainder, so that is not proper. So if one says: we saw from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he led the people in major impurity, then repeated and they did not repeat; so that indicated that their prayer was not included in his prayer. So their opponent said: he only did that because he was not certain the impurity was from him before the prayer, so he took precaution for himself and repeated, and did not command others to repeat."
"2226","2363 - And they mentioned therein what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' al-Ghudani narrated to us, he said: Za'idah ibn Qudamah informed us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from Zubayd ibn al-Salt, he said: 'Umar said: It seems to me I had a wet dream and did not perceive, and I prayed without washing. Then he said: I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see. Then he established and prayed firmly with the forenoon risen high."
"2227","2364 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from Zubayd ibn al-Salt, that he said: I went out with 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, then he looked, and behold he had had a wet dream, so he prayed without washing, then said: By Allah, it seems to me only that I had a wet dream and did not perceive, and I prayed without washing. He said: So he washed and washed what he saw in his garment, and sprinkled what he did not see, and called the adhan and the iqamah, then prayed, after the forenoon had risen, firmly. So this indicated that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was not certain that the impurity was from him before the prayer."
"2228","2365 - And the proof that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, held that the follower's prayer is invalidated by the invalidity of the imam's prayer is that Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, forgot the recitation in the Maghrib prayer, so he repeated the prayer with them. So when 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, repeated the prayer with them for leaving the recitation - and in the invalidity of prayer by leaving recitation there is disagreement - then when he prayed with them in major impurity he would be likelier to repeat the prayer with them."
"2229","2366 - So if one says: there has been narrated from 'Umar the contrary of that, then he mentioned what Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, a man said to him: I prayed a prayer in which I recited nothing. So 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Did you not complete the bowing and prostration? He said: Yes. He said: Your prayer is complete. Shu'bah said: So 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar al-Ghamri narrated to me, he said: I said to Muhammad ibn Ibrahim: From whom did you hear this hadith? So he said: From Abu Salamah, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. It is said to him: this has been narrated from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from where you mentioned, but what we narrated from him in what we began with mentioning is continuous in chain from 'Umar, with Hammam present at that from him; so what is continuous in chain from him is worthier to be accepted from him than what opposed it. And this is also among what reflection indicates, and that is because they agreed that had a man prayed behind one in major impurity while knowing of that, his prayer is void, and they made his prayer included in the imam's prayer. So since that was so when knowing of the invalidity of his imam's prayer, so likewise by reflection when not knowing of it. Do you not see that had the follower prayed while in major impurity, knowing or not knowing, his prayer would be void. So what invalidates his prayer in the state of knowing of it is what invalidates his prayer in the state of ignorance of it, and his knowing of the invalidity of his imam's prayer invalidates his prayer thereby. So reflection thereupon is that likewise his ignorance of the invalidity of his imam's prayer be so. So this is the reflection, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy upon them. And Tawus and Mujahid said likewise."
"2230","2367 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Jabir al-Ju'fi, from Tawus and Mujahid, concerning an imam who led a people while without ablution, he said: They repeat the prayer all together. And there has been narrated from a group of the predecessors what agrees with what we held of the differing of the prayer of the imam and the followers."
"2231","2368 - From that is what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, concerning a man leading a people, it being for him Zuhr and for them 'Asr. He said: They repeat, and he does not repeat."
"2232","2369 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: I heard Yunus ibn 'Ubayd saying: 'Ayyad came to the mosque on a rainy day, and found them praying 'Asr, so he prayed with them thinking it was Zuhr, and he had not prayed Zuhr. So when they prayed and behold it was 'Asr, he came to al-Hasan and asked him about that, so he commanded him to pray them both together."
"2233","2370 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin would say: He prays them both together."
"2234","2371 - He said: and Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from al-Nakha'i, he said: He prays them both together."
"2235","2372 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: He prays Zuhr, then prays 'Asr."
"2236","60 - Chapter of fixing the recitation in prayer. 2373 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Ubaydah, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite in Adha and Fitr, in the first: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and in the second: Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming."
"2237","2374 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Muntashir, from his father, from Habib ibn Salim, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite in the two Eids: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and: Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming; and when a day of Eid and a Friday coincided, he recited them both in both together."
"2238","2375 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Muntashir, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2239","2376 - Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Muntashir, from his father, from Habib ibn Salim, from Salim, from al-Nu'man, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2240","2377 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Ma'bad ibn Khalid, from Zayd ibn 'Uqbah, from Samurah ibn Jundub, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the two Eids, the like of it; and he did not mention Friday."
"2241","2378 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2242","2379 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ma'bad ibn Khalid, from Zayd ibn 'Uqbah al-Fazari, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to that these two surahs are the two the imam should recite in the prayer of the two Eids, and in Friday, with the Opening of the Book, and not pass beyond that to other than it, and they argued with these reports. And others opposed them therein and said: there is in that no fixed specification which should not be passed beyond to other than it; rather the imam may recite them both, and may recite other than them."
"2243","2380 - And from the proof for them therein was that Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Fulayh ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Damrah ibn Sa'id, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, from Abu Waqid, he said: 'Umar asked me with what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited in the two Eids; I said: Qaf, and: The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split."
"2244","2381 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik informed me. (H). He said: Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Damrah, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, asked Abu Waqid, then he mentioned the like of it. So this Abu Waqid informed from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he recited in the two Eids other than what those who narrated the first reports informed. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he recited in Friday other than what was mentioned from him also in the first reports."
"2245","2382 - From what was narrated from him therein is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Damrah ibn Sa'id al-Mazini, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, that al-Dahhak ibn Qays asked al-Nu'man ibn Bashir: What would the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite on Friday after Surah al-Jumu'ah? He said: He would recite: Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming."
"2246","2383 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, he said: Damrah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, that al-Dahhak ibn Qays asked al-Nu'man ibn Bashir: What would the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recite in Friday? He said: al-Jumu'ah, and: Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming."
"2247","2384 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ibn Abi Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would recite in Friday Surah al-Jumu'ah and: When the hypocrites come to you."
"2248","2385 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mikhwal ibn Rashid, from Muslim al-Batin, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: So when there came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in these reports that he recited in the two Eids and Friday other than what came from him in the first reports, it is not permitted to construe that as contradiction and mutual belying. Rather we construe it as agreement and mutual truthfulness, so we make all that to have been from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): he recited with this once, and with this once, and each party of the two parties related from him what was present of him. So in that is proof that there is no fixing of the recitation therein, and that the imam may recite therein with the Opening of the Book whatever of the Qur'an he wills. And likewise what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also that he would recite in the dawn prayer on Friday."
"2249","2386 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah and Sharik narrated to us, from Mikhwal, from Muslim al-Batin, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both. H."
"2250","2387 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite on Friday in the dawn prayer: Alif Lam Mim, the Revelation, and: Has there come upon man."
"2251","2388 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Aslam narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from 'Azrah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: There is in that no proof that he would not pass beyond that to other than it, because it was not related from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: nothing besides these two surahs is recited in the dawn prayer on Friday with the Opening of the Book, so that other than that would not be permitted. Rather those who narrated them both from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only informed that he would recite them both therein, just as al-Nu'man and Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, informed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite in the two Eids what we mentioned. Then there came from others that he recited otherwise, because he recited with this once, and with this once. So likewise what was related from him of recitation in the dawn prayer on Friday: it may be that he recited it once, or recited it repeatedly then recited otherwise, so everyone who was present with him relates what he heard of his recitation, and there is in that no proof of the ruling of fixing. And all that we held in this chapter is the saying of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy upon them."
"2252","61 - Chapter of the traveler's prayer. 2389 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Bishr narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'afa ibn 'Imran narrated to us, from Mughirah ibn Ziyad, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shortened in travel, and completed. Abu Ja'far said: So a people went to that the traveler has the choice: if he wills he completes his prayer, and if he wills he shortens it. And they argued therein with this hadith."
"2253","2390 - And with what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi 'Ammar narrating from 'Abdullah ibn Babah, from Ya'la ibn Munabbih, he said: I said to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him: Allah, Exalted is He, only said: There is no blame upon you to shorten from prayer if you fear those who disbelieve may afflict you; yet the people are safe. So he said: I wondered at what you wondered at, so I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said: A charity Allah has given in charity to you, so accept His charity. And others opposed them therein and said: he should not add over two, and if he completes the prayer, then if he sat in two in Zuhr and 'Asr and 'Isha', the measure of the tashahhud, his prayer is complete; and if he did not sit therein the measure of the tashahhud, his prayer is void. And from the proof for them against the people of the first saying in what they argued against them with of the two hadiths which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter."
"2254","2391 - That Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Murajja ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Dawud narrated to us from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: Prayer was first obligated two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs; then when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah, he prayed added to every prayer the like of it, except Maghrib, for it is the witr of the day, and the dawn prayer due to the length of its recitation; and when he traveled he returned to his first prayer. So this 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, informs that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would pray two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, until he came to Madinah, then prayed added to every prayer the like of it, and that when he traveled he returned to his first prayer. So she informed that he would pray in his travel as he would pray before being commanded with completing the prayer, and that is two rak'ahs. So that is contrary to the hadith of Fahd which we mentioned in the first section: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed the prayer in travel, and shortened. As for the hadith of Ya'la ibn Munabbih: the people of the first saying argued with the verse mentioned therein, which is the saying of Allah, Exalted is He: And when you travel in the land, the verse. They said: that is upon concession from Allah, Exalted is He, to them in shortening, not upon binding them with that; and it is like His saying: There is no blame upon them both to return to each other; that is upon latitude from Him to them in return, not upon binding them with that. So from our proof against them for the people of the other saying was that this wording may be upon what they mentioned, and may be upon other than that. Allah, Exalted is He, said: Whoever performs Hajj to the House or 'Umrah, there is no blame upon him to circumambulate them both; and that is upon binding with all the scholars, because no one who performed Hajj or 'Umrah may not circumambulate them both. So since the negation of blame may be upon choice, and may be upon obligation, no one may construe that upon one of the two meanings apart from the other meaning except with a proof indicating that to him, from a Book, or a Sunnah, or a consensus. And reports have come successive from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his shortening in all his travels."
"2255","2392 - From what was narrated from him therein is what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khumayr, he said: I heard Habib ibn 'Ubayd narrating from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Ibn al-Simt, he said: I heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray at Dhu al-Hulayfah two rak'ahs."
"2256","2393 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman informed me, from 'Umarah ibn 'Umayr, or Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from 'Abdullah, he said: We prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mina two rak'ahs, and with Abu Bakr two rak'ahs, and with 'Umar two rak'ahs; so would that my share of four rak'ahs be two accepted rak'ahs."
"2257","2394 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hafs informed us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from 'Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it. Except that he did not mention the saying of 'Abdullah: would that my share, to the end of the hadith."
"2258","2395 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Salam, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would fast in travel, and break fast, and would pray the two rak'ahs, not leaving them, meaning not adding over them."
"2259","2396 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from 'Asim, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) traveled and stayed nineteen days, praying two rak'ahs."
"2260","2397 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us. (H). 2398 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Shafiyy, he said: The people kept asking Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, about prayer. So he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out from his family, he would pray only two rak'ahs, until he returned to them."
"2261","2399 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from al-Zuhri, from 'Ubaydullah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed where he conquered Makkah fifteen, shortening the prayer."
"2262","2400 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed at Mina two rak'ahs, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, two rak'ahs, and 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, two rak'ahs, and 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, two rak'ahs at the beginning of his caliphate; then 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed it thereafter four; so Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, when he prayed with the imam prayed four. And when he prayed alone, he prayed two rak'ahs."
"2263","2401 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Khubayb ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, he said: I heard Hafs ibn 'Asim narrating from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mina two rak'ahs, and with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, two rak'ahs, and with 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, two rak'ahs, and with 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, two rak'ahs for six years, or eight, then he completed thereafter."
"2264","2402 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Zayd, from Abu Nadrah, that a youth asked 'Imran ibn Husayn about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in travel, so he turned to the place of al-'Awaqah and said: This youth asked me about the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in travel, so preserve it from me: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never traveled a journey except he prayed two rak'ahs until he returned, and stayed at Makkah at the time of the Conquest eighteen, praying two rak'ahs, then saying: O people of Makkah, stand and pray two other rak'ahs, for we are traveling people. Then he raided Hunayn and al-Ta'if, praying two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, then returned to al-Ji'ranah and performed 'Umrah from it in Dhu al-Qa'dah; then I raided with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and performed 'Umrah with 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he prayed two rak'ahs, and with 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, at the beginning of his rule, and he prayed two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs; then 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, thereafter prayed four at Mina."
"2265","2403 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj. (H). 2404 - And Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: my uncle narrated to me, he said: 'Amr ibn al-Harith, and Usamah ibn Zayd, and Ibn Jurayj narrated to me that Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to them from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed Zuhr at Madinah four, and prayed 'Asr at Dhu al-Hulayfah two rak'ahs."
"2266","2405 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2267","2406 - 'Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2268","2407 - Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: We went out with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he kept praying two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, until he returned. I said: How long did you stay? He said: Ten."
"2269","2408 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Ishaq, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, except that he did not mention his asking Anas, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2270","2409 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us that Bukayr narrated to him, from Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Sulaym, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at Mina two rak'ahs, and with Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, two rak'ahs, and with 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, two rak'ahs, and with 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, two rak'ahs for half his rule, then he completed thereafter."
"2271","2410 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-'Awfi, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) four, with nothing after it; and he prayed Maghrib three, with two rak'ahs after it, and said: It is the witr of the day, not shortened in travel or residence; and he prayed 'Isha' four, with two rak'ahs after it. He said: And he prayed in travel Zuhr two rak'ahs, with two rak'ahs after it; and prayed 'Asr two rak'ahs, with nothing after it; and prayed Maghrib three, with two rak'ahs after it; and prayed 'Isha' two rak'ahs, with two rak'ahs after it."
"2272","2411 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah narrated to us, he said: I heard my father narrating that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led them at al-Batha', with a spear before him, Zuhr two rak'ahs and 'Asr two rak'ahs, with the woman and the donkey passing before him."
"2273","2412 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Imran ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to me, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to me, from 'Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out traveling, and kept praying two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs, until he returned."
"2274","2413 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us. (H). 2414 - And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Wahb, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us at Mina two rak'ahs, while we were most numerous and most secure. Abu Ja'far said: So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) inform from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would in his travel shorten the prayer until he returned to his family; then it has been narrated from his Companions after him that they would do that in their travels. From that is what we mentioned in this section from Abu Bakr and 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"2275","2415 - And from it also is what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed at Makkah two rak'ahs, then said: O people of Makkah, complete your prayer, for we are traveling people."
"2276","2416 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq, and Rawh, and Wahb narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"2277","2417 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn 'Abdullah, and Malik from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Aslam, the freedman of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, when he came to Makkah, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2278","2418 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Anas and Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"2279","2419 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, he said: We went out with 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, to Siffin, and he led us two rak'ahs, between the bridge and the arch."
"2280","2420 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Layla al-Kindi, he said: Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, went out with thirteen men of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a campaign, and Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, was their eldest; so prayer came and the iqamah was called, so they said: Advance, O Abu 'Abdullah. So he said: I am not one to advance; you are the Arabs, and from you is the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so let one of you advance. So one of the people advanced and prayed four rak'ahs. So when he finished the prayer, Salman said: What have we to do with the quadrupled; half the quadrupled suffices us."
"2281","2421 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Miswar, he said: We were with Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas in a village of the villages of Sham; he would pray two rak'ahs, while we prayed four; so we would ask him about that, and Sa'd would say: We know better."
"2282","2422 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, that a man informed him, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd Yaghuth were all together on a journey; Sa'd would shorten the prayer and break fast, while the two would complete the prayer and fast. So it was said to Sa'd: we see you shortening the prayer and breaking fast while they complete. So Sa'd said: We know better."
"2283","2423 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him from Ibn Shihab, from Safwan ibn 'Abdullah ibn Safwan, that he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, came visiting 'Abdullah ibn Safwan, so he led us two rak'ahs, then left, and we completed for ourselves four."
"2284","2424 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', that Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, would pray behind the imam at Mina four, and when he prayed by himself, he prayed two rak'ahs."
"2285","2425 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: I pray the travel prayer as long as I have not resolved residence, even if I stay twelve nights."
"2286","2426 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: I came to Salim asking him, while he was at the door of the mosque, so I said: How would your father do? He said: When he issued Zuhr and said: we are staying, he completed the prayer; and when he said: today and tomorrow, he shortened, even if he stayed twenty nights."
"2287","2427 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir al-Khazzaz narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to us, he said: I accompanied Ibn 'Abbas from Makkah to Madinah, and he would pray the obligation two rak'ahs."
"2288","2428 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Anas ibn Sirin, he said: We went out with Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, to Shiqq Sirin, and he led us in the ship upon a mat, and prayed Zuhr two rak'ahs, then prayed after it two rak'ahs."
"2289","2429 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: al-Azraq ibn Qays narrated to us, he said: I saw Abu Barzah al-Aslami at al-Ahwaz pray 'Asr; I said: so how much did he pray? He said: Two rak'ahs. Abu Ja'far said: So these Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would shorten in travel, and censure one who completed. Do you not see that when it was said to Sa'd that al-Miswar and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd Yaghuth complete, he said: We know better, and did not excuse them in their completing. And the man whom Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, put forward, with thirteen of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with him, prayed four, so Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: What have we to do with the quadrupled; half the quadrupled suffices us, and those present of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not censure him for that. So that indicated that their doctrines were not the permissibility of completing in travel. So if one says: that man whom Salman put forward and al-Miswar, may Allah be pleased with him, completed, and they are two Companions, so that contradicts what Salman, may Allah be pleased with him, and those who followed him upon leaving completion in travel, narrated. It is said to him: there is in this no proof of what you mentioned, because it may be that al-Miswar, may Allah be pleased with him, and that man completed because they did not hold shortening in that travel, since their doctrine was that prayer is not shortened except in Hajj or 'Umrah, or campaign; for others besides them also held that. So since what was narrated from them both bore what we mentioned, and shortening was established from most of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that was not made contradicting what was narrated from them. Since it might be otherwise, and this 'Uthman ibn 'Affan prayed at Mina four, yet 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, and those who censured with him of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), censured him for that, even though 'Uthman only did it for a meaning by which he held completing the prayer, among what we shall describe in its place in this chapter, if Allah wills. So since what was established for us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and from his Companions is shortening the prayer in travel, not completing it, it was not permitted for us to oppose that to other than it."
"2290","So if one says: have you narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) anything indicating to you that the obligatory prayers are two rak'ahs in travel, so that it be decisive against what your opponent held? 2450 - We said: Yes; Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us. (H). 2451 - And 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us. (H). And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Darir narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Bukayr ibn al-Akhnas, from Mujahid, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said: Allah obligated prayer upon the tongue of your Prophet in residence four, and in travel two rak'ahs."
"2291","2452 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir and Rawh narrated to us, they said: al-Thawri narrated to us, from Zubayd al-Yami. (H). 2453 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Mutarrif ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Musarrif narrated to us, from Zubayd al-Yami, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Adha prayer is two rak'ahs, and Fitr two rak'ahs, and Friday two rak'ahs, and the travel prayer two rak'ahs, complete, not shortened, upon the tongue of your Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2292","2454 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir and Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, they said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Zubayd, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed us, then mentioned the like of it."
"2293","2455 - And Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zubayd, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"2294","2456 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Darir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Zubayd, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2295","2457 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, he said: Zubayd narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from al-Thiqah, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"2296","2458 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Zubayd, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, except that he did not mention from al-Thiqah."
"2297","2459 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Musa ibn Salamah, he said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: I reside at Makkah, so how much shall I pray? He said: Two rak'ahs, the sunnah of Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"2298","2460 - al-Hasan ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Jabir, from 'Amir, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar and 'Abdullah ibn al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) established the travel prayer as two rak'ahs, and it is complete."
"2299","2461 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Jabir, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2300","2462 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Safwan ibn Muhriz, that he asked Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, about prayer in travel, so he said: I fear you will lie about me; two rak'ahs; whoever opposes the sunnah disbelieves."
"2301","2463 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Tayyah narrated to us, from Mu'arraq, he said: Safwan ibn Muhriz asked 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2302","2464 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: I asked Tawus about voluntary prayer in travel. So he said: What prevents you? So al-Hasan ibn Muslim said: I shall narrate to you; I asked Tawus about this, so he said: Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Prayer was obligated for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in residence four, and in travel two rak'ahs; so just as voluntary is performed here before it and after it, so likewise in travel is prayed before it and after it."
"2303","2465 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, she said: Prayer was first obligated two rak'ahs; so the travel prayer was left affirmed, and added to in the residence prayer."
"2304","2466 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"2305","2467 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from a man of Banu 'Amir, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was eating, so he said: Come and eat; so he said: I am fasting. So he said: Come near until I inform you about fasting: Allah, Exalted is He, removed half the prayer from the traveler, and fasting from the pregnant and the nursing."
"2306","2468 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Abu al-'Ala', from a man of his people, that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"2307","2469 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Khalid al-Hadhdha' informed us, from Abu Qilabah, from a man, he said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for a need, and behold he was eating lunch, so he said: Come to lunch; I said: I am fasting. So he said: Allah, Exalted is He, removed from the traveler half the prayer and fasting."
"2308","2470 - Nasr narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Ibn 'Uyaynah informed us, from Ayyub, he said: Abu Qilabah narrated to me from an elder of Banu Qushayr, from his uncle. Then we met him one day, so Abu Qilabah said to him: Narrate to him, meaning Ayyub. So the elder said: My uncle narrated to me that he went with camels of his and reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it, and added: and from the pregnant and the nursing."
"2309","2471 - Nasr narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak informed us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sulaym informed us, from 'Abdullah ibn Sawadah, from Anas ibn Malik of Banu 'Abdullah ibn Ka'b, he said: The cavalry of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raided us, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2310","2472 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Abu 'Awanah, from Abu Bishr, from Hani' ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from a man of Baljuraysh, from his father, he said: We would travel, and we came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was eating, so he said: Come and eat; so I said: I am fasting. So he said: Come, I shall narrate to you about fasting: Allah removed from the traveler fasting and half the prayer."
"2311","2473 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya; he said: Abu Qilabah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umayyah narrated to me; or from a man, from Abu Umayyah, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from a journey; so he said: Will you not wait for lunch, O Abu Umayyah? So I said: I am fasting, then he mentioned the like of it. So these reports which we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) indicate that the traveler's obligation is two rak'ahs. And that he in his two rak'ahs is like the resident in four. So just as the resident may not add in his prayer over four anything, so likewise the traveler may not add in his prayer over two rak'ahs anything. And reflection with us therein was that we saw the agreed-upon obligations: those upon whom they are must perform them; and he has no choice not to perform what is upon him thereof. And what was agreed upon that a man may perform it if he wills, and if he wills not perform it, is the voluntary; if he wills he does it, and if he wills he leaves it. So this is the description of the voluntary, and what must be performed is the obligation; and the two rak'ahs must be performed, while what is after them is disputed. So a people say: it should not be performed; and a people say the traveler may perform it if he wills, and may not perform it. So the two rak'ahs are described with the description of obligation, so they are obligation; and what is after the two rak'ahs is described with the description of voluntary, so it is voluntary. So it was thereby established that the traveler's obligation is two rak'ahs, and the obligation upon the resident was four in what is two rak'ahs upon the traveler. So just as the resident should not pray after the four anything without greeting, so likewise the traveler should not pray after the two rak'ahs anything without greeting. So this is the reflection - with us - in this chapter, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, and Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy upon them. So if one says: there has been narrated from a group of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they would complete, and he mentioned therein what 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, did at Mina."
"2312","2474 - And what Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to me, he said: Salih ibn Kaysan narrated to me, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: Prayer was first obligated two rak'ahs, then completed four, and affirmed for the traveler. Salih said: So I narrated that to 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, so he said: 'Urwah narrated to me from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she would pray in travel four."
"2313","2475 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, he said: I sought permission from Hudhayfah from Kufah to al-Mada'in, or from al-Mada'in to Kufah, in Ramadan, so he said: I permit you upon that you neither break fast nor shorten; he said: I said: And I guarantee you that I shall neither shorten nor break fast."
"2314","2476 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Awn narrated to us, he said: I came to Madinah and caught a rak'ah of 'Isha', so I did something by my opinion, then asked al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, so he said: Did you think Allah would punish you had you prayed four? The Mother of the Believers 'A'ishah would pray four, saying: The Muslims pray four."
"2315","2477 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: I said to 'Ata': Which of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would complete the prayer in travel? So he said: I know none but 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas. So this 'Ata' related that from Sa'd, though we narrated from him the contrary of that in the hadith of al-Zuhri and Habib ibn Abi Thabit."
"2316","2478 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Habban al-Bariqi, he said: I said to Ibn 'Umar: I am of the dispatch of the people of Iraq, so how shall I pray? He said: If you pray four, you are in a garrison town; and if you pray two rak'ahs, you are a traveler. So this 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, and Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, and 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: there has been narrated from them concerning completing the prayer in travel what we mentioned. And for each one of them in the doctrine he held is a meaning we shall explain in this chapter, and mention therewith what supports his saying by way of reflection, and what is against him also by way of reflection, if Allah wills. As for 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with them both: what we mentioned from him thereof is his completing the prayer at Mina; yet that was not because he denied shortening in travel. And how could that be imagined of him, when Allah, Exalted is He, said: And when you travel in the land, the verse, so Allah permitted them shortening in this verse when they feared those who disbelieve might afflict them. Yes, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed them that is binding for them, even when safe, in the hadith of Ya'la ibn Munabbih which we narrated from him, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, at the beginning of this chapter; and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed at Mina two rak'ahs while they were most numerous and most secure, with 'Uthman with him, may Allah be pleased with him; so his completing the prayer at Mina was not because he denied shortening in travel, but for a meaning concerning which there is disagreement."
"2317","2479 - So Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'Uthman prayed at Mina four only because he resolved to stay after Hajj. So al-Zuhri informed us in this hadith that 'Uthman's completing, may Allah be pleased with him, was only because he intended residence, so his completing that while resident had exited from what they were in of the ruling of travel, and entered into the ruling of residence; so there is in his doing that no proof of his doctrine how it was concerning prayer in travel, whether it is completing or shortening."
"2318","2480 - And al-Zuhri also said otherwise: so Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, he said: Ayyub informed us, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed at Mina four only because the Bedouins were more numerous that year, so he wished to inform them that prayer is four. So this informs that he did what he did to teach the Bedouins thereby that prayer is four. So it may be that when he wanted to show them that, he intended residence, so became resident whose obligation is four, and led them four while resident, due to the cause Ma'mar related from al-Zuhri in the section before this. And it may be that he did that while traveling for that cause. And the first interpretation is likelier with us, Allah knows best, because the Bedouins were more ignorant of prayer and its rulings in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than of them and their ruling in the time of 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, while their covenant with ignorance was then more recent. So they were in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) more in need of knowledge of the obligations of prayer than of that in the time of 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. So since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not complete the prayer for that cause, but shortened it so they would pray with him the travel prayer upon its ruling, and teach them the residence prayer upon its ruling in travel, 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was likelier not to complete the prayer with them for that cause, but to pray it with them upon its ruling in travel, and teach them how is its ruling in residence. So the meaning of what is sound of the interpretation of the hadith of Ayyub from al-Zuhri returned to the meaning of the hadith of Ma'mar from al-Zuhri. And others said he completed the prayer only because he held that none shortens it but one who alights and departs."
"2319","2481 - And they argued therein with what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad said, and Qatadah informed us, he said: 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Only one who carries provision and waterskin, and alights and departs, shortens the prayer."
"2320","2482 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from 'Abbas ibn 'Abdullah, that 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to his officials that no collector nor distant one nor trader pray the two rak'ahs; only one with whom are provision and waterskin prays the two rak'ahs."
"2321","2483 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh and Abu Umar narrated to us, they both said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us that Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani informed them from Abu Qilabah al-Jaribi, from his uncle Abu al-Muhallab, he said: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote that it has reached me that some people go out either for trade, or for collecting revenue, or for mobilization, then they shorten the prayer, whereas only one who is journeying far or in the presence of an enemy shortens the prayer. He said: And the view of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, was that no one shortens the prayer except one who needs to carry provisions and a waterskin, and one who is journeying far; as for one who is on a journey in which he is free from needing to carry provisions and a waterskin, he completes the prayer. They said: And for this reason he completed the prayer at Mina, because its people at that time had become many, until it had become a city, so whoever settled there was free from carrying provisions and a waterskin. And this view, in our opinion, is corrupt, because Mina did not become, in the time of Uthman ibn Affan and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, different from Makkah in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray there two rakahs, then Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed there likewise after him, then Umar prayed there after Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, likewise. So if in Makkah, despite one who settles there not needing to carry provisions and a waterskin, the prayer is shortened, then other places are even more deserving of being so. Thus all these views are negated by their corruption from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, as being the reason for completing the prayer, except the first view which Mamar narrated from al-Zuhri, for it is possible that he completed it for that reason, and in that hadith is that his completing was due to his intention of residence, according to what we narrated therein and what we have explained of its meaning. As for what we narrated from Hudhayfah, there is in it no evidence either for completing in travel, whether that travel is for obedience or otherwise, because it is possible that it was his opinion that no one shortens the prayer except a pilgrim performing Hajj or Umrah or a warrior, as has been narrated from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2322","2484 - For Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from al-Aswad, he said: Abdullah did not consider shortening except for a pilgrim performing Hajj or Umrah or a warrior. So it is possible that the view of Hudhayfah was likewise, so he ordered al-Taymi, since he intended a journey not for Hajj nor for jihad, not to shorten the prayer, so there is negated from that hadith of his any proof for one who holds that the traveler completes the prayer in travel. As for what we narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, concerning that, then the hadith of Hayyan is about his asking him while he was in one of the cities, and he said to him: I am from the dispatch of the people of Iraq, so how should I pray? So Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, answered him and said: If you pray four, then you are in a city, and if you pray two, then you are a traveler. So that indicates his view regarding the prayer of the traveler in cities was thus. And Safwan ibn Muhriz narrated from him, when he asked him about prayer in travel, that his answer to him was that he said: It is two rakahs; whoever contradicts the Sunnah has disbelieved. That is about prayer outside the cities, so that it does not contradict what Hayyan narrated. So the hadith of Hayyan is about the prayer of the traveler in cities, and the hadith of Safwan is about his prayer outside cities, and we shall explain the proof in this chapter at its end, if Allah the Exalted wills."
"2323","2485 - As for what was narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, concerning that, then Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, he said: I said to Urwah: What led Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, to pray four in travel? He said: She interpreted as Uthman interpreted regarding completing the prayer at Mina. We have already mentioned what was interpreted regarding Uthman's, may Allah be pleased with him, completing the prayer at Mina, and what was sound of that was that it was because of his intention of residence. If it was for that reason, Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, would complete the prayer, for it is possible that no prayer came upon her except that she intended residence in that place, by which completing the prayer became obligatory upon her, so she completed the prayer for that reason. Thus her completing would be while she was under the ruling of residents, not under the ruling of travelers. Some people said: That was from her for a different meaning, which is that I heard Abu Bakrah say: Abu Umar said Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was the Mother of the Believers, and she used to say: Every place I alight is the home of one of my sons, so she counted that as a home for herself and completed the prayer for that reason. And this, in my view, is corrupt, because although Aishah was the Mother of the Believers, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the father of the believers, and he has more right over them than Aishah. He used to alight in their homes, yet that did not take him out of the ruling of travel in which prayer is shortened to the ruling of residence in which prayer is completed. Some people said: The view of Aishah regarding shortening the prayer was that it is for one who carries provisions and a waterskin, according to what we narrated from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, and she used to travel after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with sufficiency thereof, so she abandoned shortening the prayer for this reason. When these interpretations regarding the act of Uthman and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with them both, stood equal, it became necessary for us to examine the ruling of shortening the prayer, what necessitates it. The basis in that is that we saw that a man, when he is resident among his family, his ruling in prayer is the ruling of residence, whether in his residence there is obedience or disobedience, his ruling does not change by any of that, so his ruling was completion of the prayer, obligatory upon him by residence specifically, not by obedience nor by disobedience; then when he travels, he leaves thereby the ruling of residence. There has occurred in this the disagreement we have mentioned. Some people said: The ruling of shortening is not obligatory for him unless that travel is a travel of obedience. Others said: The ruling of shortening is obligatory for him in both cases. Since the ruling of completion is obligatory for him in residence by residence specifically, not by obedience nor otherwise, so likewise it follows in reasoning that the ruling of shortening is obligatory for him in travel by travel specifically, not by obedience nor otherwise, by analogy and reasoning upon what we have explained. And when it is established that shortening is obligatory for him only by the ruling of travel specifically and nothing else, it is established that he shortens as long as he is a traveler, in cities and elsewhere, because the cause for which shortening is done in travel has not left him by his entering cities. And all that we have explained in this chapter and validated is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2324","Chapter 62: The witr, is it prayed in travel upon the mount or not? 2486 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim ibn Abdullah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray upon his mount facing whichever direction it turned, and he would pray witr upon it, except that he did not pray the obligatory prayer upon it."
"2325","2487 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Said ibn Yasar, that he said: I was traveling with Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, on the road to Makkah, and when I feared the morning, I dismounted and prayed witr. Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Where were you? I said: I feared the dawn, so I dismounted and prayed witr. Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: Do you not have in the Messenger of Allah an example? I said: Yes, by Allah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray witr upon the camel."
"2326","2488 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah and Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, they both said: Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah al-Umari, from Said ibn Yasar Abu al-Hubab, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to pray witr upon his mount."
"2327","2489 - Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir said: And Abu Mashar narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: Some people adopted this and said: There is no harm in the traveler praying witr upon his mount, as he prays the rest of the voluntary prayers. They argued for that with these reports narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with the act of Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, after him. Others disagreed with them in that and said: It is not permissible for anyone to pray witr upon the mount; he prays it on the ground as he does for the obligatory prayers."
"2328","2490 - They argued for that with what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Hanzalah ibn Abi Sufyan narrated to us, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to pray upon his mount and pray witr on the ground, and he claimed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do likewise. This contradicts what the people of the first view used as proof for their saying, in what we have narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then there was also narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, through another chain, of his act, what agrees with this."
"2329","2491 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar and Bakr ibn Bakkar narrated to us, they both said: Umar ibn Dharr narrated to us, from Mujahid, that Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to pray in travel upon his camel wherever it turned with him, and when it was the time before dawn, he dismounted and prayed witr."
"2330","2492 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdullah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Mujahid, he said: I accompanied Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, between Makkah and Madinah, and he mentioned the like of it."
"2331","2493 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, the like of it. They said: In what we narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and in what we narrated from him of his act, is what contradicts what the people of the first view narrated. Part of the proof for the people of the first view was that they do not set up Hanzalah against al-Zuhri. As for what was narrated from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, of his praying witr on the ground, it is possible that he did that while it was permissible for him to pray witr upon the mount, just as he may pray voluntary prayer on the ground and may pray it upon the mount; so his praying it upon the mount indicates that he may pray it upon the mount, and his praying it on the ground does not negate that he may pray it upon the mount."
"2332","2494 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Nafi, he said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to pray witr upon his mount, and sometimes he would dismount and pray witr on the ground. So it is possible that Mujahid saw him praying witr on the ground and did not know what his view was regarding witr upon his mount, so he reported what he saw of him of praying witr on the ground. And his praying witr on the ground does not negate that he also used to pray witr upon the mount. Then Salim, Nafi, and Abu al-Hubab came and reported from him that he used to pray witr upon his mount. The correct view in our opinion in that is that it is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray witr upon the mount before witr was made decisive and its matter emphasized, then it was made decisive afterwards, and no concession was given for abandoning it."
"2333","2495 - There was narrated from him concerning that what Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to me, he said: Musa ibn Ayyub al-Ghafiqi narrated to me, from his uncle Iyas ibn Amir, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to pray at night while Aishah lay across in front of him, and when he intended to pray witr he gestured to her to move away, and said: This is a prayer you have been given as addition."
"2334","2496 - Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ayyub narrated to us, and he mentioned with a similar chain the like of it."
"2335","2497 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Luhayah and al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abdullah ibn Rashid, from Abdullah ibn Abi Murrah, from Kharijah ibn Hudhafah al-Adawi, that he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah has reinforced you with a prayer which is better for you than red camels, between the Isha prayer and the rising of dawn; the witr, the witr."
"2336","2498 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2337","2499 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Abu Luhayah narrated to us that Abu Tamim Abdullah ibn Malik al-Jayshani informed him that he heard from Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: A man from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah has added for you a prayer, so pray it between Isha and the Fajr prayer; the witr, the witr. Know that he is Abu Basrah al-Ghifari. Abu Tamim said: I and Abu Dharr were sitting, then Abu Dharr took my hand and we went to Abu Basrah, and we found him at the door next to the house of Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu Dharr said: O Abu Basrah, did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah has added for you a prayer, so pray it between Isha and the rising of dawn, the witr, the witr? Abu Basrah said: Yes. He said: Did you hear it? He said: Yes. He said: Do you say: I heard him say it? He said: Yes. So in these reports the matter of witr was emphasized, and no concession was given to anyone to abandon it, whereas before that it was not so emphasized. So it is possible that what Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his praying witr upon the mount was from him before his emphasizing it, then he emphasized it afterwards, abrogating that. We have seen the agreed-upon principle that the obligatory prayer: a man may not pray it sitting while he is able to stand, and he may not pray it in his travel upon his mount while he is able to stand and dismount. And we saw him praying voluntary prayer on the ground sitting, and praying it in his travel upon his mount. So that which he prays sitting while able to stand is that which he prays in travel upon his mount, and that which he does not pray sitting while able to stand is that which he does not pray in travel upon his mount; such are the agreed-upon principles. Then witr, by their agreement, a man does not pray it on the ground sitting while able to stand. So by reasoning upon that, he should not pray it in his travel upon the mount while able to dismount. From this angle, in my view, the abrogation of witr upon the mount is established. There is in this no evidence that it is obligatory nor voluntary. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"2338","Chapter 63: A man doubts in his prayer and does not know whether he prayed three or four? 2500 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Muhriz narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Abu Zamah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Said and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When Satan comes to one of you and confuses his prayer for him, so he does not know how much he prayed, let him prostrate two prostrations while sitting."
"2339","2501 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2340","2502 - Ibrahim ibn Munqidh narrated to us, he said: Idris ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Bakr ibn Mudar, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me from Abu Shihab, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2341","2503 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays and does not know whether he prayed three or four? Then he mentioned the like of it."
"2342","2504 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2343","2505 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2344","2506 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to me, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, with the addition: then he gives salutation."
"2345","2507 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Abd Rabbihi ibn Said, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When the call for prayer is made Satan turns away with flatulence, and when the prayer is commenced he seeks confusion; when he comes to one of you he makes him wish and reminds him of his needs which he did not remember, until he does not know how much he prayed; when one of you finds that, let him prostrate two prostrations while sitting."
"2346","2508 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they both said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Hilal ibn Iyad narrated to me, he said: Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: When one of you prays and does not know whether he prayed three or four, let him prostrate two prostrations while sitting. Abu Jafar said: Some people adopted this and said: This is the ruling for one whom doubt enters in his prayer, so he does not know whether he added or decreased: he prostrates two prostrations while sitting, then gives salutation, and there is nothing else upon him. Others disagreed with them in that and said: Rather he builds upon the lesser number until he knows that he has certainly performed what was upon him. They said: There is in this hadith no evidence that there is nothing upon the praying person besides those two prostrations, because there has been narrated from him what adds to that, obliging upon him before the two prostrations to build upon certainty until he knows with certainty the removal of what he had known with certainty was obligatory upon him."
"2347","2509 - Of what was narrated from him concerning that is what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Ismail al-Makki informed me, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I was discussing the matter of prayer with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf came and said: Shall I not narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? We said: Yes. He said: I testify that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When one of you prays and doubts about deficiency, let him pray until he doubts about excess."
"2348","2510 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Makhul, from Kurayb, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, he said: I sat with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: O Ibn Abbas, have you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a man who forgets in his prayer and does not know whether he added or decreased, what was commanded in it? He said: I said: Have you heard, O Commander of the Believers, anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in it? He said: No, by Allah, I have heard nothing in it nor asked about it. Then Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, came and said: What are you two discussing? Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him and said: I asked this youth about such-and-such and found no knowledge with him. Abd al-Rahman said: But I have it; I heard that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Umar said: You are with us just and approved, so what did you hear? He said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: When one of you doubts in his prayer, doubting between one and two, let him count it as one; and when he doubts between three and four, let him count it as three, so that the doubt is about excess, then let him prostrate two prostrations before giving salutation."
"2349","2511 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zarah Wahbullah ibn Rashid narrated to us, he said: Haywah informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, that Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to him, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When one of you prays and does not know whether he prayed three or four, let him build upon certainty and leave doubt; if his prayer was deficient, he has completed it, and the two prostrations humiliate Satan; and if his prayer was complete, what he added and the two prostrations are voluntary for him."
"2350","2512 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Hisham ibn Sad informed me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: Then he prostrates two prostrations while sitting, before the salutation."
"2351","2513 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: al-Majishun narrated to us, from Zayd, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not say: before the salutation."
"2352","2514 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him. (H) 2515 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik informed me, from Zayd, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not mention Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him. Abu Jafar said: These reports add to the first reports, because these require building upon the lesser number, and the two prostrations after that, so they are more worthy than them, because they added to them. Others said: The ruling in that is that the praying person looks to his preponderant opinion in that and acts upon it, then prostrates the two prostrations of forgetfulness after the salutation. If he has no opinion in that, he builds upon the lesser number until he knows with certainty that he has prayed what was upon him."
"2353","2516 - They argued for that with what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, he said: I asked Said ibn Jubayr about doubt in prayer. He said: As for me, if it is voluntary I restart, and if it is obligatory I give salutation and prostrate."
"2354","2517 - He said: I mentioned it to Ibrahim and he said: What would you do with the saying of Said ibn Jubayr? Alqamah narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When one of you is inattentive in his prayer, let him seek what is correct and prostrate two prostrations."
"2355","2518 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you prays and does not know whether he prayed three or four, let him look to the more likely of that to correctness, complete it, then give salutation, then prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness, recite tashahhud, and give salutation."
"2356","2519 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Mansur, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he did not say: and recites tashahhud."
"2357","2520 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Mansur, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. In this hadith is acting upon seeking correctness. Validating the reports necessitates what the people of this view say, because if this meaning is void and it becomes obligatory not to act upon seeking correctness, this hadith is negated. If acting upon seeking correctness when he has an opinion, and building upon the lesser when he has no opinion, is obligatory, then the hadith of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, the hadith of Abu Said, and the hadith of Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with them both, become consistent. Each one of them came regarding a meaning other than that in which the other came. Thus reports should be interpreted upon agreement, as far as possible, and not carried upon contradiction except when no other way is found for them. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of validating the meanings of the reports, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. Of what validates what they held is that we have narrated from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the beginning of this chapter what we mentioned, then he himself said by his opinion that he seeks correctness."
"2358","2521 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: A shaykh, I think Abu Zayd al-Harawi, narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, Idris said: He informed me from his father, he heard him narrating, he said: Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: In doubt, one seeks correctness. And the like of that has also been narrated from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him."
"2359","2522 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar al-Ramadi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Umar and Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with them, were asked about a man who was inattentive and did not know how much he prayed, three or four? They both said: He seeks the more correct of that and completes it, then prostrates two prostrations while sitting."
"2360","2523 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Shabab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Sulayman al-Yashkuri, from Abu Said al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: In doubt, one seeks correctness. He said: I said: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). What we mentioned indicates that what Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is only when he does not know whether he prayed three or four, and neither is preponderant in his heart over the other. As for when one of them is preponderant in his heart over the other, he acts upon that. Thus what was narrated from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, when combining what he narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and what he answered the one who asked him after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), agrees with what the people of this last view said, not what those who opposed them said. And there has also been narrated from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, the like regarding seeking correctness."
"2361","2524 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah and Abu Awanah informed me, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it."
"2362","2525 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Umar ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd, from Salim ibn Abdullah, that Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say: When one of you doubts in his prayer, let him aim at what he thinks he forgot of his prayer and pray it, and prostrate two prostrations while sitting."
"2363","2526 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Umar ibn Muhammad informed me from Salim, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2364","2527 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi, that Abdullah ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, when asked about forgetfulness in prayer used to say: Let each of you aim at what he thinks he forgot of his prayer, and pray it."
"2365","2528 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding seeking correctness in doubt in prayer, like what is in the hadith of Ibn Wahb, from Malik, from Umar ibn Muhammad, and from Ibn Wahb, from Umar himself. As for the aspect of that by way of reasoning, we have seen the agreed-upon principle in that, that this man, before entering prayer, had upon him to perform four rakahs; when he doubted whether he performed some of them, examination of that became necessary to know what his ruling was. We saw that if he doubted whether he had prayed at all, it would be upon him to pray until he knows with certainty that he prayed, and he does not act in that by seeking correctness. So reasoning upon this is that likewise he is regarding everything of his prayer which was obligatory upon him: he must perform it until he knows with certainty that he performed it. If one says: The obligation is not binding upon him until he knows with certainty that it is obligatory upon him. It is said to him: We have not found all acts of worship to be thus, for we have been commanded that if the sky is overcast for us on the thirtieth day of Shaban, where it is possible it is from Ramadan so its fast is obligatory upon us, and possible it is from Shaban so its fast is not upon us, that its fast is not upon us until we know with certainty that it is from the month of Ramadan, then we fast it. Likewise we saw the end of Ramadan: if the sky is overcast for us on the thirtieth day, where it is possible it is from Ramadan so its fast is upon us, and possible it is from Shawwal so its fast is not upon us, we are commanded to fast it until we know with certainty that its fast is not upon us. So whoever entered into something with certainty does not leave it except with certainty. So reasoning upon that is that likewise whoever entered into prayer with certainty that it is upon him, it is not lawful for him to leave it except with certainty that leaving it has become lawful for him. And there has come what we cited of the ruling of overcast skies in Shaban and Ramadan from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) successively as we mentioned."
"2366","2529 - Of what was narrated from him concerning that is what Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, that Muhammad ibn Hunayn informed him that he heard Ibn Abbas say: I am amazed at those who fast before Ramadan; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only said: When you see the crescent, fast, and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, count thirty."
"2367","2530 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Ibn Abbas, he said: I heard him say, and he mentioned the like of it."
"2368","2531 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Rawh narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2369","2532 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Bakr and Rawh narrated to us, they both said: Hatim ibn Abi Sagheirah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, he said: I entered upon Ikrimah and he said: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, and he mentioned the like of it."
"2370","2533 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us. (H) 2534 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shubah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, he said: We saw the crescent of Ramadan, so we sent a man to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he asked him, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has extended it for its sighting; if it is obscured for you, complete the number."
"2371","2535 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ismail ibn Jafar narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Dinar, that he heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see the crescent, fast, and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, estimate for it."
"2372","2536 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Wahb informed me that Malik informed him, from Abdullah, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2373","2537 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Usamah narrated to me, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2374","2538 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2375","2539 - Muhammad ibn Humayd Abu Qurrah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2376","2540 - Ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, he said: Zakariyya narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. And he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: Then count thirty."
"2377","2541 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Humayd al-Ruwasi narrated to us, from Mujalid ibn Said, from al-Shabi, from Adi ibn Hatim, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: When Ramadan comes, fast thirty except that you see the crescent before that."
"2378","2542 - Muhammad ibn Humayd Abu Qurrah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sad narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When you see the crescent, fast, and when you see it, break fast; if it is obscured for you, count thirty."
"2379","2543 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed me, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say: Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the like of it."
"2380","2544 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"2381","2545 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, he said: I heard a man say: O Messenger of Allah, what about the day concerning which there is disagreement? One group says: From Shaban, and one group says: From Ramadan. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, then he mentioned the like of it."
"2382","2546 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ribi ibn Hirash, from a man, or from a man from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not precede this month until you see the crescent or complete the number, and do not break fast until you see the crescent or complete the number. Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not command them to leave the breaking of fast which they had entered except with certainty that they had left it, then he also did not take them out afterwards from the fast which they had entered except with certainty that they had left it, so likewise it follows in reasoning that whoever entered into prayer while certain that it is upon him does not leave it except with certainty from him that it is not upon him."
"2383","Chapter 64: The prostration of forgetfulness in prayer, is it before the salutation or after it? 2547 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abdullah ibn Malik - he is Ibn Buhaynah - that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stand in the two rakahs and forget to sit, so he continued in his standing, then prostrated two prostrations after finishing his prayer."
"2384","2548 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Said, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abdullah ibn Buhaynah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: This hadith does not clarify what the finishing is. It is possible that the finishing is the salutation, and it is possible that it is finishing the tashahhud before the salutation."
"2385","2549 - So we examined that, and Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Yunus informed me that Ibn Shihab informed them, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, that Abdullah ibn Buhaynah narrated to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, except that he said: When he finished his prayer he prostrated two prostrations, making takbir in each prostration while sitting before giving salutation, and the people prostrated them with him, for what he forgot of sitting."
"2386","2550 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed me, he said: Malik and Amr informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Ibn Buhaynah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"2387","2551 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2388","2552 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-Araj informed me, from Abdullah ibn Buhaynah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in a prayer, we think it was Asr, and he stood in the second and did not sit. When it was before giving salutation, he prostrated two prostrations while sitting. Abu Jafar said: Thus it is established by what we mentioned in these hadiths that the finishing mentioned in the hadiths at the beginning of this chapter is before the salutation."
"2389","2553 - Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Bukayr, that Muhammad ibn Ajlan, the freedman of Fatimah, narrated to him, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf, the freedman of Uthman, narrated to him, from his father, that Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan led them in prayer, and he stood while sitting was due upon him, and did not sit. When it was at the end of his prayer, he prostrated two prostrations before giving salutation, and said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do."
"2390","2554 - Muhammad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub and Ibn Luhayah informed me, they both said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: Some people adopted these reports and said: Such is the prostration of forgetfulness, and it is before the salutation of prayer. Others disagreed with them in that and said: Whatever prostration of forgetfulness is for a deficiency in prayer is before the salutation, as in the hadith of Ibn Buhaynah and as in the hadith of Muawiyah. And whatever prostration of forgetfulness became due for an addition added in prayer is after the salutation. They argued for that with the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in the report of Dhu al-Yadayn, and with the hadith of al-Khirbaq and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding the prostration of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that day for his forgetfulness after the salutation. Of that is:"
"2391","2555 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from al-Layth, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Irak ibn Malik, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he prostrated on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn, meaning the two prostrations of forgetfulness, after the salutation. We shall mention the hadith of Dhu al-Yadayn and how it is in the chapter of speech in prayer, if Allah the Exalted wills. Others disagreed with them in that and said: Every forgetfulness incurring prostration in prayer, for addition or deficiency, is after the salutation. They argued for that with:"
"2392","2556 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun, he said: al-Masudi informed us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer and was inattentive, so he rose in the two rakahs, and we said Subhan Allah to him, but he continued; when he completed the prayer and gave salutation, he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness."
"2393","2557 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"2394","2558 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, he said: Ziyad ibn Ilaqah narrated to us, he said: al-Mughirah informed me, and he mentioned similar to it."
"2395","2559 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Malik al-Ruwasi, from among themselves, narrated to us, he said: I heard Amir narrate that al-Mughirah ibn Shubah was inattentive in the first two prostrations, so they said Subhan Allah to him, and he stood fully erect until he prayed four, then prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness and said: Thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did."
"2396","2560 - Mubashshir narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Jabir, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, from al-Mughirah, the like of it."
"2397","2561 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Shabab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubayl, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Shubah led us in prayer and stood in the two rakahs, so the people behind him said Subhan Allah, and he gestured to them to stand. When he finished his prayer he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness, then said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you stands fully erect, let him continue praying and prostrate the two prostrations of forgetfulness; if he does not stand fully erect, let him sit, and there is no forgetfulness prostration upon him."
"2398","2562 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Tahman, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubayl, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: al-Mughirah ibn Shubah led us in prayer and stood erect from the two rakahs, so we said Subhan Allah, and he gestured and said Subhan Allah, and continued in his prayer. When he finished his prayer and gave salutation, he prostrated two prostrations while sitting, then said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer and stood erect from his sitting, and continued in his prayer. When he finished his prayer, he prostrated two prostrations while sitting, then said: When one of you prays and stands from sitting, if he has not stood fully erect, let him sit, and there are no two prostrations upon him; if he has stood erect, let him continue in his prayer and prostrate two prostrations while sitting. This al-Mughirah narrates from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he prostrated for forgetfulness for what he omitted of his prayer, after the salutation. These hadiths admit several interpretations. It is possible that what we mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Buhaynah and Muawiyah, of the prostration of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for forgetfulness before the salutation, applies to every forgetfulness incurring prostration in prayer, whether deficiency or addition. It is possible that what is in the hadith of al-Mughirah, of the prostration of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the salutation, likewise applies to every forgetfulness in prayer incurring the prostration of forgetfulness, whether deficiency or addition. It is possible that what is in the hadith of Imran, Abu Hurayrah, and Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, of the prostration of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the salutation for what he added in prayer forgetfully, means that every prostration due for forgetfulness is prostrated there, without intending thereby to distinguish between prostration for addition and prostration for deficiency. And it is possible that he intended thereby to distinguish between them. So we examined that, and we found Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, had attended the forgetfulness prostration of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Dhu al-Yadayn, for the addition he had added in his prayer by giving salutation therein, and his prostration was after the salutation. And we found him prostrating after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for a deficiency from him in prayer, after the salutation."
"2399","2563 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar al-Yamami narrated to me, from Damdam ibn Jaws al-Hanafi, from Abdullah ibn Hanzalah ibn al-Rahib, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed the Maghrib prayer and did not recite anything in the first rakah. When it was the second, he recited therein the Opening of the Book and a surah twice; when he gave salutation, he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness. So the prostration of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which he performed, for the addition he had added in his prayer, and his prostrating for it after the salutation, became evidence in his view that the ruling of every forgetfulness prostration in prayer is likewise. And Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, also did the like of that."
"2400","2564 - Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Bayan Abu Bishr al-Ahmasi, he said: I heard Qays ibn Abi Hazim say: Sad ibn Malik led us in prayer and stood in the first two rakahs, so they said Subhan Allah, and he said Subhan Allah and continued; when he gave salutation, he prostrated the two prostrations of forgetfulness. And there has also been narrated from Abdullah ibn Masud, Ibn Abbas, Ibn al-Zubayr, and Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with them, that they prostrated for forgetfulness after the salutation."
